[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_header(Astronomical Unit)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Astronomical Unit (a.u.))
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Astronomical Unit (a.u.)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Measurement used to express the average distance from the Earth to the sun. For convenience, astronomers often express distances within the solar system in astronomical units. They measure these distances using radar, orbital periods, and tracking from space probes. An a.u. is equal to 92,900,000 miles (148,640,000 km).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_header(Aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_emphasis(spherical aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_emphasis(chromatic aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Flaw in how a telescope forms an image, resulting from the optical defects of lenses and mirrors; \x01\x0e+0200spherical aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is a limitation of the ability to bring light into perfect focus, and \x01\x0e+0200chromatic aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the appearance of rainbows around an image.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_listitem(Flaw in how a telescope forms an image, resulting from the optical defects of lenses and mirrors; \x01\x0e+0200spherical aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is a limitation of the ability to bring light into perfect focus, and \x01\x0e+0200chromatic aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the appearance of rainbows around an image.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_emphasis(Stellar aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200Stellar aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the apparent shift in the path of light from a star to the Earth due to the orbital motion of the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_listitem(\x01\x0e+0200Stellar aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the apparent shift in the path of light from a star to the Earth due to the orbital motion of the Earth.
