[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_header(Apollo Program)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Space program that landed 12 American astronauts on the moon. In his famous speech, President John F. Kennedy set the goal of \"landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.\" NASA tried several plans to reach the moon, but settled on a lunar orbit rendezvous. This called for a huge rocket to launch all the space vehicles at one time, and they would journey to the moon together.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Apollo Hardware)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Apollo Hardware\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(The spacecraft consisted of three parts: the command module, the service module, and the lunar module. The cone-shaped command module housed the astronauts on their journey. Behind that rode the cylindrical service module. It contained power and fuel to steer the spacecraft, as well as food and oxygen. Cameras and film, which had to be retrieved during a spacewalk, were stored in the service module. Stored behind the service module, the lunar module landed the astronauts on the moon and launched them back into lunar orbit. All of these parts were carried into space by the Saturn V rocket, but only the command module returned to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(The Missions)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The Missions\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(NASA tested the Apollo spacecrafts for the first few missions of the Apollo program. Three astronauts (Virgil I. Grissom, Edward H. White, and Robert B. Chaffee) died during a test in a tragic fire. This tradegy set back the Apollo program for 18 months. Then, Apollo 7 launched a command and service module into Earth orbit. They performed tests which NASA described as \"101 percent successful.\")
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Following the triumph of Apollo 7, Apollo 8 rocketed astronauts into lunar orbit. For the first time, humans escaped Earth\'s gravity and circled an alien world. The next two Apollo missions tested the lunar module and performed a dress rehearsal of the lunar landing.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Then, in July 1969, Apollo 11 met Kennedy\'s goal by landing Neil Armstrong and Edwin Aldrin in the Sea of Tranquillity. The two astronauts moonwalked for two and a half hours, collecting rocks and setting up experiments. Following this, Apollo 12 landed a few hundred yards from the Surveyor 3 probe that had landed on the moon 3 years before. After that came the unlucky voyage of Apollo 13. As the command module of Apollo 13 shot towards the moon, an oxygen tank exploded in the service module. For three days, the astronauts lived on minimal power in the lunar module. To the world\'s relief, the astronauts returned safely to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Apollo 14 marked a trend towards more serious science during lunar missions. During longer moon walks, astronauts triggered charges to test the moon\'s seismic properties. They also placed laser ranging reflectors on the surface that allowed scientists to measure the moon\'s distance within a few inches. Apollo 15 saw the first lunar rovers. Driving this battery-powered cart, astronauts explored the lunar surface miles away from their landing sight.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Apollo 16 made the only landing in the lunar highlands. Rocks collected here showed that the highlands are older than the lunar maria. Then, Apollo 17 made history, shattering all previous records. Astronauts stayed on the moon three days, logging 22 hours of moonwalks and collecting over 200 pounds of rock samples.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(After Apollo 17, NASA stopped the Apollo program. Budget cuts and lack of competition with the USSR gave little incentive to continue.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Apollo Missions That Carried a Crew)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Apollo Missions That Carried a Crew\x01\x0e-0100)
