[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_header(Kepler\'s Laws)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Three laws governing planetary motion observed by Kepler.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_list_start(, 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Every planet orbits the sun in an ellipse with the sun at one focus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_listitem(Every planet orbits the sun in an ellipse with the sun at one focus.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(As every planet orbits the sun it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_listitem(As every planet orbits the sun it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_emphasis(p)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_emphasis(a)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(There is a fixed ratio, p^(2)=a^(3) (where \x01\x0e+0200p\x01\x0e-0200 is the orbital period and \x01\x0e+0200a\x01\x0e-0200 is the length of the semimajor axis), that determines a planet\'s orbital period. Planets farther out take longer to complete an orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_listitem(There is a fixed ratio, p^(2)=a^(3) (where \x01\x0e+0200p\x01\x0e-0200 is the orbital period and \x01\x0e+0200a\x01\x0e-0200 is the length of the semimajor axis), that determines a planet\'s orbital period. Planets farther out take longer to complete an orbit.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. Every planet orbits the sun in an ellipse with the sun at one focus.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] \x01\x0e*032. 2. As every planet orbits the sun it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] \x01\x0e*033. 3. There is a fixed ratio, p^(2)=a^(3) (where \x01\x0e+0200p\x01\x0e-0200 is the orbital period and \x01\x0e+0200a\x01\x0e-0200 is the length of the semimajor axis), that determines a planet\'s orbital period. Planets farther out take longer to complete an orbit.
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_header(Andromeda Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Most distant object that can be seen with the naked eye. The Andromeda Galaxy, also called M31, is a spiral galaxy that is similar to the Milky Way but about twice as large. It appears as a fuzzy oval patch of light in the constellation of Andromeda. Observations of the galaxy reveal that it lies about 2.1 million light- years from Earth and contains about 300 billion stars. Images of the galaxy show light and dark bands, clusters, and supernovas. The Andromeda Galaxy has several satellite galaxies similar to the Magellanic Clouds. The Andromeda Galaxy, the Milky Way, and several smaller galaxies belong to a cluster called the Local Group.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.\x01\x0e-0100)
