[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Jupiter\'s equatorial bulge is perceptible at this scale - the equatorial diameter is 6.8% larger than the polar diameter, and it is 11.25 times wider than Earth\'s.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Jupiter\'s equatorial bulge is perceptible at this scale - the equatorial diameter is 6.8% larger than the polar diameter, and it is 11.25 times wider than Earth\'s.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Jupiter has lots of atmosphere! The upper deck of clouds is ammonia crystals. The lower deck is ammonium hydrogen sulfide tinted by molecules of sulfur. It is often stormy with thunder, lightening, and high winds.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Jupiter has lots of atmosphere! The upper deck of clouds is ammonia crystals. The lower deck is ammonium hydrogen sulfide tinted by molecules of sulfur. It is often stormy with thunder, lightening, and high winds.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_header(Jupiter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Fifth planet from the sun and the largest in the solar system. It is often said that, if Jupiter had just a little more mass, it would be a star. Actually, Jupiter would need to have about 100 times more mass to start fusion and become a star. So, saying Jupiter just missed being a star is about like saying Earth just missed being Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(With a radius of 41,504 miles (69,173 km), Jupiter is more massive than all the other planets in our solar system combined. Because of its large mass, Jupiter is a huge gravitational attractor. To escape Jupiter\'s gravity, you would need to travel 36.0 miles (60.0 km) per second. Jupiter\'s gravity is responsible for the Kirkwood gaps in the asteroid belt, major perturbations in the orbit of comets, and is partially responsible for its satellite Io\'s volcanic activity. Jupiter\'s magnetic field, which is the largest in the solar system, provides a link between Jupiter and Io because Io\'s ionosphere is pulled by Jupiter\'s magnetic field as Jupiter rotates. This magnetosphere is responsible for the radio waves given off by Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Since Jupiter is primarily a gaseous body, it rotates differently at different latitiudes: its poles rotate in 9 hours, 55 minutes, 40 seconds, while its equator rotates at 9 hours, 50 minutes, 30 seconds. It is only at great depths that Jupiter is thought to have a solid core of metallic hydrogen.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Jupiter has radiation belts that are similar to Earth\'s Van Allen Belts, but about 10,000 times stronger. Particles from the solar wind are trapped in the planet\'s magnetic field and create a deadly obstacle to anyone who wants to visit the planet.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Jupiter\'s most famous feature is its Great Red Spot, a 16,120 mile (26,000 km) wide storm that has endured for at least 100 years. In addition to the Great Red Spot, Jupiter\'s atmosphere contains white clouds, which are made of frozen ammonia and have temperatures of 140 K. Jupiter\'s bands of color come from the presence of ammonia and sulfur in its atmosphere, but the most plentiful gases in Jupiter\'s atmosphere are hydrogen and helium.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Moons and Rings)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons and Rings\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(In addition to its many (at least 16) moons, Jupiter sports a thin ring made of tiny particles that have come from either Io\'s volcanic activity, collisions of micrometeorites trapped in Jupiter\'s gravity, or cometary debris.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Interior)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Interior\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(Jupiter\'s interior is believed to have a temperature of 25,000 K and a pressure of 50,000,000 to 100,000,000 times that on the surface of Earth. Jupiter gives off more energy than it receives from the sun. Currently, this energy is thought to be left over from when Jupiter formed and produced heat as its heavier elements sank to the middle.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_double_emphasis(Moons of Jupiter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:57] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons of Jupiter\x01\x0e-0100)
