[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Introduction)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_blockcode(Welcome to Hyper\\-Quest\'s *Astronomica* encyclopedia. This is a full reference encyclopedia for both the astronomy student and the curious layman.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] To use this encyclopedia, click on the search button, then type a word or you may select one from the list by double\\-clicking there. Next, select an entry from the \"Go To\" list. Use the arrow buttons to scroll forward or backwards through the encyclopedia.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , (null))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(spherical aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(chromatic aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Flaw in how a telescope forms an image, resulting from the optical defects of lenses and mirrors; \x01\x0e+0200spherical aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is a limitation of the ability to bring light into perfect focus, and \x01\x0e+0200chromatic aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the appearance of rainbows around an image.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(Flaw in how a telescope forms an image, resulting from the optical defects of lenses and mirrors; \x01\x0e+0200spherical aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is a limitation of the ability to bring light into perfect focus, and \x01\x0e+0200chromatic aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the appearance of rainbows around an image.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(Stellar aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200Stellar aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the apparent shift in the path of light from a star to the Earth due to the orbital motion of the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(\x01\x0e+0200Stellar aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the apparent shift in the path of light from a star to the Earth due to the orbital motion of the Earth.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. Flaw in how a telescope forms an image, resulting from the optical defects of lenses and mirrors; \x01\x0e+0200spherical aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is a limitation of the ability to bring light into perfect focus, and \x01\x0e+0200chromatic aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the appearance of rainbows around an image.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] \x01\x0e*032. 2. \x01\x0e+0200Stellar aberration\x01\x0e-0200 is the apparent shift in the path of light from a star to the Earth due to the orbital motion of the Earth.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Absolute Magnitude)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Measure of the total light output of a star. Absolute magnitude is calculated by determining how bright a star would be if it were placed 32.6 lightyears (10 parsecs) away. By comparing absolute magnitude with apparent magnitude, astronomers can find the distance to the star. It is like watching two identical flashlights at different distances: by comparing how bright they appear you can determine which lies farther away.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Absolute Zero)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Lowest temperature possible. At absolute zero (-459.67\xc2\xb0 Fahrenheit; -273.15\xc2\xb0 Celsius) there is no energy available for transfer to other atoms. Therefore, the object is as cold as it can ever get.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Absorption Line)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(See Spectral Lines Or Spectroscopy.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Spectral Lines Or Spectroscopy.\x01\x0e-0200 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Absorption Spectrum)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Continuous spectrum that has black lines dividing it. These lines are caused by electrons absorbing radiation of a particular wavelength. Where these lines appear in the spectrum depends on the wavelength of the radiation absorbed by the electrons.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Accretion Disk)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Whirlpool-like disk formed by material falling onto the surface of a compact object, such as a black hole or a neutron star. If this compact object orbits a red giant, its gravity may strip gas from the outer layers of the red giant. This gas speeds up as it spirals inward, and its particles rub together, heating up to millions of degrees and radiating X-rays.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Achondrite)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Rocky meteorite.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Adams, John Crouch)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Adams, John Crouch (1819-1892))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Adams, John Crouch (1819-1892)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(British mathematician and astronomer who, independent of Leverrier, calculated the position of Neptune from gravitational disturbances (perturbations) in the orbit of Uranus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aerolite)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Fancy, largely obsolete, name for a meteorite.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Albedo)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Measure of how much light is reflected from a surface (like a planet or moon). If an object has an albedo that is almost one, such as a blanket of snow, then it reflects all the light that hits it. A body with an albedo close to zero, such as a block of coal, absorbs all light and is pitch black. The albedo of a body helps reveal the nature of its surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Albireo)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Second-brightest star in Cygnus. Albireo is famous as a beautifully contrasting double star. One is yellow while the other looks blue. The two stars appear 35 arc seconds apart and, like most double stars, can only be separated with a telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Alcyone)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The brightest member of the Pleiades star cluster. It twinkles at magnitude 2.9 and is about 240 lightyears away. Alcyone is a very hot star; its blue-white surface seethes at 36,0000\xc2\xb0 F (20,0000\xc2\xb0 C).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aldebaran)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(A red giant star, also called Alpha Tauri, that represents the red eye of Taurus as he charges towards Orion. It is 64 lightyears distant and 36 times wider than the sun. Aldebaran shines as brightly as 100 suns.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aldrin, Edwin Eugene)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aldrin, Edwin Eugene (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aldrin, Edwin Eugene (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Co-pilot of the first moon landing mission, on July 20, 1969. While assisting Neil Armstrong for a two and a half hour moonwalk during the Apollo 11 mission, he became the second person to tread on the surface of the moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Algol)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Famous blinking star that is 80 lightyears away in the constellation Perseus. Also called Beta Persei and the Demon Star, Algol represents the severed head of Medusa, whom Perseus killed with a shiny shield. Algol\'s brightness varies over a 2.87 day cycle due to a companion star that orbits in front of the main star and eclipses its light. Algol was first identified as an eclipsing binary star in 1782 by English astronomer John Goodricke. Later, astronomers discovered that Algol is actually a triple-star system.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Alpha Centauri)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Nearest star system to the sun. At only 4.3 lightyears away, Alpha Centauri is a triple-star system containing a red dwarf and two stars that are very similar to our sun. The third-brightest star in the sky, Alpha Centauri\'s three stars have a combined apparent light of -0.27 magnitude.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Altair)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Brightest star in the constellation Aquila. Altair is far hotter than and 1.5 times as wide as the sun. At sixteen lightyears away, Altair has an apparent magnitude of 0.77.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Altazimuth)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Crude mounting allowing a telescope to move up and down (altitude) and left and right (azimuth). These mounts, which are used on a Dobsonian telescope, do not allow a telescope to track celestial objects against the Earth\'s rotation as well as equatorial mountings do.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Altitude)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(How far an object lies above the horizon. Altitude is measured in degrees that are perpendicular to the horizon.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Anaxagoras)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Anaxagoras (c. 500- c. 428 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Anaxagoras (c. 500- c. 428 BC)\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Greek philosopher who believed that objects on the Earth and in the heavens were made from the same substance. He thought the sun was a large glowing rock, like a hot coal stuck in the sky. He correctly thought that the moon reflected light from the sun and therefore understood why the moon darkens during an eclipse.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Anaximander)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Anaximander (c. 610- c. 545 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Anaximander (c. 610- c. 545 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Greek philosopher and astronomer who is called the father of astronomy. He correctly thought the Earth is a cold, solid body. Unfortunately, he promoted the idea that stars sit fixed on a giant globe that rotates around a fixed Earth. This thinking was not corrected until 2,000 years later by Kepler and Galileo.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Andromeda)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Andromeda (The Princess))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Andromeda (The Princess)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Northern constellation that depicts the beautiful daughter of Cassiopeia chained to a rock, awaiting Cetus the whale. This constellation, which shares a star with the Great Square of Pegasus, is best observed in autumn. At the heart of the constellation lies the great Andromeda Galaxy (M31).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Andromeda Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Most distant object that can be seen with the naked eye. The Andromeda Galaxy, also called M31, is a spiral galaxy that is similar to the Milky Way but about twice as large. It appears as a fuzzy oval patch of light in the constellation of Andromeda. Observations of the galaxy reveal that it lies about 2.1 million light- years from Earth and contains about 300 billion stars. Images of the galaxy show light and dark bands, clusters, and supernovas. The Andromeda Galaxy has several satellite galaxies similar to the Magellanic Clouds. The Andromeda Galaxy, the Milky Way, and several smaller galaxies belong to a cluster called the Local Group.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Andromeda Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Most distant object that can be seen with the naked eye. The Andromeda Galaxy, also called M31, is a spiral galaxy that is similar to the Milky Way but about twice as large. It appears as a fuzzy oval patch of light in the constellation of Andromeda. Observations of the galaxy reveal that it lies about 2.1 million light- years from Earth and contains about 300 billion stars. Images of the galaxy show light and dark bands, clusters, and supernovas. The Andromeda Galaxy has several satellite galaxies similar to the Magellanic Clouds. The Andromeda Galaxy, the Milky Way, and several smaller galaxies belong to a cluster called the Local Group.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Andromeda Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Most distant object that can be seen with the naked eye. The Andromeda Galaxy, also called M31, is a spiral galaxy that is similar to the Milky Way but about twice as large. It appears as a fuzzy oval patch of light in the constellation of Andromeda. Observations of the galaxy reveal that it lies about 2.1 million light- years from Earth and contains about 300 billion stars. Images of the galaxy show light and dark bands, clusters, and supernovas. The Andromeda Galaxy has several satellite galaxies similar to the Magellanic Clouds. The Andromeda Galaxy, the Milky Way, and several smaller galaxies belong to a cluster called the Local Group.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M31- Andromeda galaxy with its two companion galaxies, NGC 205(top) and M32, which is close to the edge of the galaxy has distorted Andromeda\'s spirals.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Angstrom)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Length measurement equal to one ten-billionth of a meter. The angstrom is named after the Swedish physicist Anders Jonas Angstrom (1814-1874), who studied the spectrum of wave energy (visible light, ultraviolet rays etc.) from the sun. Angstroms are often used to express the wavelength of light, with visible light falling between 4000 and 7000 angstroms. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Angular Diameter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Measure, given in degrees, of how large an object appears in the sky. An interesting coincidence is that the sun and the moon show the same angular diameter (about one-half of a degree or about half the width of your finger held at arms length))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Angular Distance)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Measure, given in degrees, of the apparent separation between objects in the sky. For example, if you hold your fist at arm\'s length, your fist will be about 10 degrees wide.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Annular Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Annular means \"ring-like\" in Latin: Annular eclipses result when the moon, because it is close to the far point of its oval orbit, is too small to completely cover the sun during a solar eclipse. This results in a ring (\"annulus\") of sunlight. Even though the moon blocks most of the sun\'s light, it is not dark enough to see the sun\'s corona and it is unsafe to view without proper eye protection.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Antares)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Antares (Rival of Mars))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Antares (Rival of Mars)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Brightest star in Scorpius, with a magnitude of 1.1. It gets its name from its distinctive red hue and from the fact that the planet Mars occasionally drifts near the star. A strange companion, shining at magnitude 6.8, orbits Antares. In 1971, astronomers found radio emissions from Antares. They think these arise from glowing gas spiraling from Antares onto its companion.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Antimatter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Matter, with its electrical charges reversed. An atom of antimatter contains positively-charged electrons and negatively-charged protons. If matter and antimatter were to meet, they would explode in a shower of gamma rays.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Antimatter Cosmology)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(One question plagues cosmologists today: why does the universe seem to only contain ordinary matter? Big Bang theory predicts nearly equal amounts of matter and antimatter were created. Some cosmologists think just a bit more matter was created, with most of it annihilated early on. Others, antimatter cosmologists, think the universe is divided into regions of ordinary matter and antimatter. They think antisuperclusters contain antigalaxies. Except for charge, antimatter behaves like ordinary matter, so antiplanets could hold antiants and antielephants.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Antila)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Antila (the Air Pump))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Antila (the Air Pump)\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation located south of Hydra.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Antoniadi, Eugene M.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Antoniadi, Eugene M. (1870-1944))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Antoniadi, Eugene M. (1870-1944)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(French astronomer who made fine star maps, charting the positions of Mercury and Mars. Antoniadi invented the Antoniadi scale, which gives a measure of the quality of observing (called seeing). A 1 on the Antoniadi scale means perfect skies while a 5 means do not bother to step outside.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apastron)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Point in the orbit of double stars where they lie farthest apart.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aperture)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Diameter of the lens or mirror of a telescope. Aperture is the most important part of an astronomical telescope, measuring how much light a telescope can gather. Always rate a telescope on aperture and not on magnifying power. Never buy a telescope that is advertised by its magnification; aperture and quality are far more important.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aperture Synthesis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Used in radio astronomy to overcome the problem of steering one enormous dish. A computer drives many smaller antennas in unison and combines their data.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aphelion)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aphelion (Away from Sun))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aphelion (Away from Sun)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Point in an orbit that is farthest from the sun. It is directly opposite the perihelion, or closest point to the sun. The Earth is at it\'s aphelion in the month of July.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apogee)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Apogee (Away from Earth))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Apogee (Away from Earth)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Point in a satellite\'s (or the moon\'s) orbit where it is farthest from the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apollo Program)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Space program that landed 12 American astronauts on the moon. In his famous speech, President John F. Kennedy set the goal of \"landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.\" NASA tried several plans to reach the moon, but settled on a lunar orbit rendezvous. This called for a huge rocket to launch all the space vehicles at one time, and they would journey to the moon together.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Apollo Hardware)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Apollo Hardware\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The spacecraft consisted of three parts: the command module, the service module, and the lunar module. The cone-shaped command module housed the astronauts on their journey. Behind that rode the cylindrical service module. It contained power and fuel to steer the spacecraft, as well as food and oxygen. Cameras and film, which had to be retrieved during a spacewalk, were stored in the service module. Stored behind the service module, the lunar module landed the astronauts on the moon and launched them back into lunar orbit. All of these parts were carried into space by the Saturn V rocket, but only the command module returned to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(The Missions)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The Missions\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(NASA tested the Apollo spacecrafts for the first few missions of the Apollo program. Three astronauts (Virgil I. Grissom, Edward H. White, and Robert B. Chaffee) died during a test in a tragic fire. This tradegy set back the Apollo program for 18 months. Then, Apollo 7 launched a command and service module into Earth orbit. They performed tests which NASA described as \"101 percent successful.\")
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Following the triumph of Apollo 7, Apollo 8 rocketed astronauts into lunar orbit. For the first time, humans escaped Earth\'s gravity and circled an alien world. The next two Apollo missions tested the lunar module and performed a dress rehearsal of the lunar landing.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Then, in July 1969, Apollo 11 met Kennedy\'s goal by landing Neil Armstrong and Edwin Aldrin in the Sea of Tranquillity. The two astronauts moonwalked for two and a half hours, collecting rocks and setting up experiments. Following this, Apollo 12 landed a few hundred yards from the Surveyor 3 probe that had landed on the moon 3 years before. After that came the unlucky voyage of Apollo 13. As the command module of Apollo 13 shot towards the moon, an oxygen tank exploded in the service module. For three days, the astronauts lived on minimal power in the lunar module. To the world\'s relief, the astronauts returned safely to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Apollo 14 marked a trend towards more serious science during lunar missions. During longer moon walks, astronauts triggered charges to test the moon\'s seismic properties. They also placed laser ranging reflectors on the surface that allowed scientists to measure the moon\'s distance within a few inches. Apollo 15 saw the first lunar rovers. Driving this battery-powered cart, astronauts explored the lunar surface miles away from their landing sight.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Apollo 16 made the only landing in the lunar highlands. Rocks collected here showed that the highlands are older than the lunar maria. Then, Apollo 17 made history, shattering all previous records. Astronauts stayed on the moon three days, logging 22 hours of moonwalks and collecting over 200 pounds of rock samples.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(After Apollo 17, NASA stopped the Apollo program. Budget cuts and lack of competition with the USSR gave little incentive to continue.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Apollo Missions That Carried a Crew)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Apollo Missions That Carried a Crew\x01\x0e-0100)
