[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apollo-Soyuz Test Project)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(On the last Apollo mission, in July 1975, an American space capsule docked with the Soviet Soyuz craft using a special airlock. The mission tested this device, which was constructed to rescue stranded astronauts or cosmonauts.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apparent Magnitude)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Brightness of a star as it appears from Earth. The distance to the star as well as the star\'s absolute magnitude (real brightness) determine the apparent magnitude. By comparing a star\'s apparent magnitude with its absolute magnitude, astronomers can calculate the star\'s distance.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apsides)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Points where a satellite is closest to or farthest away from the body it is orbiting. For planets, the perihelion and aphelion are apsides. For a double star, the periastron and apastron are apsides.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Apus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Apus (The Bird of Paradise))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Apus (The Bird of Paradise)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Faint irregular constellation near Crux, around the celestial south pole.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aquarius)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aquarius (The Water Carrier))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aquarius (The Water Carrier)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Equatorial zodiacal constellation, best viewed in autumn. Aquarius shows a man pouring water from an urn on his shoulder. Aquarius contains no particularly bright stars, but it has several nebulas including the famous Helix nebula along with the globular cluster M2.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aquila)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aquila (The Eagle))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aquila (The Eagle)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Constellation that lies on the celestial equator and depicts an eagle being hunted by Hercules. The Milky Way wanders through this summer constellation. The brightest star, Altair, represents the eye of the eagle and, along with Deneb and Vega (in Cygnus and Lyra, respectively), forms the distinctive summer triangle. Several variable stars and nebulas reside in Aquila.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Ara)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Ara (The Alter))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ara (The Alter)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Feeble constellation, once part of the zodiac, lying south of Scorpius and containing no bright stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Arc Minute, Arc Second)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Tiny divisions of a degree. There are 60 minutes in a degree and 60 seconds in a minute (therefore 60 X 60 or 3,600 seconds in a degree). These angular units should not be confused with units of time. The full moon is approximately 30 arc minutes (one-half degree) across.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Arcturus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Bright orange star in Bootes. Arcturus is located by extending an arc from the handle of the Big Dipper (remember: arc to Arcturus). Shining at -0.06 magnitude, it is the fourth-brightest star in the sky and the brightest star in Bootes. This giant is 25 times wider than the sun, and as brilliant as 100 suns.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Arecibo Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(World\'s largest radio telescope, located in the mountains of Puerto Rico. Built in a natural crater in 1963, this 1,000 ft (305 m) radio dish cannot be moved, but it can receive signals in the sky from 43 degrees north to six degrees south.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Argo Navis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Large southern constellation depicting the ship of Jason and the Argonauts. Because this constellation was so large, astronomers divided it into four smaller constellations: Carina (the keel), Puppis (the stern), Pyxis (the navigational compass), and Vela (the sails).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Ariel)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Moon of Uranus discovered in 1851 by English amateur astronomer William Lassell. Ariel completes an orbit every 2.5 days at a distance of 119,000 miles (192,000 km) and is about 900 miles (1,500 km) across. Ariel is thought to consist of water ice mixed with some frozen methane.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aries)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aries (The Ram))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aries (The Ram)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small zodiacal constellation found between Taurus and Pisces that marked the location where the sun crossed the equator heading north 2,000 years ago. The sun only appears to circle the Earth, passing through the zodiacal constellations, when in reality the Earth orbits the sun. This event was called the vernal (or spring) equinox and named the first point in Aries. Right ascension (a form of celestial longitude) is measured from here, but the Earth\'s precession has caused the crossing point of the sun to drift away.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aristarchus of Samos)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aristarchus of Samos (280 BC-264 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aristarchus of Samos (280 BC-264 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Greek astronomer who first determined the relative sizes of the Earth, sun, and moon. Realizing that the sun is far larger than the Earth, he proposed that the sun, and not Earth, was the center of the universe. The Greeks rejected this claim, and the heliocentric (sun-centered) universe remained out of favor until the time of Copernicus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aristotle)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Aristotle (384 BC-322 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aristotle (384 BC-322 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Founding Greek philosopher whose geocentric (Earth-centered) model of the universe went unquestioned for 2,000 years. Not until the time of Galileo did scientists challenge Aristotle\'s beliefs. However, some of Aristotle\'s writings were correct. For example, he thought that the Earth was not flat, but spherical.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Armstrong, Neil Alden)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Armstrong, Neil Alden (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Armstrong, Neil Alden (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(American astronaut who started his career as a test pilot and whose first space mission was commanding Gemini 8 in 1962. During this flight, a malfunctioning thruster caused his capsule to spin out of control, forcing Armstrong to perform an emergency landing. Leading the Apollo 11 moon mission in July 1969, Armstrong became the first person to walk on the moon. After successfully landing the lunar module (he had only one second of fuel to spare!), he and Edwin Aldrin explored the Sea of Tranquillity for two and a half hours, collecting rocks and setting up experiments.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(The first step on the moon: July 20, 1969.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The first step on the moon: July 20, 1969.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Armstrong, Neil Alden)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Armstrong, Neil Alden (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Armstrong, Neil Alden (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(American astronaut who started his career as a test pilot and whose first space mission was commanding Gemini 8 in 1962. During this flight, a malfunctioning thruster caused his capsule to spin out of control, forcing Armstrong to perform an emergency landing. Leading the Apollo 11 moon mission in July 1969, Armstrong became the first person to walk on the moon. After successfully landing the lunar module (he had only one second of fuel to spare!), he and Edwin Aldrin explored the Sea of Tranquillity for two and a half hours, collecting rocks and setting up experiments.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(The first step on the moon: July 20, 1969.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The first step on the moon: July 20, 1969.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Armstrong, Neil Alden)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Armstrong, Neil Alden (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Armstrong, Neil Alden (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(American astronaut who started his career as a test pilot and whose first space mission was commanding Gemini 8 in 1962. During this flight, a malfunctioning thruster caused his capsule to spin out of control, forcing Armstrong to perform an emergency landing. Leading the Apollo 11 moon mission in July 1969, Armstrong became the first person to walk on the moon. After successfully landing the lunar module (he had only one second of fuel to spare!), he and Edwin Aldrin explored the Sea of Tranquillity for two and a half hours, collecting rocks and setting up experiments.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(The first step on the moon: July 20, 1969.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The first step on the moon: July 20, 1969.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Asterism)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Bright pattern of stars that forms a familiar figure with a well-known name but which is not a separate constellation. The Big Dipper, for example, is an asterism within the constellation Ursa Major. The belt of Orion and the Great Square of Pegasus, and the teapot of Sagittarius are also asterisms.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Asteroid)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small airless bodies usually found in the region between Mars and Jupiter, called the asteroid belt. The asteroids in this belt may represent a planet that never formed because it was caught in the gravitational tug of war between Jupiter and the sun. Some asteroids have nearly circular orbits, while others have highly elliptical orbits which take them across the orbits of several planets. These small bodies range in size from Ceres, 600 miles (940 km) across, to boulders a few feet (1 to 2 meters) in size. Occasionally, asteroids crash into one another and release dust particles that fall to Earth as meteors.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Discovery)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Discovery\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(In 1772, German astronomer Johann Bode predicted that a planet should reside in the space between Mars and Jupiter. Baron Franz Xavier von Zach decided that he would find this planet, so he formed a crew of astronomers, affectionately called the \"Celestial Police\", and set out to look for it. However, the first asteroid was found, not by a member of this group, but independently by Giuseppi Piazzi in 1801. But the band of astronomers persevered. German astronomer Wilhelm Olbers identified Pallas in 1802 and Vesta in 1807. Karl Ludwig Harding found Juno in 1804. The numbers of known asteroids grew rapidly. 100 were known by 1869, 200 by 1879. 300 asteroids had been identified by 1891 when Max Wolf employed photographs to search for more. With the aid of modern computers, astronomers now track about 2,500 asteroids. The total mass contained in all asteroids is about 0.0004 times Earth\'s mass.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Groups)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Groups\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Asteroids can be grouped by their position in the solar system. Most asteroids fall into 3 groups: the Main Belt, the Apollo and Amor group, and the Trojans.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The Main Belt comprises about 95% of all asteroids. They lie between 2 and 4 a.u.(1 a.u. = the distance from the Earth to the sun), between the orbits of Jupiter and Mars. Spaces called Kirkwood gaps divide the region at fractions of Jupiter\'s distance from the sun. Asteroids have been removed from here by repeated perturbations with Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The second group is the Apollo and Amor asteroids. These have more elliptical orbits that carry them from the inner rim of the main belt to the inner solar system. Some have highly tilted orbits that cross the path of Earth. The last set, Trojans, orbit at the same distance as Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Nature of Asteroids)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nature of Asteroids\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Most asteroids appear only as points of light. They change their brightness about 1.5 magnitudes, probably because of their rotation. Most are also irregularly-shaped, with albedos ranging from 7% to 18%. Astronomers think asteroids are cold solid bodies with rough stony surfaces and iron-nickel cores. These islands in space show scars from eons of impacts.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Origin of Asteroids)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Origin of Asteroids\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(In the late 1800\'s, Wilhem Oblers suggested asteroids represent material that never coalesced into a planet because of the gravitational tugs of Jupiter. Today, astronomers do not think there is enough mass to form a planet, but only dust that assembled 15-30 small bodies. Collisions between them fragmented the parent bodies into what we see today.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(ASTEROID FACTS:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100ASTEROID FACTS:\x01\x0e-0100)
