[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Astrometry)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Astrometry (Star Measure))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Astrometry (Star Measure)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Charting the positions of objects in the sky. Navigators and surveyors need precise listings of bright stars. Astronomer at places like the U.S. Naval Observatory and the Royal Greenwich Observatory spend a lot of time charting the positions of celestial bodies.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Astronomical Unit)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Astronomical Unit (a.u.))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Astronomical Unit (a.u.)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Measurement used to express the average distance from the Earth to the sun. For convenience, astronomers often express distances within the solar system in astronomical units. They measure these distances using radar, orbital periods, and tracking from space probes. An a.u. is equal to 92,900,000 miles (148,640,000 km).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Astronomy\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Study of space beyond Earth\'s atmosphere. Before the twentieth century, this term meant the charting of the heavens.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(History)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100History\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Astronomy is probably the oldest science. Prehistoric humans gazed at the night sky and invented stories from the patterns they traced out of the stars. The earliest written records of astronomical observations go back about 4,000 years to when ancient civilizations tracked stars to mark annual events. The Egyptians, for example, watched for Sirius to rise. They knew the flooding of the Nile would soon follow.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The Babylonians recognized that the moon repeats its phases every 8 and 19 years. They laid the foundation for the Greeks. The famous Greek philosopher Aristotle wrote that Earth must sit at the center of a perfect universe, an idea that survived 2,000 years. He thought the stars were crystal spheres hanging on a great transparent globe, turning around the Earth. He had a harder time explaining the planets.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Since the planets appear to change their position against the \"fixed\" backdrop of stars, the Greeks simply thought they revolved around the Earth. If this were true, the planets would move in a straight path around the sky. And they usually do. But sometimes, they appear to drift backwards, called retrograde motion. To explain this, Ptolemy added \"epicycles\", where a planet revolves in one direction around the Earth, but moves in the other direction in a smaller path. Imagine you are riding on a carousel, twirling a weight tied on a string above your head. If someone watched the weight from the center of the carousel, they wound see the weight seem to move with the platform, but also take sudden jumps backwards.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(De Revolutionibus Orbium Coelestium)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(After the fall of the Roman empire, the ancient Arabs preserved and added to Greek knowledge. As a result, many stars still have Arabic names today. By the 1500\'s, problems with the geocentric model grew worse. Astronomers had to add countless epicycles to explain motions they observed. To remedy this, a Polish astronomer-monk named Nicholaus Copernicus decided to place the sun at the center of the universe, with Earth as a planet that revolved around the sun. Although this was only a slightly more accurate predictor, it was simpler than the hundreds of epicycles used in the geocentric model. Copernicus published his heliocentric model of the univers in \x01\x0e+0200De Revolutionibus Orbium Coelestium\x01\x0e-0200 only a year before he died. Over the next 50 years, the sun-centered universe grew in popularity until the Catholic Church banned it.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(To settle the debate, a Danish astronomer named Tycho Brahe built a castle-observatory named Uraniborg on a small island north of Copenhagen. Over the next 20 years, he made the most detailed pre-telescopic observations in history. Later in his life, he met Johannes Kepler, a German mathematician who later analyzed Tycho\'s data. From this data, Kepler formulated his laws of planetary motion, showing that planets trace elliptical, not circular orbits. This explains why Copernicus\' heliocentric model was not accurate.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(About the same time as Kepler conceived his laws, Galileo Galilei trained the newly-invented telescope on the heavens. He found the universe alive with change. Craters and mountains dotted the moon. Venus showed phases. Moons circled Jupiter. He saw spots on the surface of the sun, proving it was not a flawless sphere. Gazing at the Milky Way, he beheld thousands of stars too faint too be seen with the naked eye.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(The Starry Messenger.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Galileo mocked the geocentric theory in his book \x01\x0e+0200The Starry Messenger.\x01\x0e-0200 This infuriated the Catholic Church, which banned his books and forced him to recant his arguments. But the damage was already done. Galileo inspired other scientists to experiment and observe the heavens.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The year Galileo died, Isaac Newton was born. While not professing to be an astronomer, Newton did more for astronomy than most observers. His laws of motion and gravitation, combined with his reflecting telescope caused some of the greatest advances astronomy had ever seen.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(During the 1800\'s, the science of spectroscopy (study of the spectrum of a radiating body) emerged from the work of Fraunhofer and his absorption lines. Astrophysicists like Norman Lockyer and William Huggins studied stellar motions using spectrographs. This brought astronomy into the twentieth century.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Great advances in our understanding of the universe come from improvements of photographic techniques and large telescopes. Henrietta Leavitt found the famous mass-luminosity ratio in Cepheid variables. Using this data, Edwin Hubble discovered red shifts in distant galaxies, showing that the universe is expanding. Recent trends have replaced individuals with teams of astronomers working with advanced equipment. Space probes like Voyager and Magellan revealed images of the planets Galileo only dreamed about. Astronomy advances every day and will continue to reshape our understanding of the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Astrophotography)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Use of plates, film, or electronic devices to collect light from celestial bodies. Astrophotography is the backbone of modern astronomy, opening vast new windows of the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(History)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100History\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The first use of photography in astronomy was in the 1840\'s when John W. Draper took a shot of the moon. Film used then was rather slow with exposure times of several minutes. The first photograph of a star was a 100 second exposure of Vega, taken by W. C. Bond in 1850 from the Harvard College Observatory. Then, in 1858, Warren de la Rue invented the spectroheliograph. With it, he started taking daily pictures of the sun. Since then, film has become about 50,000 times more sensitive.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Applications)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Applications\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Because photographs can accumulate light over time, they can reveal dimmer objects and greater detail than the human eye. Photos show changes too minute for any visual observer to notice such as stellar parallax and proper motion. Stellar brightness is compared on photographic plates, as are the drifts of planets, asteroids, and comets. Spectrographs of stars must be collected over time because they are too faint to be studied with the eye.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Recent Advances)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Recent Advances\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(During the 1980\'s, CCD (Charged Coupled Devices) were invented and first used by astronomers. Today, they have replaced traditional photographic plates in almost all major observatories. CCDs employ the same technology as home video cameras. A chip planted at their focus collects light on a grid of sensors that are like tiny buckets. They send signals to a computer, which processes the data and displays the image. Astronomers use the computer to enhance and manipulate images, unmasking details 100 times fainter than traditional plates.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(When CCD cameras appeared on the market, they were terribly expensive. But demand has driven the price down. CCDs are now growing more popular among amateur astronomers, who are enjoying their quality and convenience. Instead of taking a 45 minute photo on film and spending several hours developing it, amateurs use CCD\'s to get the same image in a 1 minute exposure displayed instantly on their computer. CCD technology has revolutionized modern astronomy.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Astrophysics)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Science of using the known laws of physics to study the heavens. Astrophysicists calculate how bodies behave under extreme conditions. For example, they have theorized how a star supports its own great weight through fusion and how energy is radiated by gas streaming into a black hole. Astronomers then look for these phenomena using photographs and spectrographs.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Atlas Rocket)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Rocket which launched capsules into space during the Mercury program. NASA continued to use these rockets, with the help of Agena and Centaur boosters, to launch satellites. The Atlas rocket, which was designed from military missiles, is 10 feet (3 m) wide and stands 82 feet (25 m) tall. The rocket weighs 300,000 pounds (140,000 kg).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Atom)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Building block of matter. An atom has three main parts: protons and neutrons which make up the nucleus, and electrons which swarm around the nucleus. An atom has an equal number of electrons and protons, and is classified by the number of protons in its nucleus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Atomic Time)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Method of tracking time based on the vibrations of the cesium atom. The cesium atom oscillates (vibrates) over 9 billion times every second. Counting these oscillations allows scientists to build a clock that is accurate to one second in ten thousand years. Astronomers use these clocks to measure the rotation of the Earth. Every few years, a leap second must be added to keep the atomic clocks in synch with the rotating Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(a.u.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(See Astronomical Unit)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Astronomical Unit\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Auriga)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Auriga (The Charioteer))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Auriga (The Charioteer)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Prominent pentagon-shaped northern constellation that is best viewed during winter. This constellation shares the star El Nath with the horn of Taurus. Auriga depicts a confused scene of a man driving a chariot and holding a baby goat. The bright yellow star, Capella (\"goat mother\" in Arabic), and the three dim stars nearby (the kids) make up Auriga..)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Aurora)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(aurora borealis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(aurora australis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Fluorescent sheets of light frequently seen near the north and south poles of the Earth. Named \x01\x0e+0200aurora borealis\x01\x0e-0200 in the north and \x01\x0e+0200aurora australis\x01\x0e-0200 in the south, these eerie lights dance red or green, and can come and go in a heartbeat, or remain for many minutes at a time.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(In order to study the northern lights, scientists have launched many rockets and satellites. They believe these lights arise from solar flares shooting towards the Earth. As these particles slam into the atmosphere, they cause oxygen and nitrogen molecules to glow. The sun emits these flares in an eleven-year cycle of violent activity. Recently, scientists found that auroras in the northern and southern hemispheres are linked by the Earth\'s magnetic field.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(An image of Earth in ultraviolet light shows a ring of aurora surrounding the magnetic north pole (marked with a cross). The aurora forms in this ring because the charged particles from the solar wind that induce it follow the lines of magnetic force (arrows).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100An image of Earth in ultraviolet light shows a ring of aurora surrounding the magnetic north pole (marked with a cross). The aurora forms in this ring because the charged particles from the solar wind that induce it follow the lines of magnetic force (arrows).\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(The beautiful northern lights can take many forms.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The beautiful northern lights can take many forms.\x01\x0e-0100)
