[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Baade Walter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Baade Walter (1893-1960))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Baade Walter (1893-1960)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Used the 100 inch (254 cm) Mount Wilson telescope in the 1940\'s to measure the distance to the Andromeda galaxy and found that it was twice as far away as anyone had previously thought. This caused astronomers to double the scale they were using to measure inter-galactic distances (the distances between galaxies), effectively doubling the size of the universe. Baade went on to become the first person to take pictures of individual stars in the Andromeda galaxy. Baade also discovered that there are two different types of stars, Population 1 and Population 2, that are separated by the amount of heavy metals that they contain. Population 2 stars are old and have few heavy metals, while Population 1 stars are young and have more heavy metals.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Background Radiation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Infrared and radio radiation coming from all directions in space with a current temperature of 2.726 K (just above absolute zero). This radiation is thought to be the remains of energy left over from the Big Bang. Its existence by Gamow and Dicke, was confirmed by Wilson and Penzias in 1965. You can detect this background radiation with your TV. If you turn it to a station that only shows static and turn the brightness down, then 1% of the snow you see is radiation left over from the Big Bang.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Baily\'s Beads)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Baily)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(s Beads)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Baily\x01\x0e-0100\'\x01\x0e+0100s Beads\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Beaded necklace around the moon seen by Francis Baily in 1836 during an annular eclipse. This effect happens because the moon has mountains and jagged edges, which break up the sun\'s light. When one bead outshines the rest, the moon sports a diamond in addition to a beaded necklace.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Barnard\'s Star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Second closest star system to the sun, lying in the constellation Ophiuchus. It is six lightyears away and approaching us at a velocity of 67 miles (108 km) per second, and it has the largest proper motion of any star. If you looked at the position of Barnard\'s star every night for 180 years you would find that it would move a distance across the sky equal to that taken up by the moon. It probably has two planets, whose masses are several times that of Jupiter, orbiting it.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Barycenter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Center of mass of two objects that are orbiting each other. The term barycenter is usually applied to the Earth - moon system.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Basin)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Circular dip left by a meteor on a surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bayer, Johann)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bayer, Johann (1572-1625))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bayer, Johann (1572-1625)\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(Uranometria)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(German astronomer who created a system, that we still use today, of assigning Greek and Latin letters to stars in the order of their brightness in the constellation where they are found. For example, the brightest star in Draco would be Alpha Draconis, the second brightest would be Beta Draconis and so on. In 1603, he published \x01\x0e+0200Uranometria\x01\x0e-0200, the first star atlas that covered the entire sky. It included over 2,000 stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bean, Alan Lavern)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bean, Alan Lavern (b. 1932))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bean, Alan Lavern (b. 1932)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(American astronaut who landed on the moon with the Apollo 12 mission and commanded the second Skylab space station and crew.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bessel, Friedrich Wilhelm)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bessel, Friedrich Wilhelm (1784-1846))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bessel, Friedrich Wilhelm (1784-1846)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The first person to measure the distance to a star, he went on to calculate the positions of more than 50,000 stars. In 1838, he calculated the distance to 61 Cygni, and his calculation of 10.3 lightyears is fairly close to the modern figure of 11.2 lightyears. In 1844 Bessel analyzed the motions of the stars Sirius and Procyon and determined that they must have unseen companions. Modern calculations showed Bessel to be correct: there are white dwarfs near both these stars. Bessel\'s method of looking at stars\' motions to determine if they have unseen companions is still used today.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Betelgeuse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Huge red giant star, about 650 lightyears away. It is so large that 300 of our suns could easily be slipped inside it with room to spare. It is a variable star whose size changes from 300 to 420 times the size of our sun every 400 days and whose magnitude changes from 0.4 to 0.9.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Special instruments called interferometers have shown this super-large star to have features similar to our own sun\'s bright and dark areas. Notice how Betelgeuse bulges out at its equator.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Special instruments called interferometers have shown this super-large star to have features similar to our own sun\'s bright and dark areas. Notice how Betelgeuse bulges out at its equator.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Betelgeuse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Huge red giant star, about 650 lightyears away. It is so large that 300 of our suns could easily be slipped inside it with room to spare. It is a variable star whose size changes from 300 to 420 times the size of our sun every 400 days and whose magnitude changes from 0.4 to 0.9.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Special instruments called interferometers have shown this super-large star to have features similar to our own sun\'s bright and dark areas. Notice how Betelgeuse bulges out at its equator.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Special instruments called interferometers have shown this super-large star to have features similar to our own sun\'s bright and dark areas. Notice how Betelgeuse bulges out at its equator.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Betelgeuse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Huge red giant star, about 650 lightyears away. It is so large that 300 of our suns could easily be slipped inside it with room to spare. It is a variable star whose size changes from 300 to 420 times the size of our sun every 400 days and whose magnitude changes from 0.4 to 0.9.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Special instruments called interferometers have shown this super-large star to have features similar to our own sun\'s bright and dark areas. Notice how Betelgeuse bulges out at its equator.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Special instruments called interferometers have shown this super-large star to have features similar to our own sun\'s bright and dark areas. Notice how Betelgeuse bulges out at its equator.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bethe, Hans Albrecht)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bethe, Hans Albrecht (b. 1906))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bethe, Hans Albrecht (b. 1906)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(In 1938, explained that stars use fusion as their source of power and, in 1967, was awarded the Nobel Prize in physics for this work.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Big Bang)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Most likely explanation for the beginning of the universe. This theory, which was originated by Friedmann and Lemaitre in the 1920\'s and adapted by Gamow in the 1940\'s, states that the universe was created around 15 billion years ago when the primitive fireball containing all space, time and matter began expanding. Before that, everything (space, time, and matter) existed in a space smaller than that taken up by the period at the end of this sentence. Since the laws of physics as we know them did not apply before the Big Bang, it is impossible to say exactly what form the matter took. As the universe expanded, it was not expanding into anything because nothing existed. It is best not to imagine this expansion from the outside in; instead, consider it from the inside out. In a sense, the universe created the space into which it expanded as it expanded.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(This theory, which rests on the assumption that the universe is homogenous (or the same everywhere) and that Einstein\'s theories of gravity are correct. This theory is supported by the presence of cosmic background radiation. The universe was unimaginably hot before the Big Bang, and as it began expanding it began cooling off. At the present time the temperature in the universe, as measured by the Cosmic Background Explorer, is 2.726 K.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Big Dipper)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(See Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Ursa Major.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Binary Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(See Double Star.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Double Star.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Binoculars)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Providing a telescope for each eye, binoculars allow you to see distant objects with a telexed, 3-D, effect.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Black Dwarf)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(White dwarfs that have completely cooled. It is not known whether our universe is old enough for any of these objects to have formed.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Black Hole)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(\')
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Compact, crushed object with incredibly large density. In theory, the space occupied by a black hole and where all the mass lies, takes up less space than the point of a pin. This is referred to as a singularity. Anything that goes closer than the event horizon (where the escape velocity becomes greater than that of light) gets pulled in by the black hole\x01\x0e+0100\'\x01\x0e-0100s tremendous gravity. Once inside a black hole, nothing can ever escape. Black holes may form in several ways. For example, they may be the core remains of a supernova whose mass was more than three times that of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(When a star can no longer fuse atoms to produce the energy necessary to hold up its outer layers, then these layers collapse on the core. When the final mass of a star is larger than about three solar masses, the weight of its outer layers is too great to be stopped by any known force. The outer layers crash down on the core continually collapsing in on itself; this results in a black hole.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Since nothing can escape from black holes, it is impossible to detect them directly. Instead, black holes can be detected by looking at the effects of their gravity on nearby stars. For example, the X-ray system Cygnus X-1 is thought to contain a black hole because it has been determined that one of the companions in this system has such a huge mass that it could only be a neutron star or a black hole. Also, bursts of X-rays have come from this system, which are thought to be due to the black hole pulling in pieces of its companion, which emit the x-ray energy as they swirl into the black hole at high speeds. This swirling matter gradually spiraling into the black hole is known as an accretion disk. Additionally, in May of 1994, it was announced that the Hubble Space Telescope has found conclusive evidence of a black hole in the middle of the galaxy M87, 50 million lightyears away. This black hole, whose mass is thought to be two billion times that of the sun, was detected through the presence of a cloud of gas swirling around it at 1.2 million miles per hour. This gas, which is thought to be whirling in toward the black hole, has a temperature of 18,0000 F. Both the speed and the temperature of this gas cloud convinced astronomers that it is being sucked in by a black hole. Astronomers also think that there may be similar black holes at the center of other galaxies, including the Milky Way.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The astrophysicist Stephen Hawking has speculated about the existence of another kind of black hole, with a mass similar to that of an asteroid, which formed billions of years ago out of the heat from the Big Bang. These mini black holes could create protons, anti-protons, and other subatomic particles. These black holes, however, would eventually disappear because, while creating electrons and anti-electrons (which would annihilate each other), the black holes would lose their energy and \"evaporate.\" )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Blue Giant)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Big, hot, brilliant stars, with a size around ten times that of the sun, a temperature of about 36,0000 F (or 20,000 K), and a brightness greater than that of 20,000 suns. The Pleiades are examples of blue giants. Because they are so big and hot, blue giants burn themselves out and die relatively quickly.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Blue Moon)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(\')
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Expression which means \"not very often.\" Originally, it may have referred to the occurrence of two full moons in one month or to the color the moon sometimes takes because of volcanic ash in the Earth\x01\x0e+0100\'\x01\x0e-0100s atmosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bode, Johann Elert)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bode, Johann Elert (1747-1826))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bode, Johann Elert (1747-1826)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(German astronomer who, in 1801, created the first accurate chart of all the stars that can be seen by the naked eye.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bode\'s Law)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(String of numbers that match most planetary positions. Take the numbers 0, 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96, 192, and 384, add four to each of them, and divide by 10. The result comes near to the distance, expressed in astronomical units, to most of the planets in our solar system. This law is actually misnamed because it was not Bode who created it. A German mathematician named Johann Daniel Titus proposed it in 1772; Bode merely popularized it.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bok, Bart J.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bok, Bart J. (1906-1983))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bok, Bart J. (1906-1983)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(The Milky Way)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Dutch-born American astronomer who is known for his study of Bok globules (small dark globules of gas that stand out against a bright background) and the Milky Way. He and Priscilla Bok popularized astronomy when they wrote \x01\x0e+0200The Milky Way\x01\x0e-0200 in 1941.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bolide)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Exploding meteor.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Booster)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Boosters, called strap-on boosters, get used and thrown away after the lift-off.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(Boosters, called strap-on boosters, get used and thrown away after the lift-off.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(First stage of a launch rocket.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(First stage of a launch rocket.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. Boosters, called strap-on boosters, get used and thrown away after the lift-off.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] \x01\x0e*032. 2. First stage of a launch rocket.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bootes)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bootes (The Herdsman))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bootes (The Herdsman)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Large northern constellation that is best seen in the spring and lies near Ursa Major and Corona Borealis. The brightest star of Bootes is the orange giant Arcturus. Bootes also contains double stars such as Epsilon Bootis, which is also called Izar and Pulcherrima.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Borman, Frank)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Borman, Frank (b. 1928))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Borman, Frank (b. 1928)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Commander of the Apollo 8 mission, which orbited the moon 10 times between December 24 and 25, 1968.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Bradley, James)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Bradley, James (1693-1762))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Bradley, James (1693-1762)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(English astronomer who discovered not only nutation, but also the aberration of starlight.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Brahe, Tycho)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Brahe, Tycho (1546-1601))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Brahe, Tycho (1546-1601)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Danish astronomer who originally planned to study law but was so impressed by the sight of a solar eclipse in 1560 that he gave up law and turned to astronomy. After discovering that the star observations existing at his time were not accurate, he decided to make his own. Brahe\'s observations, which were accurate enough for Kepler to use as a basis for his calculations of laws of planetary motion, challenged the common notion of an unchanging starry universe. Included in these observations was the sighting of a 1572 supernova in Cassiopeia, named Tycho\'s supernova. Brahe, however, clung to a geocentric view even though a 1577 comet, traveling among the stars, demonstrated that the planets do not orbit the Earth on crystal spheres as was once thought.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Although he was able to give up the traditional ideas of a perfect realm of stars and crystal spheres on which planets orbited, he could not give up the idea of everything orbiting the Earth. Instead, he proposed a system in which the other planets and the comets orbit the sun while the sun and the moon orbit the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Brown Dwarfs)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Objects that would be stars, except that they are just shy of the amount of mass needed to start nuclear reactions. No brown dwarfs have ever been conclusively found, but there is evidence that there may be some forming in the cloud Chameleon I, and there may be some in the Pleiades. Brown dwarfs would have a mass somewhere between Jupiter and the lowest mass stars. Some astronomers think brown dwarfs may account for some of the dark matter in the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Burbidge, Eleanor Margaret and Geoffrey)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Burbidge, Eleanor Margaret (b. 1922) and Geoffrey (b. 1925))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Burbidge, Eleanor Margaret (b. 1922) and Geoffrey (b. 1925)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(British astrophysicists who, in 1956, determined, along with William Fowler and Fred Hoyle, that some elements that are heavier than hydrogen are created in stars, through fusion. This went against the previously held notion that all elements were produced in the Big Bang. The Burbidges also observed that Quasars have red shifts in their spectral lines, showing that they are giving off matter. Also, Eleanor Burbidge used the rotation of galaxies to estimate their masses.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Caelum)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Caelum (The Chisel))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Caelum (The Chisel)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small, faint, southern constellation lying south of Orion.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Calendar)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Calendar)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Calendar\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Defined system used to measure the year and then divide it into smaller units, such as months, weeks, and days. The modern calendar for most of the world is the Gregorian calendar, which was instituted in 1582 and is 365.2422 days long. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The earliest calendars were probably based on the phases of the moon. Using this method, it can be determined that the length of the month is 29.53 days. Twelve lunar months form a lunar year; thus the lunar year is only 354.36 days long, about 11 days shorter than the time it actually takes the Earth to circle the sun. This difference of days caused the calendar to get out of step with the agriculture and solar year. Julius Caesar realized this and, with the aid of the astronomer Sosigenes, worked to get the calendar back in sequence. In their calendar, called the Julian calendar, every fourth year was a leap year.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(However, with this calendar, there were three leap years too many every 385 years. This seemingly slight error caused the Julian calendar to be 10 days off by the year 1545. A new calendar was ordered by Pope Gregory XIII, and it was delivered to him with the help of Christopher Clavis. In this new calendar, called the Gregorian calendar, a leap year can only occur in years equally divisible by four. Century years must be exactly divisible by 400 to be leap years.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Callisto)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Second largest moon of Jupiter (3,100 miles; 4,900 km). This outermost Galilean moon is the darkest and least dense of Jupiter\'s moons, and its surface is pitted with craters, showing that has not been altered by geological activity since its formation. On Callisto, there is a giant meteor impact point 1,612 miles (2,600 km) wide named Valhalla.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Caloris Basin)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Eight hundred mile (1,300 km) wide impact basin, the most striking feature of Mercury.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Camelopardalis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Camelopardalis (The Giraffe))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Camelopardalis (The Giraffe)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Large, dim constellation near Polaris that was first outlined by the German mathematician Jakob Bartsch, who said it was the camel that carried Rebecca to Isaac.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cancer)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Cancer (The Crab))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cancer (The Crab)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Faintest zodiacal constellation. It lies near Hydra, is most visible in winter, and is the home of the open cluster Praesepe (M44, the Beehive).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cancer, Tropic of)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Furthest point north of the equator where the sun passes directly overhead. The sun appears overhead at this latitude (23.5\xc2\xb0) at the summer solstice.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Canes Venatici)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Canes Venatici (The Hunting Dogs))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Canes Venatici (The Hunting Dogs)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Northern constellation lying between Ursa Major and Bootes, containing the beautiful Whirlpool galaxy (M51).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Canis Major)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Canis Major (The Greater Dog))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Canis Major (The Greater Dog)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Prominent southern constellation near Puppis that contains Sirius, the brightest star in the sky. It is best seen during the winter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Canis Minor)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Canis Minor (The Lesser Dog))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Canis Minor (The Lesser Dog)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small constellation lying near Gemini. Both this and Canis Major represent the dogs of Orion, at whose feet they rest.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Canopus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Second brightest star in the sky (mag. -0.72). Since this yellow supergiant is so bright and is separated from other stars, it is often used for navigation by spacecraft. Canopus is 600 lightyears away and has a diameter 25 times that of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cape Canaveral)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Location on Florida\'s Atlantic coast and the site of the Kennedy Space Center, which has been the launch site of American spacecraft since 1947. From 1963 to 1973, Cape Canaveral was named after John F. Kennedy. Cape Canaveral\'s Vehicle Assembly Building (VAB), which was enlarged to accommodate the space shuttle, is so large that it contains enough air to have its own weather patterns.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Capella)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Capella (Alpha Aurigue))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Capella (Alpha Aurigue)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Double star system whose members are three times as large as the sun. With an apparent magnitude of 0.09, this is the seventh brightest star in the sky.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Capricorn, Tropic of)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Southernmost latitude (-23.50) where the sun appears directly overhead. This event happens on the winter solstice.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Capricornus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Capricornus (The Sea Goat))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Capricornus (The Sea Goat)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small zodiacal constellation near Sagittarius that is best seen during summer.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Carbonaceous Chondrite)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Type of meteoroid containing carbon and chondrites which, excluding its volatile elements, has a chemical composition similar to that of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cardinal Points)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(North, East, South, and West.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Carina)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Carina (The Keel))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Carina (The Keel)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Southern constellation near Puppis that contains the bright star Canopus and the variable star Eta Carinae and is well known for its many nebulas.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Carpenter, Malcolm Scott)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Carpenter, Malcolm Scott (b. 1925))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Carpenter, Malcolm Scott (b. 1925)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(On May 24, 1962 became the second American to orbit the Earth, circling it three times.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cassegrain Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Type of reflecting telescope in which light falls on the primary concave mirror, then is reflected onto another concave mirror, which then reflects the image through a hole in the primary mirror to the eye piece. It was invented in 1672 by the French physicist Jacques Cassegrain.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(Also See Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200Also See Telescope\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cassini, Giovanni Domenic)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Cassini, Giovanni Domenic (1625-1712))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cassini, Giovanni Domenic (1625-1712)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Italian-French astronomer who calculated the rotation period of Jupiter to within four minutes and later did the same for Mars. In 1668 he made accurate tables of the orbits of four satellites of Jupiter, which were later used by Roemer to determine the speed of light. Cassini later became director of the Paris Observatory, where he discovered four satellites of Saturn and, in 1675, noticed a gap in the rings of Saturn, which was later named for him. This gap showed that the rings were not solid and led Cassini to postulate that they were composed of countless particles, each following an orbit determined by Kepler\'s laws. In 1672 Cassini, helped by Richer, determined the distance to Mars, giving us an accurate scale of distances in the solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cassini Division)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Seventeen hundred mile (2,700 km) wide elliptical gap between the A and B rings of Saturn that was first noticed by Giovanni Cassini. Although this gap is not completely empty, its particles are removed by gravitational effects of Saturn\'s moon Mimas.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cassiopeia)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Northern constellation that is named for a queen from Greek mythology but looks more like the letter W at first glance. The top of this W is like a finger pointing to the North Star.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Castor)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(One of the two bright stars of Gemini. The closer you look at Castor, which is 45 lightyears from us, the more stars it appears to have. From a distance, it looks like just one star. With a telescope, you can see that the one star is actually two stars with magnitudes of 1.96 and 2.89 which orbit each other every 420 years. Special instruments have revealed that these two stars are actually each double stars. But that is not all: Castor has two hidden red dwarfs, bringing its final star total to 6.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Catadioptric System)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Type of telescope, which is basically a corrected reflecting telescope combining reflecting and refracting features, that was pioneered in the 1940\'s by Bernard Schmidt.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Celestial Equator)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Projection of the Earth\'s equator onto the celestial sphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Celestial Mechanics)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Branch of astronomy that deals with the movement and gravitational interactions of bodies in space. Astronomers use celestial mechanics to calculate the orbits of astronomical bodies and spacecraft. Celestial mechanics was born when Isaac Newton derived his three laws of motion in 1687. He discovered that all bodies attract each other by the force of gravity. By using celestial mechanics astronomers have discovered that Jupiter and Saturn perturb (alter) the orbit of Haley\'s comet. Leverrier and Adams also used celestial mechanics to determine the position of Neptune by examining its effect on the orbit of Uranus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Celestial Sphere)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Hollow globe thought by ancient astronomers to encircle the Earth. Celestial objects were thought to rest on this sphere, which rotated about the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Centaurus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Centaurus (The Centaur))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Centaurus (The Centaur)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Southern constellation lying near Lupus and the Crux. Centaurus contains Alpha Centauri, the closest star system to the sun and the third brightest star in the sky, and a globular cluster called Omega Centauri, the brightest globular cluster in the sky.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Center of Mass)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Imaginary location of all an object\'s mass. This location is used to calculate an object\'s gravitational attraction.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cepheid Variable)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Class of stars named after Delta Cephei, which was the first Cepheid variable star discovered. The mass and brightness of these stars change in a regular rhythm, and the length of time it takes them to go from one size to another is linked to their absolute magnitude. Because of this relationship, which was calculated by Henrietta Leavitt in 1912, it has been possible to determine the distance to galaxies where other Cepheids have been found. For example, once the period of brightness change for a cepheid has been determined, it is a simple matter to determine the star\'s real or absolute brightness (magnitude). Brighter stars have longer period. Comparing the absolute magnitude to the apparent magnitude then gives the distance.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cepheus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Northern constellation lying near Cassiopeia and Draco that depicts Cassiopeia\'s husband. It contains the star Delta Cephei, the first Cepheid variable identified.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Ceres)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(With a diameter of 567 miles (913 km), Ceres, the largest of the known asteroids, contains one-third the mass of all the asteroids. At 257,120,000 miles away from the sun, Ceres\' orbit takes 1,682 days. Ceres, at magnitude 6.9, just unable to be seen with the unaided eye, was the first asteroid to be discovered.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cernan, Eugene Andrew)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Cernan, Eugene Andrew (b. 1934))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cernan, Eugene Andrew (b. 1934)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(In addition to being the commander of Apollo 17, the last Apollo mission to the moon, he, along with Jack Schmitt, holds the record for the longest lunar exploration, spending 22 hours romping around the surface over a three day visit in 1972.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cerro-Tololo Inter-American Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Observatory that is located on Cerro-Tololo mountain in Chile and has the largest telescope (158 inches, 400 cm) in the southern hemisphere. It is managed by the Association of Universities for Research in Astronomy (AURA). This observatory is respected for its work on the Magallanic clouds, the Milky Way, and optical studies of X- ray sources.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cetus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Cetus (The Whale))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cetus (The Whale)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Equatorial constellation near Pisces that is easiest to see during the fall. Cetus contains Mira (which means \"wonderful\"), a star which changes its brightness erratically and often disappears completely.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Chamaeleon)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Chamaeleon (The Chameleon))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Chamaeleon (The Chameleon)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Faint southern constellation lying near Musca and Octans.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Chandrasekhar, Subrahmanyan)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Chandrasekhar, Subrahmanyan (b. 1910))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Chandrasekhar, Subrahmanyan (b. 1910)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Indian-born astrophysicist who challenged the assumption that all stars, no matter what their mass, would become white dwarfs. Chandrasekhar found that stars ending their lives with masses greater than 1.44 times the mass of the sun could not support their weight as white dwarfs and instead collapsed into neutron stars. The 1.44 sun masses became known as the Chandrasekhar limit.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Chondrite)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Stony meteorites that carry with them tiny bits of rock, called chondrules. Eighty-four percent of all meteorites are chondrites.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Chromatic Aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Failure of a lens to focus all wavelengths of light properly. Lenses work by bending light. Because different colors of light are in different wavelengths, lenses bend them by different amounts. If chromatic aberration occurs, then, as you look through the lens, you will see blue closest to the lens, then green, then yellow, then orange, and finally red. Since different lenses bend light by different amounts, chromatic aberration can be corrected either by combining two types of lenses or by using a reflecting telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Chromosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Pinkish-red outer layer of the sun that is only visible during total solar eclipses. So dim that the rest of the sun outshines it, the chromosphere is composed of gas that is about 10,000 miles (16,000 km) thick and ranges in temperature from 5,000\xc2\xb0 K to more than 1,000,000\xc2\xb0 K. Because the chromosphere is so thin compared to the rest of the sun, it does not seem to put out much light at all. The chromosphere sputters and shoots out jets of hot gas that are called spicules, which can reach up to heights of 10,000 miles (16,000 km). Thanks to these spicules, the chromosphere is able to throw material out to the corona.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Circinus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Circinus (The Drawing Compass))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Circinus (The Drawing Compass)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation near Centaurus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Circumpolar Objects)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Celestial objects that do not seem to rise or set but instead orbit one of the poles. The closer one lives to the poles, the more circumpolar objects will be present.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Clark, Alvan)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Clark, Alvan (1804-1887))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Clark, Alvan (1804-1887)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Produced refracting telescopes that were so large they beat the world record five times. One of his company\'s telescopes, a 40 inch (101 cm) refractor, is still the world\'s largest. His son George, while testing a telescope, found the Pup (Sirius B ), Sirius\' white dwarf companion.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Clementine)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(American lunar spacecraft that was designed to test the durability of electronic components. Clementine had a CCD camera, one camera for visual and infrared wavelengths, and two cameras for near and far infra- red wavelengths. It also had two cameras, called star trackers, that were the main parts of its military objective. Clementine was to orbit the moon and then fly by the asteroid Geographos. However, due to an error that let the engines run for 11 minutes and thus depleted the supply of rocket fuel, Clementine was not able to make the asteroid flyby. This mission was otherwise a stunning success due to its extraordinary mapping of the entire moon\'s surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Coalsack)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Forty lightyear-wide dark nebula, 400 lightyears from us, that appears as a hole in the sky surrounding the Southern Cross. Its hole-like appearance comes from the fact that this gas cloud, whose mass is more than 100 times that of the sun, blocks or dims the light of the stars behind it.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Collins, Michael)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Collins, Michael (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Collins, Michael (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Command-module pilot on July 1969\'s Apollo 11 flight; he remained in Columbia while Neil Armstrong and Edwin Aldrin landed on the moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Columba)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Columba (The Dove))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Columba (The Dove)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small southern constellation located near Canis Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Coma)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Flaw in a telescope that distorts stars in the edge of the field into a comet or pear-shaped object.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(Flaw in a telescope that distorts stars in the edge of the field into a comet or pear-shaped object.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Envelope of gas surrounding the nucleus of a comet.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(Envelope of gas surrounding the nucleus of a comet.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
