[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Conrad, Charles)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Conrad, Charles (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Conrad, Charles (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(American astronaut who commanded both the second Apollo mission to land on the Moon and the first crew at Skylab.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Constellation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Apparent pattern of stars in the sky. Because of the human tendency to see familiar objects in random patters, most cultures who have looked to the skies devised their own constellations. Few of these have survived however. Most of the constellations used today, including the twelve zodiacal constellations, are the ones that were listed by Ptolomy in 150 BC. The zodiacal constellations represent Greek mythological figures and constellations are given Latin names and follow Latin rules for case endings. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Constellation Facts)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Constellation Facts\x01\x0e-0100)
