[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Continuous Spectrum)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Rainbow of colors produced when white light shines through an object such as a prism.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Copernicus, Nicolaus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Copernicus, Nicolaus (1473-1543))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Copernicus, Nicolaus (1473-1543)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Polish astronomer who revived and popularized the heliocentric (sun centered) view of the universe. Although the ancient philosophers Ecphantus, Heraclides, Hicetas, and Philolaus had all speculated that the universe was heliocentric, the predominant view in Copernicus\' time was that of the geocentric universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(De Revolutionibus Orbium Coelestium)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph( In 1543, Copernicus published his ideas of a heliocentric universe in \x01\x0e+0200De Revolutionibus Orbium Coelestium\x01\x0e-0200 (On the Revolution of Celestial Spheres). He believed that the Earth and the other planets made circular orbits about the sun. He offered both philosophical (such as, the sun is so majestic, where else would it be but at the center of the universe?) and mathematical (such as the modern view that there are seasons because the Earth is tilted on its axis and, as it orbits the sun, the northern and southern hemispheres receive direct light at different times) support for his view.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(De Revolutionibus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Although Andreas Osiander, a Lutheran theologian, added an anonymous preface to \x01\x0e+0200De Revolutionibus\x01\x0e-0200, which stated that the ideas in the book were just models that could be used to predict celestial motions, it is fairly certain that Copernicus felt that the universe actually was heliocentric.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Core)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Center regions of a body.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Coriolis Effect)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Phenomenon named after Gaspard Gustave de Coriolis, who first described it in 1835. Due to the rotation of the Earth, if a shot is fired from the equator toward either the north or the south, it will veer off its target.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Corona)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Outer layer of the sun. The corona is made of plasma. Although its size varies with the sun\'s magnetic activity, the corona is generally about 8,000,000 miles (13,000,000 km) thick and has a temperature of roughly 2,000,000\xc2\xb0 K. Even though it is very hot, its low density prevents it from emitting much heat because there are few gas molecules to give off energy. The corona\'s brightness is just half that of the moon, and it is only visible during total solar eclipses.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Corona Australis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Corona Australis (The Southern Crown))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Corona Australis (The Southern Crown)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Southern constellation that is the counterpart of the Corona Borealis. It lies near Sagittarius and includes a globular cluster that is about 14,000 lightyears away.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Corona Borealis)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Corona Borealis (The Northern Crown))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Corona Borealis (The Northern Crown)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Northern constellation that is the counterpart of the Corona Australis. It lies between Hercules and Bootes and is best viewed during the summer. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Corvus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Corvus (The Crow))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Corvus (The Crow)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Equatorial constellation that is shaped like a lopsided square and lies in the neighborhood of Virgo. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_emphasis(See Background Radiation.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Background Radiation.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cosmic Ray)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Radiation composed of charged particles whose exact origin is uncertain. This radiation is made of protons (the nuclei of hydrogen atoms), alpha particles (the nuclei of helium atoms), the nuclei of other atoms (such as beryllium, boron, and lithium), electrons, positrons, and some antiprotons.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The particles that make up cosmic rays move at nearly the speed of light. The precise origin of cosmic rays is still unclear. Once, they were thought to come from supernovas, but that theory has been rejected. Currently, it is thought that cosmic ray particles reach their incredibly high energy levels when they bounce off of magnetic shock waves. As they bounce around, the particles are thought to accumulate energy from the magnetic shock waves, which are thought to have received their energy from supernovas. However, the accuracy of this theory is being questioned because it still does not explain the high energy levels of some of the cosmic ray particles. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cosmology)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Study of the past, present, and future of the universe. Ancient cosmologists thought that the Earth was the center of the universe, but modern cosmologists realize that the Earth is just an ordinary planet orbiting an ordinary star. Cosmologists\' ability to study the entire universe when they can see just a tiny piece of it rests on their theory that the universe is homogenous, or the same all over. Given the ordinariness of the Earth and sun, there does not seem to be any reason to think otherwise.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(When cosmologists look out into the universe, they see that galaxies farther away, and farther back in time, are closer together. interpreting from this closeness, cosmologists believe that the universe began with the Big Bang, which caused everything to keep expanding. Looking ahead, they predict four possible fates of the universe:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list_start(, 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The Big Crunch: There is enough mass in the universe for the force of gravity to overcome the force of expansion, and the universe will collapse.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(The Big Crunch: There is enough mass in the universe for the force of gravity to overcome the force of expansion, and the universe will collapse.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The Big Bounce: Like the Big Crunch, but after the universe collapses, there will be another Big Bang. Big Crunches and Big Bangs will continue forever.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(The Big Bounce: Like the Big Crunch, but after the universe collapses, there will be another Big Bang. Big Crunches and Big Bangs will continue forever.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The force of gravity is equal to the force of expansion. The universe will stop expanding, but it will not start to collapse. Instead, it will be forever balanced between expansion and collapse.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(The force of gravity is equal to the force of expansion. The universe will stop expanding, but it will not start to collapse. Instead, it will be forever balanced between expansion and collapse.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(The force of gravity will not be able to stop the expansion of the universe, and the expansion will continue forever.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(The force of gravity will not be able to stop the expansion of the universe, and the expansion will continue forever.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. The Big Crunch: There is enough mass in the universe for the force of gravity to overcome the force of expansion, and the universe will collapse.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] \x01\x0e*032. 2. The Big Bounce: Like the Big Crunch, but after the universe collapses, there will be another Big Bang. Big Crunches and Big Bangs will continue forever.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] \x01\x0e*033. 3. The force of gravity is equal to the force of expansion. The universe will stop expanding, but it will not start to collapse. Instead, it will be forever balanced between expansion and collapse.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
