[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cosmos Satellites)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Descendants of the Soviet Sputnik series of satellites. Cosmos satellites made their debut on March 16, 1962.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Crab Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Crab Nebula (M1))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Crab Nebula (M1)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Gaseous remains of the supernova that took place 6,300 lightyears away in 1054 AD. In the middle of this mass of gas is the remains of a star that exploded. Although it used to be the central core of a star, during the explosion it collapsed into a rapidly spinning neutron star called a pulsar. The nebula is now expanding around this pulsar. The Crab Pulsar bathes the nebula in deadly radiation. Bursts of X-rays, visible light, and radio waves shoot away from the pulsar, causing the nebula to glow.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Crater)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Bowl-like indention due to a meteorite smashing into a body. Just after the solar system formed and the planets had cooled, the interplanetary space was littered with randomly orbiting debris. Some of this debris occasionally slammed into a planet or moon. The surface of Mercury, as well as that of most of the moons and asteroids, still shows the signs of this early stage of development.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Crepuscular Rays)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Beams streaming away from the sun at dawn or dusk. They are caused by light shining through breaks in the clouds and reflecting off atmospheric dust.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Crux)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Crux (The Southern Cross))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Crux (The Southern Cross)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Small but distinctive southern constellation, near Musca, that was separated from Centarus by Royer in 1673.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Culmination)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Highest point reached by a star. An object\'s culmination happens as it crosses the meridian.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_listitem(Highest point reached by a star. An object\'s culmination happens as it crosses the meridian.
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] 
