[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Curvature of Space)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Ripple in space from the gravitational force of an object. Scientists once thought of space in terms of Euclidean geometry, where the shortest distance between two points is a straight line. The surface of the Earth, however, is not like that, and neither is space. On Earth, the shortest path between New York and England is not a straight line, but a curve that crosses over Ireland.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(According to Einstein\'s theory of relativity, space may be distorted by massive objects in such a way that it cannot be thought of in Euclidean terms. If space were a giant trampoline, masses such as the Earth, the sun, and black holes would be basketball-like objects. If set on the trampoline, they would make a curve-like depression, and the shortest distance between two points on the curve would be a curve, not a straight line. Consequently, although it is clear that space is not a simple Euclidean structure, its exact nature is still unknown.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cygnus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Cygnus (The Swan))
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cygnus (The Swan)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Northern constellation, also called the Northern Cross, that is easiest to see during the summer. It is located in a crowded part of the Milky Way near Vega. The brightest star in Cygnus is Alpha Cygni which is part of the summer triangle. 61 Cygnus was the first star other than the sun to have its distance to us measured. Some of the several nebulas included in Cygnus are the North American nebula and the Veil nebula. It is even possible that Cygnus includes an exploding galaxy (Cygnus A, which is giving off strong radio signals). There may even be a black hole in Cygnus. Astronomers think that the X-rays given off by Cyg X-1 are evidence for a black hole. Visually, Cygnus is interesting because it contains Deneb, one of the brightest stars in the sky and the most luminous of all the major stars. It shines with the light of 60,000 suns.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Cygnus X-1)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(A source of violent bursts of X-rays first detected by X-ray observing satellites. Lying in the constellation of Cygnus, it is thought to be a black hole swallowing the outer layers of its companion star.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Dark Matter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Mass which is thought to make up 90 to 99% of the universe. Cosmologists have determined that, for the universe to exist, it must have a critical mass of around six atoms of hydrogen per cubic meter. If it had more, it would have collapsed soon after the Big Bang, and, if it had less, it would have expanded too quickly for stars and galaxies to form.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(According to these calculations, there is not enough visible matter for the universe to exist so, if their theories are correct, there must be some undetected matter. Support for the existence of dark matter comes from its gravitational effects on visible objects. For example, the movements of distant stars may be slowed down by the gravity of dark matter. This matter may exist in the form of \"shadow matter,\" which is a mirror image of our own matter and would only interact with our matter through gravity.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(More likely explanations involve brown dwarfs or other small, dim, undetectable stars. Scientists have found 10% of the expected dark matter through looking at its gravitational effects on visible matter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Day)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Planet\'s rotation period, which can be measured with respect to either the stars (sidereal day) or the sun (solar day). Sidereal and solar days are not quite equal because the sun\'s location among the background of stars changes during the planet\'s orbit. Earth\'s sidereal day measured in mean solar time is 23 hours, 56 minutes, and 4 seconds. Earth\'s 24 hour day (mean solar day) comes from measuring its rotation, with respect to the sun, at an average location in Earth\'s orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Declination)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Equivalent of latitude on the celestial sphere. Degrees north of the celestial equator are positive, while those south of it are negative.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Deep Sky Object)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Any visible object not belonging to our solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_header(Degenerate Star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_double_emphasis(Degenerate star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Degenerate star\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:58] render_paragraph(Stars such as white dwarfs and neutron stars that, due to their immense weight, have gone through some gravitational collapse and contain degenerate matter. Degenerate matter is made of atoms whose electrons have been separated from their nuclei by gravity. Gravity forces these electrons to bind with protons, forming neutrons, and these neutrons are then packed tightly together. In normal atoms more than 99% of the atom\'s volume is empty space. In degenerate matter nearly all of this space has been removed. Thus when a star like our sun becomes degenerate in the form of a white dwarf, it will shrink to about the size of the Earth. A star more massive than 1.44 suns (Chandrasakhar limit) will shrink to about the size of a city in becoming a degenerate neutron star.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Deimos)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Smallest and most distant of Mars\' two moons, lying 14,661.8 miles (23,458.9 km) from its center. Deimos completes its orbit, and rotates, in 30 hours, 17 minutes, and 54.87 seconds. The dark, dust covered surface of this irregularly shaped moon (9 X 7.5 X 7 miles or 15 X 12 X 11 km) is speckled with tiny craters. Deimos is thought to be a captured asteroid.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Delphinus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Delphinus (The Dolphin))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Delphinus (The Dolphin)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Northern constellation lying between Pegasus and Aquila that has four stars which form a diamond-shaped asterism named Job\'s Coffin.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Delta)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Rocket, introduced in 1960, that has a liquid-fuel second stage and an optional solid-fuel third stage. It is able to launch up to 3, 600 pounds (1, 630 kg) into a 230 mile (370 km) orbit. It has launched hundreds of communications and scientific satellites, some Explorer probes, Echo, Relay, Syncom, and Tiros. It was modeled after Thor intermediate-range missiles.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Deneb)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(White supergiant star, 1,500 lightyears away, that has an apparent magnitude of 1.26 and is the brightest star in the constellation Cygnus. Deneb forms the tail of the swan and one apex of the summer triangle. It is among the most luminous of the night\'s bright stars and shines with the light of 50,000 suns.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Descending Node)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(See Nodes.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Nodes.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dichotomy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The point at which the moon, Mercury, or Venus is at exactly half phase.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dicke, Robert Henry)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Dicke, Robert Henry (b. 1916))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Dicke, Robert Henry (b. 1916)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American physicist who, in the 1960\'s, experimentally demonstrated the principle of equivalence, which states that all objects fall with the same velocity when subjected to the force of gravity. In 1964, he proposed searching for the cosmic background radiation (which has since been found) that is thought to be left over from the Big Bang.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Differential Rotation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Various parts of gaseous objects spinning at different rates. This occurs in stars, gaseous planets, and galaxies; the sun, Jupiter, and Saturn, for example, all display differential rotation. All parts of solid object such as the Earth, however, must rotate at the same rate.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Differentiation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Layering of a gaseous body so that its densest parts are at the center. You can see this process at work when you dump some sand into a cup of water. After the water is stirred, the sand will sink to the bottom.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Diffraction)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Bending of electromagnetic radiation (light, ultraviolet waves, etc.) as it goes around objects. Radiation with a longer wavelength is bent more. Diffraction causes Airy disks, or circles of light with faint rings around them, in telescopes that do not have good resolution. Diffraction is one reason why shadows do not have sharp edges.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Diffraction Grating)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Device with a series of narrow parallel slits that separates light into its spectrum of colors. When you look at the back of a CD (compact disk), you can see the same process as white light encounters narrow bumps and produces a rainbow.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dione)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Satellite of Saturn discovered in 1684 by Giovanni Cassini. Its diameter is 694 miles (1,120 km), and it is 233,990 miles (377,400 km) from Saturn\'s center. During its 2 day, 17 hours, and 41 minute orbit, the same side of Dione always faces Saturn. It has an icy surface and was once geologically active.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dispersion)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Separation of radiation into its component wavelengths through refraction. A common example of this is using a prism to form a spectrum out of white light or a rainbow as sunlight passes through water droplets in the atmosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Distance Determination of Stars and Galaxies)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Calculation of how far away something is. The most direct and accurate way of determining distances to nearby stars is through parallax. Getting accurate parallaxes of nearby objects (less than 100 light years) is very important as it represents the first step in obtaining distances in the universe. Most other techniques which are used at greater distances have been calibrated using parallax. One important mission of the Hubble Space Telescope will be to increase the accuracy of presently accepted parallaxes.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(For clusters and other objects beyond the reach of parallax measurements we turn to Cepheid variables, whose pulsation rate is related to their absolute magnitude, or by examining main sequence stars. Since the relation between luminosity, temperature, and absolute magnitude of main sequence stars is known, it is possible to calculate their distance. At distances farther than 1,000,000 lightyears, it is possible to use the expansion of the universe to determine distance. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Since more distant objects are moving away form us more quickly, the speed of an object can be used to determine its distance. It is, however, still tremendously difficult to make distance calculations, and astronomers usually allow themselves a relatively large percentage error.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dobson, John)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Legendary amateur astronomer who has popularized astronomy since the 1960\'s. He and his friends, the San Francisco Sidewalk Astronomers, have toured national parks in the western United States, introducing astronomy to millions of visitors. As a master telescope maker, Dobson ground some of the finest amateur reflectors from porthole glass. He built all of his telescopes from left-over supplies. This type of simple, sturdy construction has become so popular among amateurs, that they are now called \"Dobsonians\" )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dobsonian Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Inexpensive, basic telescope that is mounted on an open-topped plywood box and has a tube that is generally made of stiff cardboard. These telescopes are prized by amateur astronomers for their economy. They were popularized by John Dobson, a San Francisco amateur astronomer.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dollfus, Audouin Charles)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Dollfus, Audouin Charles (b. 1924))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Dollfus, Audouin Charles (b. 1924)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(French astronomer who found Janus, the tenth moon of Saturn to be discovered, on December 15, 1966.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Doppler Effect)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Change in the frequency of an objects radiation as it moves closer to and farther away from an observer. This effect, which was first studied by Christian Johann Doppler in 1842, is similar to change in the sound of a siren as it approaches and then goes away from you. As it comes toward you, its siren seems to increase in pitch, and, as it goes away from you, its siren seems to decrease in pitch. Coming toward you, the sound waves radiating out from the siren are pushed together into a lower wavelength and higher frequency. As it goes away from you, the waves appear to stretch out, and the opposite happens.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The same is true for light, which also travels in waves. By studying the light waves from stars and galaxies, astronomers can tell whether they are moving towards us or away from us. Objects moving towards us have their light waves compressed into shorter wavelengths with higher frequencies and are said to be \"blue shifted\". Objects moving away from us have their light red shifted as their light waves are stretched into longer waves with lower frequencies. Red waves are the longest waves our eyes can see.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dorado)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Dorado (The Swordfish))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Dorado (The Swordfish)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Southern constellation lying near Mensa. It contains the Larger Magellanic Cloud and includes the Tarantula nebula, which surrounds the star 30 Doradus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Double Star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Two stars that orbit around a common center of gravity. Forty-six percent of all stars are thought to be part of double star systems, and 39% of these stars are thought to be part of multiple star systems (with more than two stars). The orbits of double stars, which follow very regular paths, may be as short as a few hours, as in the case of Algol, or as long as millions of years, as in the case of Proxima and Alpha Centauri.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The closer two stars are, the shorter their orbital period. Some stars, called contact binaries, are so close that they actually touch. Many of these are spectroscopic binaries because they can only be resolved into two stars with the aid of a spectroscope. Stars this close are thought to have formed out of one cloud that collapsed into the two stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Double stars with more separation may be distinguishable if the moon goes in front of them because their light will blink out in two steps. If double stars pass in front of each other as seen from Earth during their orbit, they are called eclipsing binaries. The brighter star in double star systems is called the primary, and the dimmer one is called the secondary. Often two stars will start out separately, but, as the primary grows, it will donate some material to the secondary. If the secondary star is a white dwarf and enough material is added to it for it to pass the Chandrasekhar limit, then it may explode into a Type I supernova.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Some stars, called optical doubles, only appear to be doubles; one is actually lightyears farther away than the other.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Draco)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Draco (The Dragon))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Draco (The Dragon)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Constellation whose star Thuban was over the north pole in about 2832 BC but lost its position there because of precession. Draco, which also contains a planetary nebula that is 1,700 lightyears away, winds between the two dippers.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Drake, Frank Donald)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Drake, Frank Donald (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Drake, Frank Donald (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American radio astronomer who, during Project Ozma in 1960, aimed an 85 foot (26 m) radio telescope at the stars Tau Ceti and Epsilon Eridani and became the first person to search for alien signals. Drake also derived an equation to calculate the probability of finding aliens. This equation states that the number of advanced civilizations in the Milky Way is equal to the average rate at which stars in the Milky Way form, times the fraction of stars with planetary systems, times the number of planets that are ecologically able to support life, times the fraction of planets where life develops, times the fraction of planets where life develops into an intelligent form, times the fraction of planets where intelligent life invents systems to communicate with other planets, times the average lifetime of advanced civilizations. Using assumptions and approximations for all his variables, Drake deduced that there are around 1,000,000 advanced civilizations in the Milky Way.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Draper, Henry)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Draper, Henry (1837-1882))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Draper, Henry (1837-1882)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American who, in 1872, aimed his camera at Vega and took the first successful photograph of a star\'s spectrum. Later, he recorded the spectrum of the Orion nebula.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dreyer, Johan Ludwig Emil)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Dreyer, Johan Ludwig Emil (1852-1926))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Dreyer, Johan Ludwig Emil (1852-1926)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American who put together the New General Catalogue of Nebulas and Clusters of Stars (NGC). The NGC, which was first published in 1888, included 7,840 objects and updated Herschel\'s catalog.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dwarf Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Stars with low to average luminosity, mass, and size that are generally red. Most main sequence stars are dwarf stars. These are not to be confused with white dwarfs.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Dyson, Sir Frank Watson)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Dyson, Sir Frank Watson (1868-1939))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Dyson, Sir Frank Watson (1868-1939)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(English astronomer who examined the chromosphere and corona of the sun during eclipses. During a 1919 eclipse, he helped to confirm Einstein\'s theory of general relativity by determining that light is bent by the gravity of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Earth)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Third planet from the sun and the only place in the universe known to support life. It is 1 a.u. (92,960,000 miles or 149,573,000 km) from the sun. Its 18.5 mile (29.8 km) per second orbit takes 365.25 days, and its revolution takes 23 hours, 56 minutes, and 4 seconds.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Nitrogen makes up 78%, oxygen 21%, and a mixture of argon, carbon dioxide, water vapor, and other gases makes up the remaining 1% of the Earth\'s atmosphere. At lower altitudes, the water vapor particles combine to form clouds. From 90 miles (140 km) and up, the Earth is surrounded by a magnetosphere, which is in the form of two donut shaped zones named the Van Allen belts and which traps ions, most of which come from the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Surface)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Surface\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The Earth\'s surface is covered mostly with water. This water is divided several oceans and many seas, rivers, lakes, and streams. Its land masses are divided into seven continents: Africa, Asia, Australia, Europe, North America, South America and Antarctica. At one time, many or all of these land masses may have been connected in one giant continent called Pangea, but, due to the movement of the Earth\'s plates, they have been separated. There are many types of land on the Earth, from dry, flat deserts to lush fertile country and from areas that are covered with trees to gently sloping meadows where sturdy weeds grow.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Interior)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Interior\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(From the outside in, the Earth is made of a 22 mile (35 km) deep crust, a Mohorovicic discontinuity (or Moho), a mantle which extends down to 1,800 miles (2,900 km), and a core. The outer part of the core is thought to be solid and made entirely of iron. There is thought to be an electric current in the outer part of the core, which is believed to be responsible for over 90% of the Earth\'s magnetic field.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Earthshine)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Ghostly illumination of the dark portion of the crescent moon by the Earth\'s reflection of sunlight. It is our own light, reflected back to our eyes from the moon\'s surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eccentricity)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Measure of an orbit\'s elongation. Circles have an eccentricity of zero, and parabolas have an eccentricity of one. Ellipses have eccentricities between zero and one. The Earth\'s orbit has a very low eccentricity of 0.02 (nearly circular) while a look at the orbit of Halley\'s Comet reveals a rather high eccentricity.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Echo Satellites)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Series of passive reflector communications satellites that started with Echo 1 on August 12, 1960. Echo 2 was the product of the first joint US-USSR space venture.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(One celestial body passing into the shadow of another. Eclipses are named after the object that is blocked from sight.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Lunar Eclipses)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lunar Eclipses\x01\x0e-0100: When the moon passes into the Earth\'s shadow. This can only happen during a full moon, when the Earth is between the sun and the moon. There is not a lunar eclipse with every full moon because the plane of the moon\'s orbit is tilted 5\xc2\xb0 from the plane of the ecliptic, thus the moon does not always pass through the shadow of the Earth. When the moon crosses completely into the Earth\'s umbra, there is a total eclipse. Total lunar eclipses can last as long as 100 minutes. Plus, it can take two hours for the moon to enter and leave the Earth\'s shadow.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Solar Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Solar Eclipse\x01\x0e-0100: When the moon moves between the Earth and the sun, blocking some of the sun\'s light. Since the moon must come between the Earth and the sun, solar eclipses can only occur when there is a new moon. Like lunar eclipses, solar eclipses do not occur every month because, due to the 5\xc2\xb0 tilt of the moon\'s orbit, the Earth, moon and sun are not always lined up when there is a new moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Annular)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Annular\x01\x0e-0100 \x01\x0e+0100Eclipse\x01\x0e-0100: When the moon is at or near its apogee (farthest point in its orbit) and appears too small to fully block out the sun, a bright ring (annulus) of sunlight surrounds the moon. When the moon is at or near perigee (closest point in its orbit), a total eclipse occurs. The farther away the Earth is from the sun, the longer the total eclipse lasts. The only time it is possible to see the outer layers of the sun is during a total eclipse. The rest of the time, these layers are outshone by their brighter neighboring layers .)
