[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eclipsing Binary)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Double star system in which one star,as seen from Earth, drifts in front of the other. During its orbit the light output from the star system varies in a steady pattern. A primary eclipse is when the fainter star passes in front of the brighter one, and the system\'s light output drops the most. A secondary eclipse is when the brighter star passes in front of the dimmer one, and the light output drops slightly. The size of the stars involved can be determined by measuring how long it takes for one star to pass completely in front of the other. Algol, seen by Goodricke in 1782, was the first star to be identified as an eclipsing binary.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Ecliptic)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Plane in which the Earth revolves around the sun. It gets its name from the occurrence of lunar and solar eclipses when the moon is crossing this plane. Most of the orbits of the other planets in our solar system lie near the plane of the ecliptic. The plane of the ecliptic is not exactly perpendicular to the Earth\'s axis of rotation, and the 23.5\xc2\xb0 angle between the Earth\'s equator and the plane of the ecliptic is what causes our seasons. It is also referred to as the \"obliquity of the ecliptic.\")
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eddington, Sir Arthur Stanley)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Eddington, Sir Arthur Stanley (1882-1944))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Eddington, Sir Arthur Stanley (1882-1944)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(British astrophysicist who, during the 1919 total eclipse, led a team of scientists in measuring the sun\'s gravitational bending of light and helped to confirm Einstein\'s relativity theory. In 1924, he determined the mass-luminosity ratio relation of stars, which says that the mass of a star determines its brightness, and he calculated the density of Sirius B.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Einstein, Albert)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Einstein, Albert (1879-1955))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Einstein, Albert (1879-1955)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Special Theory of Relativity)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Physicist best known for his theory of relativity. His \x01\x0e+0200Special Theory of Relativity\x01\x0e-0200, published in 1905, stated that mass, length, and time all appear to vary, but the speed of light is constant. This was in contrast to Newton\'s theories of gravity, which state that measurements such as mass, length, and time are absolute and unchanging.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(General Theory of Relativity)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(In 1915, he included gravity in his theory and published the \x01\x0e+0200General Theory of Relativity\x01\x0e-0200, which was proven to be at least partially correct in 1919 when it was observed during a solar eclipse that the sun\'s gravity does bend light. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(In 1921, he won the Nobel Prize in physics for his 1905 explanation of the photoelectric effect. He said that if light is considered as a flow of particles, the fact that electrons are given off from surfaces that are hit by light makes sense.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Einstein\'s theories are being further tested by space probes such as Ulysses and Galileo. These spacecrafts are looking for gravitational waves that, if Einstein\'s theories are correct, are caused by supernovas and other catastrophic events. If one of the spacecraft encounters one of these waves, it will wobble. Through examination of the spacecraft\'s radio signals, this wobble can be detected from Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Electromagnetic Radiation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Energy that travels in a vacuum at the speed of light and is visualized as both a wave and a stream of particles. Depending on its wavelength, it may be (from shortest to longest) radio waves, microwave, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, or gamma-rays.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Electromagnetic Spectrum)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(All wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation. From longest to shortest wavelength: radio, microwave, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Electron)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Along with protons and neutrons, one of the building blocks of atoms. There are as many electrons surrounding the nucleus as there are protons in the nucleus. Electrons are very light compared to protons, and they carry a charge equal and opposite of that of the proton, giving the atom an overall zero charge.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Elements)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Basic building blocks of matter; each element has a different number of protons in its nucleus. It is possible to determine which elements are present in stars by looking at their spectra. Analyses of the surface elements of stars which have remained the same since their formation show that the Milky Way probably formed from a cloud of hydrogen and helium. The heavier elements present were produced in old stars through fusion and spread throughout the solar system by supernovas.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Cosmic Element Amounts in the Universe)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cosmic Element Amounts in the Universe\x01\x0e-0100)
