[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Emission)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Creation of electromagnetic radiation (light, ultraviolet waves, etc.) which happens when electrons around the nucleus of an atom change their distance from the nucleus. By examining the wavelengths of the radiation, it is possible to determine what elements are present in an object.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Emission Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Cloud of gas that glows as it emits light. This is similar to the way light is produced in a fluorescent light bulb. An emission nebula is usually red.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Emission Spectrum)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Bright lines across a black background. Emission spectra are caused by electrons, having absorbed energy and moved up an orbital level, falling down to their original level. When they do this, energy is released as light and appears in the spectrum as bright lines.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Enceladus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Saturn\'s third moon at 147,572 miles (238,020 km) away from its center takes 1 day, 8 hours, 53 minutes, and 22 seconds to complete one orbit. It is 313.75 miles (502km) wide, and has a very smooth surface apparently because it is continuously re-coated with ice when its volcanoes spew out water. Enceladus, with an albedo of one, is the most reflective body in our solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Encke, Johann Franz)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Encke, Johann Franz (1791-1865))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Encke, Johann Franz (1791-1865)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(German astronomer who used pre-existing observations of Venus\' motions to determine that the Earth is over 95 million miles from the sun. In 1819, he calculated the 3.3 year period of Encke\'s comet, then the smallest period of any known comet. He then used Mercury and Jupiter\'s gravitational effects on the comet\'s orbit to determine their masses. In 1837 he discovered Encke\'s division, a gap in the outer ring of Saturn.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Encke\'s Comet)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Comet that is the cause of the Taurid meteor shower. Encke\'s comet has a 2 mile (3.2 km) wide nucleus and a mass of about 100 billion tons. Encke\'s comet loses 1/200 of this mass with each orbit. It was discovered by Jena Louis Pons in 1818 and named after Johann Franz Encke.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Ephemeris)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Record of past, present, and future positions of a comet, planet, or satellite. Ephemerides have been made since at least the fourth century BC. Although it is relatively easy to confidently predict the position of planets and satellites, it is more difficult with comets because it is often possible to see only part of their orbits.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Equation of Time)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Equation that corrects the discrepancy between apparent and mean solar time, necessary when reading time from a sundial. This discrepancy comes from the sun\'s varying position in the sky during the Earth\'s orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Equator)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Imaginary line that is an equal distance from each of the poles of a rotating celestial object.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Equatorial Mounting)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Means of placing a telescope in which one axis, called the polar axis, is aligned with the Earth\'s axis. Consequently, it is easy to correct for the effects of the Earth\'s rotation by just rotating the telescope about the polar axis. In contrast, the Altazimuth mounting does not line up with the Earth\'s axis, making it necessary to move the telescope in two planes to correct for the Earth\'s rotation.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Equinox)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Equinox (Equal Night))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Equinox (Equal Night)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The occasion when the sun crosses the celestial equator, making the lengths of day and night equal everywhere in the world. The autumnal, or fall, equinox takes place around September 23, and the vernal, or spring, equinox takes place around March 21.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Equuleus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Equuleus (The Horse))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Equuleus (The Horse)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Faint, tiny, northern constellation that lies next to Pegasus. It contains no bright stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eratosthenes of Cyrene)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Eratosthenes of Cyrene (c. 276-c. 194 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Eratosthenes of Cyrene (c. 276-c. 194 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Greek astronomer who was the first person to correctly measure the size of the Earth. He used the angle of the sun\'s shadow at noon in two different towns, Alexandria and Syene, and the distance between the towns to set up a proportion with the 360 degrees in a circle and the unknown size of the Earth. When Columbus set out to sail from Europe to the Indies, he used a smaller estimate of the Earth\'s size, which had been determined by Poseidonius, and underestimated the distance.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eridanus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Eridanus (The River))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Eridanus (The River)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Long southern constellation that starts at the celestial equator near Rigel and meanders towards its brightest star, Achernar. Eridanus also contains a spiral galaxy and the triple star system Omicron Eridani. Scientists have directed radio telescopes at Epsilon Eridani in hopes of detecting alien signals because it is one of the nearest sun-type stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eros)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Eros (Minor Planet 433))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Eros (Minor Planet 433)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(One of Amor group\'s asteroids and the first asteroid found to travel mostly inside Mars\' orbit. Discovered by Gustav Witt and Auguste Charlois in 1898, Eros is 15 miles (24 km) long and rotates once every 5 hours and 16 minutes. Its orbit takes 1.8 years, and its distance from the sun varies from 1.1 a.u. to 1.8 a.u. In January 1975, when it was just 14 million miles (225 million km) from Earth, it could be seen through a telescope as a seventh-magnitude body. Because Eros is so close to the Earth, its parallax was used to determine the distance from the Earth to the sun before radar was used.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Escape Velocity)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Speed an object must attain to get out of a gravitational field. The strength of the gravitational field determines the escape velocity. The Earth\'s escape velocity is 7 miles (11 km) per second. Some objects have so little mass that their escape velocities are not large enough to hold on to gas particles, and, consequently, they have no atmosphere. On the other hand, Black Holes have an escape velocity that exceeds the speed of light.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eudoxus of Cnidus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Eudoxus of Cnidus (c. 400-c. 350 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Eudoxus of Cnidus (c. 400-c. 350 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Greek astronomer who created a complicated system of spheres to explain the motion of planets because he believed, like Aristotle, that everything in the heavens was perfect and traveled in circular orbits. He also introduced geometry to astronomy, and he may have been the originator of a geometric method of calculating the distance from the Earth to the sun and moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Europa)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Smallest of Jupiter\'s Galilean moons. At 415,900 miles (670,900 km) from Jupiter\'s center, its orbit takes 3 days, 13 hours, 17 minutes, and 53 seconds. With a mass 0.66 times that of Earth\'s moon, Europa is 1,946 miles (3,138 km) across and has a density three times that of water. Its incredibly smooth surface (it is the smoothest satellite in our solar system) is made of ice with cracks that have been filled in with more ice.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(European Southern Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Observatory with a 142 inch (360 cm) reflecting telescope that is located on Cerro La Silla, one of northern Chile\'s mountains, at an altitude of 7,870 feet (2,400 m). Since its opening in the 1960\'s, it has been run through the cooperation of Belgium, Denmark, France, Germany, the Netherlands, and Sweden.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(European Space Agency)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(European Space Agency (ESA))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100European Space Agency (ESA)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Collaborative effort by Austria, Belgium, Denmark, France, Germany, Ireland, Italy, the Netherlands, Spain, Sweden, Switzerland, and the United Kingdom that, on August 9, 1975, launched its first satellite, COS-B, which was used to examine gamma-rays. ESA also assembled Spacelab, the laboratory and workshop that rides in the payload bay of the space shuttle. ESA plans to contribute to space station Freedom.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Evening Star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Name for Venus when it shines after sunset. When Venus appears shortly before sunrise, it is often called the Morning Star. Occasionally, other bright planets are also called evening stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Event Horizon)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Area surrounding a black hole that is defined by the Schwarzschild radius. Light emitted within this area cannot escape from the gravitational pull of the black hole. The escape velocity inside of the event horizon is greater than the speed of light.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Exobiology)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Search for extraterrestrial life, including studies of the composition of other planets. This field is also called xenobiology or astrobiology.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Expanding Universe)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Movement, which can be measured by the Doppler effect, of objects in the universe away from each other. Scientists have deduced from this movement that the universe is expanding. Distant objects are moving away more quickly than nearby objects.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Explorer Satellites)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Scientific series of 55 satellites launched from 1958 to 1975. They were started by the Advanced Research Projects Agency (ARPA) and taken over by NASA. The satellites\' achievements include the discovery of Earth\'s Van Allen radiation belts (by Explorer 1) and the first pictures of Earth from space (by Explorer 6).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Eyepiece)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(An arrangement of lenses used to look at images formed in telescopes. Most eyepieces are made of a field lens, which increases the area that can be seen through the telescope, in addition to the eye lens. Different amounts of area can be seen through different eyepieces. Some eyepieces correct chromatic and spherical aberration better than others, some give more room between the observer\'s eye and the lens (a distance known as eye relief), and some are better than others at avoiding the double images that can arise from reflections within the telescope and eyepiece.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Facula)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Bright hot spots that appear by the sun\'s edge, or limb, and are usually seen around sun spots. Since they usually occur around high magnetic field areas, magnetism may play a role in heating faculas.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Fireball)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Bright meteor whose magnitude is at least -4. Some fireballs are so bright that their magnitude reaches -25, rivaling the sun. Most fireballs get their brightness from their large masses. Sometimes, a meteorite will disintegrate as it falls through the atmosphere and, often with a sonic boom, produce a bolide (an exploding meteor).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(First Quarter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Time when the moon is one-fourth the way around its orbit. Many people think \"first quarter\" means that only one-fourth of the moon is lit, but this is not true. During first quarter, from our perspective, half of the moon is lit.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Flamsteed, John)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Flamsteed, John (1646-1719))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Flamsteed, John (1646-1719)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Historia Coelestis Britannica)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(First royal astronomer of England and the creator of \x01\x0e+0200Historia Coelestis Britannica\x01\x0e-0200, the first star catalog to be made with a telescope. His catalog, which was published in 1725, includes the positions of nearly 3,000 stars. His system of listing constellations\' stars in order of increasing ascension led to the Flamsteed numbers, which are still used today. Additionally, he determined that the gravity of Jupiter and Saturn affect each other\'s movements. From his observations of sunspots, he calculated that the sun\'s rotational period is about 25.25 days.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Flare)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Explosions that occur in active regions of the sun\'s chromosphere and usually last for a few minutes. When the charged particles released by flares reach the Earth (which takes a few days), they often cause auroras.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Flare Star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Flare Star (UV Ceti Variable))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Flare Star (UV Ceti Variable)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Star whose brightness suddenly increases and then goes back to its normal level. These flares are thought to be similar to those observed on the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Floccule)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Spicules seen head-on. They occur mainly in 20,000 mile (32,000 km) wide chains on the boundaries of supergranules, which are bubble-like convection cells in the chromosphere of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Flying Saucer)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(See UFO.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See UFO.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Focal Length)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Distance between a telescope\'s lens or mirror and the image it forms.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Focal Plane)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Place where a telescope\'s image is formed.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Focal Ratio)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Focal Ratio ()
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(f)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(\x01\x0e+0200f\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(ratio))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Focal Ratio (\x01\x0e-0100\x01\x0e+0100\x01\x0e+0200f\x01\x0e-0200\x01\x0e-0100 \x01\x0e+0100ratio)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Mirror or lens\' focal length divided by its aperture. The larger the focal ratio, the longer the tube, the narrower the field of view, and the dimmer the image.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Fomalhaut)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Brightest star in the constellation Piscis Austrinus and the 18th brightest in the sky. It is a white star with a magnitude of 1.2 that is 22.5 lightyear away and has a yellow sixth magnitude companion. Because Fomalhaut is one of the few bright stars in its region of the sky, it is often used in navigation.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Forbidden Lines)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Bright lines in spectra that are produced when electrons change orbits. Although seen in the gases around stars, forbidden lines cannot be produced on Earth because it takes too long for the electrons to leave their stable orbits, go down energy levels, and produce the lines. In order for forbidden lines to occur, gas must be thin. In the thicker gases found on Earth, electrons are bumped around and cannot jump orbits and produce the lines. Hence forbidden lines are only observed in space. An example are the forbidden lines of oxygen seen in many nebula.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Fornax)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Fornax (The Furnace))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Fornax (The Furnace)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Dim, tiny, southern constellation that lies next to Eridanus. It contains a dwarf elliptical galaxy that is about 800,000 lightyears away which belongs to our Local Group.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Fraunhofer, Joseph)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Fraunhofer, Joseph (1787-1826))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Fraunhofer, Joseph (1787-1826)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(German physicist who studied the sun\'s spectrum and noticed dark lines, which came to be known as Fraunhofer lines.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Fraunhofer Lines)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Dark absorption lines in some wavelengths of the sun\'s spectrum. They appear because the cooler gas toward the surface of the sun absorbs radiation of certain wavelengths before it can reach us. The Fraunhofer lines of stars\' spectra can be used to help determine what elements are in the stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Frequency)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Number of waves counted in a certain amount of time, measured in cycles per second, or Hertz. Frequency can also be determined by dividing the velocity of a wave by its wavelength.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Fusion)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Fusion (Nuclear Fusion))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Fusion (Nuclear Fusion)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Reaction, usually found in the center of stars, that produces heat. The nuclei of lighter elements join to make heavier elements, releasing energy. The most common type of fusion is when four hydrogen nuclei fuse to form one helium nucleus. The resulting helium nucleus is about 7% lighter than the combined mass of the four original hydrogen nuclei. This missing mass has changed to energy.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gagarin, Yuri Alekseyevich)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Gagarin, Yuri Alekseyevich (1934-1968))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Gagarin, Yuri Alekseyevich (1934-1968)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Soviet cosmonaut who, on April 12, 1961, became the first person in space. During his 108 minutes in space, Gagarin orbited the Earth once in Vostok 1. His distance from the Earth varied from 112 miles (190 km) to 203 miles (344 km). Gagarin reported having no problem being weightless. While on a training flight with another pilot, he died in a plane crash on March 27, 1968.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Galactic Cluster)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Group of galaxies that are gravitationally bound. Most galaxies occur in clusters, and it is more likely that a galaxy will be found near another galaxy than in any other part of the sky. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Galactic clusters may be compact, with a width of roughly 50,000 lightyears, and contain 10 to 50 galaxies of all different types. The Local Group, to which the Milky Way belongs, is one such cluster. Other compact clusters may contain mostly elliptical or SO (which are a cross between elliptical and spiral) galaxies. Some cluster members come close enough to one another for one to pull material from the other. This, which is presently happening between the Milky Way and several small neighboring galaxies (with the Milky Way eating its neighbors), is called \"galactic cannibalism.\")
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Other clusters are diffuse, with widths of 10 million to 50 million lightyears, and contain roughly 1,000 galaxies of various types. Like galaxies, clusters often group together. Groups of roughly 7 clusters form superclusters, whose widths can reach 200 million lightyears. As yet, it is uncertain whether superclusters formed and then broke up into clusters and galaxies, or whether galaxies formed first and then joined together to form clusters and superclusters.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Galactic Core)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Center of a galaxy. The stars here are generally old and have fused their way up to the heavier elements. Stars here are also packed more tightly together than they are towards the outer regions of galaxies. Many galaxies, including the Milky Way, have radio emissions streaming from their cores. This leads scientists to think that there may be black holes at the center of these galaxies, and that these radio emissions are given off as the black holes pull in matter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(System made of billions of gravitationally bound stars. Galaxies are generally found in clusters, most of which are further grouped into clusters of clusters, or superclusters.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The structural differences across different types of galaxies may have come from how they were formed: some appear to have formed quickly from rapidly swirling gas, while others appear to have formed gradually from slowly swirling gas. Astronomers have used the classification system proposed by Edwin Hubble to divide galaxies into four categories:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Elliptical:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Elliptical:\x01\x0e-0100 These galaxies formed slowly, and, from far away, they look like bright circles. There is no noticeable middle bulge, and they are made of old (population II) stars. They range in size from the size of globular clusters to 30,000 lightyears across.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Spiral:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Spiral:\x01\x0e-0100  These galaxies formed rapidly. They have a bright middle bulge of old (population II) stars, around which a disk of younger (population I) stars and interstellar material revolves. The middle bulge is usually 100,000 lightyears across and 2,000 lightyears thick, and the spiral arms are a structural formation through which stars pass. The Milky Way is a spiral galaxy and so are 70% of all other bright galaxies. Spiral galaxies are subdivided by how tightly their arms are wound, how smooth the arms are, and how large the nucleus, or middle bulge, is.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Barred spirals:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Barred spirals:\x01\x0e-0100  These are like spiral galaxies, but the spiral arms reach out, not directly from the nucleus, but from both ends of en elongated bar or band of stars and interstellar material that runs through it.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Irregular:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Irregular:\x01\x0e-0100  These galaxies have no noticeable symetrical shape or pattern. They are a bit like blobs because they have no noticeable central bulge.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Some galaxies, called radio galaxies, can be detected through radio astronomy. Seyfert galaxies have bright central bulges and violent gases swirling up to thousands of miles per second.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The most distant galaxy observed so far lies about 15,000 million lightyears away. This galaxy, which is just developing, is called 4C41.17 and has a huge gas cloud of bright hydrogen surrounding it. Some other galaxies are the Andromeda galaxy, the Whirlpool galaxy, the Pinwheel galaxy, and the Black-eye galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(NGC 4565 is a beautiful example of a spiral galaxy seen perfectly edge-on.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100NGC 4565 is a beautiful example of a spiral galaxy seen perfectly edge-on.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Beautiful spiral galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Beautiful spiral galaxy\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(From an imaginary planet, we see various types of galaxies: spiral, elliptical, barred and irregular.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100From an imaginary planet, we see various types of galaxies: spiral, elliptical, barred and irregular.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(M82 appears to be a spiral galaxy in the process of exploding. In fact, it is producing billions of new stars out of a new cloud with which it collided.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M82 appears to be a spiral galaxy in the process of exploding. In fact, it is producing billions of new stars out of a new cloud with which it collided.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(The \"Black-eye\" galaxy (M64) in Coma Berenices is caused by vast clouds of dust across its bright center.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The \"Black-eye\" galaxy (M64) in Coma Berenices is caused by vast clouds of dust across its bright center.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(It\'s rare that a dust lane girdles the nucleus of an elliptical galaxy, but that\'s only one mystery about Centaurus A. It also has two giant jets of gas extending across 2.5 million light years. They could engulf both Andromeda and our own galaxy if they were between us.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100It\'s rare that a dust lane girdles the nucleus of an elliptical galaxy, but that\'s only one mystery about Centaurus A. It also has two giant jets of gas extending across 2.5 million light years. They could engulf both Andromeda and our own galaxy if they were between us.\x01\x0e-0100)
