[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Galileo Galilei)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Galileo Galilei (1564-1642))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Galileo Galilei (1564-1642)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Italian astronomer, mathematician, and physicist. He discovered that a pendulum\'s length determines how long it will take to swing, regardless of the size of the swing. His other discoveries however were more controversial. Before he became a mathematics professor in Pisa, he observed that all objects fall at the same speed. This went against the current belief that heavier objects fall faster. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(When he heard about telescopes in 1609, he built one of his own and turned it toward the sky. He saw Venus\' phases, Jupiter\'s moons, and craters on the moon. This showed that bodies can orbit objects other than the Earth and that the moon, even though it is a celestial object, is not perfect as Aristotle had stated. All these provided support for Copernicus\' view of the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(The Starry Messenger)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems:  Ptolemaic and Copernican)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(In 1610, Galileo wrote \x01\x0e+0200The Starry Messenger\x01\x0e-0200 and, in 1632, he wrote \x01\x0e+0200Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems:  Ptolemaic and Copernican\x01\x0e-0200, both of which discussed his observations and opinion that the Copernican model was correct. Galileo\'s style, however, was sarcastic, and in his 1632 book he not only went against the Church doctrine, he also made it look idiotic. The \x01\x0e+0200Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems\x01\x0e-0200 was added to the Church\'s list of banned books, Galileo was called before the Inquisition, forced to take back his support of the Copernican view, and placed under house arrest. (Only recently, in October 1992, did the Catholic Church pardon Galileo.))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Discourses on Two New Sciences)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(In spite of this, he published \x01\x0e+0200Discourses on Two New Sciences\x01\x0e-0200 in 1638, which yet again went against the Church\'s doctrine. The message in this book sums up Galileo\'s policy in life: do not just accept things, do experiments and look for evidence.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Galileo Spacecraft)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Space probe launched in October of 1989 and sent to explore Jupiter. Galileo was launched from the payload bay of the Atlantis shuttle and was scheduled to investigate the structure and motions of four of Jupiter\'s satellites as well as send a probe into Jupiter\'s atmosphere. After launch, however, its main antenna did not open properly (unfurl), thus hindering the mission\'s efficiency. Galileo has taken pictures of two asteroids, 951 Gaspra and Ida, and discovered that Ida has a moon. It will reach Jupiter in December 1995, but its ability to fulfill its mission there is still under question because it can only transmit data at a rate of 10 bits per second.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Galle, Johann Gottfried)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Galle, Johann Gottfried (1812-1910))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Galle, Johann Gottfried (1812-1910)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(German astronomer who discovered Neptune on September 23, 1846 by following Leverrier\'s accurate calculations of its position.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gamma-Ray Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Study of space by examining radiation with wavelengths of less than one angstrom (one 10 billionth of a meter). The Earth\'s atmosphere blocks gamma rays so they can only be studied by rockets and satellites that are above the atmosphere. In 1967 the Vela satellites, the first gamma-ray satellites built, detected bursts of gamma-ray radiation. It is now known that there is usually one of these bursts per day and that they must come from an object with a size similar to that of the sun, but the bursts come from random directions and their origin is unknown. There appear to be many weak sources of gamma-rays scattered about because a general background of gamma-ray radiation has been found.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gamow, George)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Gamow, George (1904-1968))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Gamow, George (1904-1968)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Astrophysicist who, with Ralph Asher Alpher and Hans Bethe, predicted that there would be background radiation left over from the Big Bang. This microwave radiation has been found.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Ganymede)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(One of Jupiter\'s four Galilean moons and the largest moon in the solar system. It is 3,262 miles (5,262 km) wide, 665,120 miles (1,070,400 km) from Jupiter, and orbits it every 7 days, 3 hours, 42 minutes, and 30 sec. Its density is twice that of water, and it is made of ice and silicate rock. Ganymede is divided into light and dark colored bands. The light regions are believed to have formed when Ganymede\'s crust cracked shortly after its formation.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gegenschein)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Gegenschein (Counterglow))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Gegenschein (Counterglow)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Dim light that was discovered in 1854 and lies opposite the sun almost on the plane of the ecliptic and is best seen in the fall and spring. This light is produced by distant dust particles reflecting the sun\'s light.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gemini)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Gemini (The Twins))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Gemini (The Twins)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Northern zodiacal constellation, near Orion, that is best seen in the winter. Its two brightest stars are Pollux and Castor. M35, an open cluster, is also in Gemini.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gemini Project)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American series of 12 two-person space missions, designed to practice and improve space capabilities in preparation for the Apollo program\'s moon landing.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Geminid Meteors)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Major annual meteor shower that starts around December 7 and reaches its maximum of 55 meteors per hour around December 13-14.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Geocentric)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Earth-centered view of the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Geostationary Orbit)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Geostationary (Geosynchronous) Orbit)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Geostationary (Geosynchronous) Orbit\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Circular orbit that is 22,300 miles (35,900 km) above the equator and whose speed matches that of the Earth\'s rotation. Since the two orbits are exactly matched or synchronous, from the view of the satellite, the Earth appears to stand still. This type of orbit is often used for communications satellites.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Giant Star)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Star whose size is large for its mass and temperature. Because its size is so big, its luminosity is also large. The mass of giant stars is 10 to 30 times that of the sun, their volume is 1,000,000 to 10,000,000 times that of the sun, and their luminosity is 10 to 1,000 times that of the sun. Very big giants are supergiants, but there is no firm dividing line between giants and supergiants. Stars enter a short giant phase when they run out of hydrogen to fuse. Even though giants are rare (because the giant stage is brief in the life of a star) they are easy to spot because they are so bright. Many of the brighter appearing stars in our sky are giants.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gibbous)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Phase of a planet or moon that comes between half and full.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Glenn, John Herschel, Jr.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Glenn, John Herschel, Jr. (b. 1921))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Glenn, John Herschel, Jr. (b. 1921)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Friendship 7)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(First American to orbit the Earth, circling it three times in \x01\x0e+0200Friendship 7\x01\x0e-0200. His February 20, 1962 flight took 4 hours, 55 minutes, and 23 seconds, and his distance from the Earth varied from 99 miles (159 km) to 162 miles (261 km).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Globular Cluster)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Spherical group of thousands, and sometimes millions, of densely packed old stars. Most globular clusters are in the galactic halo, the bubble-like structure around the center of the Milky Way. The stars in globular clusters are some of the oldest known (some are at least 13 billion years old), and they contain few elements heavier than helium because they were formed before these elements were created. Some globular clusters, such as M13, 47 Tucanae, and Omega Centauri, are visible as tiny, faint smudges of light without a telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Globule)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Spherical or cigar-shaped clouds of gas and dust that are lit by nearby stars or bright nebulas. Their size ranges from tiny Bok globules, whose size is similar to that of our solar system and whose mass is similar to that of our sun, to huge globules that are a few lightyears across and have a mass equal to that of many suns.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Goddard, Robert Hutchings)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Goddard, Robert Hutchings (1882-1945))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Goddard, Robert Hutchings (1882-1945)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American who built and launched the first liquid-fueled rocket and, in 1935, became the first person to shoot a liquid-fueled rocket faster than the speed of sound. Robert Goddard, Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, and Hermann Oberth, who were all space flight enthusiasts, each devised the theory of rocket propulsion independently.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Goddard Space Flight Center)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(NASA research and design center built in Greenbelt, Maryland in 1959, specializing in satellite technology. It has developed Explorer satellites, Landsat, and other weather and communications satellites.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Granulation)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Pattern on sun\'s photosphere that arises from convection and is responsible for the photosphere\'s brightness. This is due to hot gases from within the sun rising and the cooler gases toward the surface of the sun falling. Granules are like conveyor belts, always churning up hot gas but only lasting a few minutes. They are between 200 and 1,000 miles (300 and 1,500 km) wide. Their centers, where the hot gases are rising, shine brightly. Their edges, where the cooler gases are falling, are darker.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gravity)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Attractive force between all bodies that increases with mass and decreases with the square of the distance. Gravitiy is one of several types of forces that follows the inverse square law. Although the weakest of all known forces, gravity is important in astronomy because the masses involved are so large. According to Newton\'s inverse square law, the gravitational force between two bodies is proportional to the product of their masses divided by the square of the distance between their centers. For example, if the moon became 3 times further from the Earth, the gravity between the Earth and moon would become 9 times less. This is because 3 squared is equal to 9. Einstein\'s general theory of relativity, however, treats gravity not as a force, but as a result of warps in space and time.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Gravity Slingshot)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Using the gravitational pull of a planet to accelerate a craft on to another destination. Imagine you are standing still and another person is running towards you. As they approach you grab their arm and swing them partially around. As you let go they go flying off in another direction. The other person actually goes away faster than they arrived. This conserves the fuel that the craft would use to rocket directly to its destination. Both Voyager probes, for example, used gravitational slingshot as they flew past the outer planets and propelled themselves out of the solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Great Bear)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(See Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Ursa Major.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Great Red Spot)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Persistent hurricane-like storm located 24\xc2\xb0 south of Jupiter\'s equator that was first observed by Giovanni Cassini in 1666. This elliptical cyclone, with a width of 35,200 miles (22,000 km), is large enough to swallow three Earths. Its color changes chaotically from orange to red to cream. It appears to be Jupiter\'s only continuous atmospheric feature.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Greenhouse Effect)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Warming of a planet due to the effects of its atmosphere in a process that is similar to what happens in a greenhouse. The glass in a greenhouse lets the short wave solar energy in. As the inside absorbs this energy and warms up it begins to radiate its own longer waves. These longer terrestrial waves are blocked from escaping by the glass. Because the atmosphere traps more radiation than it reflects, the planet\'s surface temperature rises. If the Earth did not have an atmosphere, its average temperature would be around -22 F (-30 C) instead of the comfortable 60.8 F (16 C). Venus on the other hand has a surface temperature 730 K because of the greenhouse effect.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Greenwich Mean Time (GMT))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Local time at the Royal Greenwich Observatory (Sussex, England) and called Universal Time (UT) by astronomers all over the world.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Grissom, Virgil Ivan \"Gus\")
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Grissom, Virgil Ivan \"Gus\" (1926-1967))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Grissom, Virgil Ivan \"Gus\" (1926-1967)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American astronaut who was the first person to make two space flights. On July 21, 1961, in the second launching in the Mercury series, he went into sub-orbital flight. On March 23, 1965, with John Young, he flew Gemini\'s first peopled spacecraft. He was scheduled for an Apollo flight, but he, Roger Chaffee, and Edward White died in a spacecraft fire during a practice countdown on January 27, 1967.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Grus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Grus (The Crane))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Grus (The Crane)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Southern constellation next to Phoenix whose brightest star, Alnair, is a 2.2 magnitude white star.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Guidance (or Guidance System))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Computer or machinery that steers a spacecraft by directing thrust in the rocket engine. This is similar to rudder controls in an airplane in that guidance controls the path of the craft. There are two types of guidance systems, manual and automatic. Manual guidance is controlled by a pilot, usually using a control stick like the pilot of an airplane. Automatic guidance is controlled by a computer with the path pre-programmed, similar to an automatic pilot on an airplane. A spacecraft must have one kind of guidance system, and most modern crafts have both.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(HI, HII Regions)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Interstellar clouds containing hydrogen, which is cool and un-ionized (neutral) in HI and hot and ionized (sometimes called emission nebula) in HII. HII regions often glow with a brilliant red color as seen in many deep space photographs.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hale, George Ellery)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hale, George Ellery (1868-1938))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hale, George Ellery (1868-1938)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American astronomer who founded the Mount Palomar, Mount Wilson, and Yerkes observatories and who played a role in the creation of the field of astrophysics. He was also responsible for the creation of some of the biggest telescopes in the world, such as a 200 inch (508 cm) reflector, which he built in order to study galaxies other than the Milky Way.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hale Observatories)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Mount Palomar and Mount Wilson, which were founded by Hale and named after him in 1970. Mount Palomar is operated by the California Institute of Technology, and Mount Wilson is operated by the Carnegie Institution of Washington, DC. The Mount Palomar Observatory, located just outside of San Diego at an elevation of 5,597 feet (1,706 m), opened its main telescope, the 200 inch (508 cm) Hale reflector, in 1948. Its 72 inch (183 cm) Schmidt telescope was used to take the Sky Survey photographic atlas. Mount Wilson, where Hale started observing in 1904, opened its 100 inch (254 cm) Hooker telescope in 1917. Its performance has been seriously inhibited by the lights of nearby Los Angeles.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hall, Asaph)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hall, Asaph (1829-1907))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hall, Asaph (1829-1907)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American astronomer who discovered Mars\' two moons, Deimos and Phobos, on August 11 and 17, 1877. Hall went on to become an expert in the satellites of our solar system. Later, in 1892, Hall demonstrated that 61 Cygni\'s two stars orbit each other.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Halley, Edmund)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Halley, Edmund (1656-1742))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Halley, Edmund (1656-1742)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(A)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Synopsis of the Astronomy of Comets)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Second English astronomer royal, who is best known for calculating the 76 year period of Halley\'s comet. In 1678 he published the first catalog of southern stars to be based on telescopic observations of star positions. In 1679, he suggested using observations of Venus crossing the face of the sun to determine the size of our solar system, and he made predictions of Venus\' movements, which were confirmed in 1761 and 1769. In 1705, he published \x01\x0e+0200A\x01\x0e-0200 \x01\x0e+0200Synopsis of the Astronomy of Comets\x01\x0e-0200, which includes the orbit of Halley\'s comet and 23 other comets. In 1718 he saw that Aldebaran, Arcturus, and Sirius had moved from their positions recorded by Ptolemy, showing their proper motions through space.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Halley\'s Comet)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Most prominent and first identified periodic (one that returns regularly to the sun) comet. Halley realized that comets that appeared in 1531, 1607, and 1682 were all actually the same comet that was orbiting with a 76 year period, and he predicted that it would return around 1758. It is now known that the comet had been seen several times before 1531, with the earliest known observation in 240 BC. In 1910, the comet came close enough to the Earth for it to travel through its tail. The comet was most recently observed in 1986, and it will come again around 2061 and 2137. During the 1986 passage, the spacecraft Vega 1, Vega 2, and Giotto all observed the comet, and its nucleus has been calculated to be 9 X 5 miles (15 X 8 km) in size, to have a temperature of about 330 K, and to eject dust, gas, and ice.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Halo)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Halo)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Halo\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(A large ring around the sun or moon (usually 22\xc2\xb0 in radius) that is caused by light refracting through ice crystals in high clouds.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Harvard College Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Harvard University\'s astronomical facility, founded in 1839. In 1973, it became part of the Harvard-Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics when it joined with the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Harvest Moon)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Full moon closest to the autumnal equinox, when the moon appears to rise at roughly the same time for several nights.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hawking, Stephen William)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hawking, Stephen William (b. 1942))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hawking, Stephen William (b. 1942)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(A Brief History of Time)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(British physicist who studies relativity and strange objects such as black holes. He proposed that there could be mini black holes, which would eventually \"disintegrate\" because they give off radiation. (Eventually all their energy would be radiated away.) Hawking has written many books, including \x01\x0e+0200A Brief History of Time\x01\x0e-0200, which explores the origins of our universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Heavy Elements)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(In astronomy, this refers to all elements except for helium and hydrogen. Astronomers sometimes refer to these heavy elements, which are formed during nuclear fusion and are spread throughout the universe by supernovas, as metals.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Heliacal Rising And Setting)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Heliacal Rising and Setting)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Heliacal Rising and Setting\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(First observable rising or setting of a star that occurs when it is closest in line (conjunction) with the sun. Ancient astronomers who used the rising and setting of the stars such as Sirius to keep track of the Earth\'s orbit about the sun often based their calendars on these events.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Heliocentric)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Sun-centered view of the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Helium)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Second-most plentiful and second lightest element in the universe. About 15% of interstellar helium has been created through fusion in stars and spread throughout the universe by supernovas. The rest of the helium is left over from the Big Bang.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Heracleides Ponticus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Heracleides Ponticus (c. 388- c 315 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Heracleides Ponticus (c. 388- c 315 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Greek astronomer who is the first person known to propose that since Mercury and Venus stay so close to the sun, they might orbit it and that the Earth might rotate on an axis. Although his ideas were ignored at the time, they influenced later astronomers, including Copernicus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hercules)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Northern constellation lying between Lyra and Corona Borealis that is easiest to see in the summer. Its brightest star, Ras Algethi, is a triple star. The brightest of these three stars is a red supergiant that, at 500 or 600 times the size of the sun, is one of the largest known stars. The other two stars in the system form a spectroscopic binary with a 52-year period. Hercules also contains M13, which is 22,500 lightyears away, contains about 300,000 stars, and is one of the brightest globular clusters.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hercules Globular Cluster (M13))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(The globular cluster lying 23,000 lightyears away. The Hercules Globular Cluster appears as a smudge of light to the naked eye, but, with a telescope, it becomes a densely packed swarm of stars. Lying in the constellation Hercules, it is the brightest globular cluster in the northern hemisphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hermes)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(One of the Apollo group\'s asteroids that has an eccentric orbit which brings it extremely close to the Earth. This 0.5 mile (0.8 km) wide asteroid once came about 485,000 miles (780,000 km) from the Earth (about twice the distance from the Earth to the moon).)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Herschel, Sir William (Frederick))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Herschel, Sir William (Frederick) (1738-1822))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Herschel, Sir William (Frederick) (1738-1822)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(British amateur astronomer who discovered Uranus. Because he wanted better telescopes than those currently available, he constructed his own by either grinding and polishing available mirrors or creating his own larger ones. In 1781, he became famous when, during his third time surveying the sky, he discovered Uranus, which was the first planet to be discovered since prehistoric times. In 1785, with the king\'s financial backing, he built a 48 inch (122 cm) telescope, the largest at that time. He also analyzed over 3,000 sections of the sky, discovered that the band that forms the Milky Way is made of stars, and came up with a theory of how stars evolve.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hertzsprung, Ejnar)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hertzsprung, Ejnar (1873-1967))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hertzsprung, Ejnar (1873-1967)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Danish astronomer who discovered that stars can be divided into several categories and demonstrated that main sequence stars\' color and luminosity are related. When he plotted stars\' brightness and luminosity on a graph, he found that most stars fall on the diagonal strip now called the main sequence.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hertzsprung-Russell Diagram)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Graph, developed independently by both Ejnar Hertzsprung and Henry Norris Russell in the early 1900\'s, which plots stars\' brightness against their temperature. Similar charts that use color instead of temperature are known as color-magnitude diagrams. If a star\'s spectrum is known, either chart can be used to find its absolute magnitude, which can then be used to find its distance. Also, a star\'s relative age (evolutionary stage) can be determined by looking at its position on these charts.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hevelius, Johannes)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hevelius, Johannes (1611-1687))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hevelius, Johannes (1611-1687)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(Selenographica)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Polish astronomer who created star atlases and \x01\x0e+0200Selenographica\x01\x0e-0200, which contains the first major map of the moon and shows the moon\'s rocking motion (or libration). )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hewish, Antony)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hewish, Antony (b. 1924))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hewish, Antony (b. 1924)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(British astrophysicist who, along with Jocelyn Bell, discovered pulsars and, in 1968, determined that they are rotating neutron stars. In 1974, he won the Nobel Prize in Physics, the first Nobel Prize to be awarded for observational astronomy.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hipparchus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hipparchus of Nicea (146-127 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hipparchus of Nicea (146-127 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Greek astronomer who discovered precession and who strongly influenced Ptolemy. He completed the first star catalog, using a system of dividing stars into 6 magnitudes of brightness. The brightest stars were ranked first magnitude while those stars just visible to the naked eye were ranked sixth. The lower the number the brighter the star. This scale is still used today. He also determined the length of a year to within 6 minutes.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Horologium)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Horologium (The Pendulum Clock))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Horologium (The Pendulum Clock)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation lying next to Eridanus.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hoyle, Sir Fred)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hoyle, Sir Fred (b. 1915))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hoyle, Sir Fred (b. 1915)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(British astronomer who supported the Steady-State Theory, which says that the universe has always and will always look the same and that, as it expands, matter is created to fill in the gaps. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(H-R Diagram)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(See Hertzsprung-Russell Diagram)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Hertzsprung-Russell Diagram\x01\x0e-0200 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hubble, Edwin Powell)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hubble, Edwin Powell (1889-1953))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hubble, Edwin Powell (1889-1953)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American astronomer who discovered that the universe is expanding and, when he discovered a Cepheid variable star in the Andromeda galaxy in 1923, proved that there are galaxies other than the Milky Way. This increased the size of the known universe many times. In 1925, during his studies of galaxies, Hubble proposed a system that is still used today to classify galaxies by their shape and structure.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hubble\'s Constant)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Galaxy\'s speed moving away from us divided by its distance from us. Assuming the universe is expanding, astronomers think that more distant galaxies recede faster than closer ones do.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hubble\'s Law)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Relation between the distance of a galaxy from the Milky Way and the speed with which it is traveling away from us. After studying the spectra of galaxies outside of the Local Group, Edwin Hubble found that, due to the expansion of the universe, galaxies that are further from the Milky Way recede faster.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hubble\'s Variable Nebular)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Gaseous nebula located in the constellation Monoceros. Discovered by Sir William Herschel in 1783. Noteworthy for displaying variation in nebular details which change nightly.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hubble Space Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hubble Space Telescope (HST))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hubble Space Telescope (HST)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Orbiting optical-ultraviolet telescope that is a collaborative effort of NASA and ESA. After its launch on April 25, 1990, it was discovered that there was a flaw in the main mirror that was causing spherical aberration and also that one of its solar cell arrays was warped. However, during an Endeavor Space Shuttle mission in December 1993, the old Wide Field/Planetary Camera (WFPC) was replaced with WFPC2, which corrects for the spherical aberration. The HST is now performing up to original expectations and gives the clearest telescopic images of any instrument that has been built so far. It has already confirmed the existence of a massive black hole in the core of the galaxy M87. The HST will undoubtedly solve many mysteries of the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Huygens, Christiaan)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Huygens, Christiaan (1629-1695))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Huygens, Christiaan (1629-1695)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Dutch scientist who, in 1656, proposed that Saturn\'s odd shape was due to rings encircling it, but he thought that these rings were made of solid material. He also discovered Titan. Later, he advanced the wave theory of light.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hyades)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(V-shaped star cluster, located in Taurus, that is the closest star cluster to our solar system. It lies about 140 lightyears away.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hydra)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hydra (The Water Snake))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hydra (The Water Snake)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Biggest constellation. It stretches from the southern hemisphere to north of the celestial equator and lies near Centaurus and Crater. Alphard, the brightest star of this dim constellation, is an orange giant with a magnitude of 2.16.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hydrogen)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hydrogen)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hydrogen\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Most plentiful and lightest element in the universe. About one atom of hydrogen, which is composed of a proton holding a single electron, exists in every 5 cubic centimeters of interstellar space. It can exist in three forms: ions (HII), where each atom has been stripped of its electron, molecules (H2), where two hydrogen atoms have joined or bonded together, and neutral atoms (HI) holding all of its electrons. Hydrogen has two isotopes named deuterium and tritium.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hydrus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Hydrus (The Lesser Water Snake))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Hydrus (The Lesser Water Snake)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Southern constellation that lies between Phoenix and the celestial south pole.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hyperbola)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Open, symmetrical curve formed by slicing a cone. An object with a hyperbolic orbit about the sun will pass by it just once and never return.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Hyperion)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Eighth distant satellite of Saturn. At 8,840 miles (1,482,000 km) from Saturn\'s center, this 225 mile (360 km) wide moon orbits it once every 21 days, 7 hours, 39 minutes, and 6 seconds. Hyperion has an irregular shape and a shaky orbit. Its reddish surface is made mostly of ice.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Iapetus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Saturn\'s second most distant moon. At 2,212,800 miles (3,561,000 km) from Saturn\'s center, this 889 mile (1, 448 km) wide body orbits it every 79 days, 22 hours, and 5 minutes. During its orbit, the same side of Iapetus always faces Saturn. One half of this moon, whose density is three times that of water, is bright and has an albedo of 0.5 while the other half is black and has an albedo of 0.05. This is the largest variance of albedo in our solar system. It is believed that the material on the dark half lies on top of Iapetus\' bright surface and came from Iapetus itself.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(IAU)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(International Astronomical Union.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Icarus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Asteroid of the Apollo group that is 0.5 miles (1.8 km) wide and whose highly eccentric 1.1 year orbit takes it from 0.19 to 2.0 a.u. from the sun. Of all known objects in the solar system, only comets come closer to the sun than Icarus does. The plane of its orbit is tilted 23\xc2\xb0 off the ecliptic, and it comes by the Earth every 19 years.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Ida)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(First asteroid observed (by the Galileo probe in 1993) to have a moon orbiting it.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Image Intensifier)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Image intensifier)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Image intensifier\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Device that, by electronically increasing a faint image\'s brightness, decreases photographic exposure times.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Inclination)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Angle between a reference plane and the plane of an object\'s orbit. For objects orbiting the Earth, the reference plane is usually the Earth\'s equator. For other objects, it is usually the plane of the Earth\'s orbit, the ecliptic. The moon\'s orbit for example has an inclination of 5 degrees to the ecliptic.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Indus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Indus (The Indian))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Indus (The Indian)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Constellation that lies near the celestial south pole and whose star Epsilon is 11.1 lightyears from the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Inferior Conjunction)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(An orbital line-up occurring when Earth, sun, and either Mercury or Venus are aligned, with Mercury or Venus between the Earth and the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Inferior Planet)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Planet that is closer than the Earth to the sun. (Mercury and Venus.))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Infrared Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Study of space using radiation of wavelengths from 7,800 angstroms (red light) to 1 millimeter (microwave). The infrared part of the spectrum is composed of mostly the wavelengths that we feel as heat. Objects which radiate mainly in infrared wavelengths are not as hot as the sun, which radiates mainly in visible wavelengths. Since the atmosphere\'s water vapor absorbs much infrared radiation, infrared observations are best made by instruments that are carried above the Earth\'s atmosphere. In 1983, the Netherlands, the United Kingdom, and the United States launched the Infrared Astronomical Satellite (IRAS), which has discovered, among other things, clouds of debris around the stars Vega and Fomalhaut. IRAS is so sensitive it can detect the heat from a light bulb at the distance of Pluto.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Intelsat)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(International Telecommunications Satellite Organization, which was formed by eighteen countries in 1964.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Interferometry)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Linking of several smaller telescopes (e.g., radio and optical) to resolve as finely as one large receiving telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(International Astronomical Union)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(International Astronomical Union (IAU))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100International Astronomical Union (IAU)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Organization started in 1919 that now includes thousands of astronomers from around the world. A committee at the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory in Cambridge, Massachusetts keeps observatories informed of events such as sightings of novas and discoveries of comets.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Interstellar Absorption)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Absorption of light by tiny dust particles. These dust particles are roughly 0. 00001 cm in size and are and scattered throughout space, but mostly concentrated in the Milky Way\'s spiral arms. Since these dust particles absorb less red light than blue light, the amount of red light present in an object seen from the Earth can be used to calculate its distance. Also the more distant a light source is, the redder its light appears. This is known as interstellar reddening.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Io)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Jupiter\'s second closest moon. With a size of 2,255.5 miles (3,632 km), Io lies 261,813.6 miles (421,600 km) from Jupiter and orbits it every 42 hours, 28 minutes, and 36 seconds. This red-colored splotchy moon has a density that is 3.5 times that of water. Io, one of the few moons in our solar system that has an atmosphere, is the only body in our solar system other than Earth where volcanic explosions have been witnessed. Since Io has an ionosphere, it is subjected to Jupiter\'s magnetic field. Because Jupiter\'s rotation is faster than Io\'s and because Jupiter\'s magnetic field rotates with Jupiter, Io\'s gases have been pulled by Jupiter\'s magnetic field so that they stream in front of Io and fill its path, generating radio signals that are detectable from Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Io Facts:)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Io Facts:\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Average Temperature is about 140 K)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Maximum Pressure is 9.8 X 10^(-7) Earth Atmospheres)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Io has trace amounts of oxygen and is not known to have nitrogen or any significant amounts of either carbon dioxide or water.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Ion)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Atom which has either gained or lost an electron and, consequently, has either a negative or a positive charge. The soup of ions and their lost electrons, which exists in stars and emission nebulas, is called plasma.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Ionosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Part of the Earth\'s atmosphere that contains atoms that have been ionized by the sun\'s radiation. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Jansky, Karl Guthe)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Jansky, Karl Guthe (1905-1950))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Jansky, Karl Guthe (1905-1950)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(American communications engineer whose discovery that radio waves were coming from sources other than Earth led to the development of radio astronomy.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Janus)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(136 mile (220 km) wide moon of Saturn that orbits it every 16 hours, 40 minutes, and 19 seconds at a distance of 94,064 miles (151,472 km). Thanks to the gravitational influence of Janus (along with Epimetheus, Prometheus, and Pandora), Saturn\'s A ring resembles a braided string.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(JD)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_emphasis(See Julian Date)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Julian Date\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Jet Propulsion Laboratory)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Part of NASA, located in Pasadena, California, that is the site of NASA\'s Space Flight Operations Facility, which is the mission control center for robotic space and moon probes. JPL is famous for its processed images from the Voyager and Magellan missions.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Jodrell Bank Radio Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Home to one of the world\'s largest fully steerable radio telescopes, whose reflector is 250 feet (76 m) wide.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Johnson Space Center)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Division of NASA, located near Houston, Texas, that is the site of Mission Control, which works with human space missions after they take off.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Julian Date)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Dating system, started by Scaliger in 1582, used to record world historical events and to predict eclipses. Each day begins at noon, and there are no months or years; days are numbered consecutively. )
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Juno)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(140 mile (226 km) wide asteroid that orbits the sun once every 4.4 years. Discovered in 1807 by Karl Ludwig Harding, Juno was the third asteroid ever discovered.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Jupiter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Fifth planet from the sun and the largest in the solar system. It is often said that, if Jupiter had just a little more mass, it would be a star. Actually, Jupiter would need to have about 100 times more mass to start fusion and become a star. So, saying Jupiter just missed being a star is about like saying Earth just missed being Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(With a radius of 41,504 miles (69,173 km), Jupiter is more massive than all the other planets in our solar system combined. Because of its large mass, Jupiter is a huge gravitational attractor. To escape Jupiter\'s gravity, you would need to travel 36.0 miles (60.0 km) per second. Jupiter\'s gravity is responsible for the Kirkwood gaps in the asteroid belt, major perturbations in the orbit of comets, and is partially responsible for its satellite Io\'s volcanic activity. Jupiter\'s magnetic field, which is the largest in the solar system, provides a link between Jupiter and Io because Io\'s ionosphere is pulled by Jupiter\'s magnetic field as Jupiter rotates. This magnetosphere is responsible for the radio waves given off by Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Since Jupiter is primarily a gaseous body, it rotates differently at different latitiudes: its poles rotate in 9 hours, 55 minutes, 40 seconds, while its equator rotates at 9 hours, 50 minutes, 30 seconds. It is only at great depths that Jupiter is thought to have a solid core of metallic hydrogen.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Jupiter has radiation belts that are similar to Earth\'s Van Allen Belts, but about 10,000 times stronger. Particles from the solar wind are trapped in the planet\'s magnetic field and create a deadly obstacle to anyone who wants to visit the planet.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Jupiter\'s most famous feature is its Great Red Spot, a 16,120 mile (26,000 km) wide storm that has endured for at least 100 years. In addition to the Great Red Spot, Jupiter\'s atmosphere contains white clouds, which are made of frozen ammonia and have temperatures of 140 K. Jupiter\'s bands of color come from the presence of ammonia and sulfur in its atmosphere, but the most plentiful gases in Jupiter\'s atmosphere are hydrogen and helium.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Moons and Rings)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons and Rings\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(In addition to its many (at least 16) moons, Jupiter sports a thin ring made of tiny particles that have come from either Io\'s volcanic activity, collisions of micrometeorites trapped in Jupiter\'s gravity, or cometary debris.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Interior)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Interior\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Jupiter\'s interior is believed to have a temperature of 25,000 K and a pressure of 50,000,000 to 100,000,000 times that on the surface of Earth. Jupiter gives off more energy than it receives from the sun. Currently, this energy is thought to be left over from when Jupiter formed and produced heat as its heavier elements sank to the middle.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Moons of Jupiter)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons of Jupiter\x01\x0e-0100)
