[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Jupiter\'s equatorial bulge is perceptible at this scale - the equatorial diameter is 6.8% larger than the polar diameter, and it is 11.25 times wider than Earth\'s.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Jupiter\'s equatorial bulge is perceptible at this scale - the equatorial diameter is 6.8% larger than the polar diameter, and it is 11.25 times wider than Earth\'s.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Jupiter has lots of atmosphere! The upper deck of clouds is ammonia crystals. The lower deck is ammonium hydrogen sulfide tinted by molecules of sulfur. It is often stormy with thunder, lightening, and high winds.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Jupiter has lots of atmosphere! The upper deck of clouds is ammonia crystals. The lower deck is ammonium hydrogen sulfide tinted by molecules of sulfur. It is often stormy with thunder, lightening, and high winds.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(K)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Symbol for degrees Kelvin, the unit for temperature measured from absolute zero (-273.15\xc2\xb0 C -459.67\xc2\xb0 F). A Kelvin degree is equal to a centigrade degree, and water freezes on the Kelvin scale at 273.15\xc2\xb0, or 0\xc2\xb0 C.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Keck Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(Two identical 400 inch (10 meter) reflecting telescopes on Mauna Kea in Hawaii. These telescopes employ a unique design: they are composed of 36 interlocking hexagonal segments instead of just one large mirror, and, as of 1995, they are the largest telescopes in the world.)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_header(Kennedy Space Center)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_double_emphasis(Kennedy Space Center (KSC))
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Kennedy Space Center (KSC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:35:59] render_paragraph(NASA\'s 84, 000 acre institution located in Cape Canaveral, Florida. KSC is NASA\'s primary civilian launch facility.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Kepler, Johann)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Kepler, Johann (1571-1630))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Kepler, Johann (1571-1630)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(German astronomer and mathematician who discovered the true shape of planetary orbits. In 1601 Kepler succeeded Tycho Brahe as the imperial mathematician at Benatek, an observatory outside Prague. Using Brahe\'s accurate sightings, Kepler attempted to explain the orbital motions of the planets. First, however, he focused his attention on Mars. Because Kepler believed there was a divine order to the universe that could be expressed in mathematical terms, he tried long and hard to describe the world mathematically. However, he was unable to get Mars\' orbit to match any mathematical form because he kept trying to explain the orbit by having Mars travel in a circle at a constant speed. After many tries he discovered that an ellipse, with the sun at one focus, accurately modeled the orbit of Mars. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(Astronomia Nova)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Although using an ellipse was opposed to the astronomy handed down by Ptolemy (because Ptolomy believed that circles were a divine shape and that, since the planets were divine, they would travel in circles), using one allowed Kepler to accurately predict planetary motion. In 1609 he published his findings in \x01\x0e+0200Astronomia Nova\x01\x0e-0200 (New Astronomy). After discovering that the planetary orbits were ellipses, he discovered that a planet\'s orbital period depends on its distance from the sun and that a planet\'s speed is not constant as it orbits the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Later in his life Kepler worked with optical theory and designed a telescope that was widely adopted.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Kepler\'s Laws)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Three laws governing planetary motion observed by Kepler.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_list_start(, 1)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Every planet orbits the sun in an ellipse with the sun at one focus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(Every planet orbits the sun in an ellipse with the sun at one focus.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(As every planet orbits the sun it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(As every planet orbits the sun it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(p)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(a)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There is a fixed ratio, p^(2)=a^(3) (where \x01\x0e+0200p\x01\x0e-0200 is the orbital period and \x01\x0e+0200a\x01\x0e-0200 is the length of the semimajor axis), that determines a planet\'s orbital period. Planets farther out take longer to complete an orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(There is a fixed ratio, p^(2)=a^(3) (where \x01\x0e+0200p\x01\x0e-0200 is the orbital period and \x01\x0e+0200a\x01\x0e-0200 is the length of the semimajor axis), that determines a planet\'s orbital period. Planets farther out take longer to complete an orbit.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. Every planet orbits the sun in an ellipse with the sun at one focus.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] \x01\x0e*032. 2. As every planet orbits the sun it sweeps out equal areas in equal times.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] \x01\x0e*033. 3. There is a fixed ratio, p^(2)=a^(3) (where \x01\x0e+0200p\x01\x0e-0200 is the orbital period and \x01\x0e+0200a\x01\x0e-0200 is the length of the semimajor axis), that determines a planet\'s orbital period. Planets farther out take longer to complete an orbit.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Kirkwood Gapss)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Kirkwood Gaps)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Kirkwood Gaps\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Gaps in the asteroid belt where there are few or no asteroids. Daniel Kirkwood explained these gaps by observing that they lie at exact orbital ratios with Jupiter, which would cause any object orbiting in this zone to be pulled out of its orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Kitt Peak National Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Observatory near Tucson, Arizona that has the largest collection of research telescopes in the world. The largest telescope of the collection is the 158 inch (4 meter) Mayall Telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Kuiper, Gerard Peter)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Kuiper, Gerard Peter (1905-1973))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Kuiper, Gerard Peter (1905-1973)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pioneer in many fields of solar and stellar astronomy. He was a great observer who discovered many binary stars, white dwarfs, Uranus\' satellite Miranda and Neptune\'s satellite Nereid. He was also a notable theoretician who defined the relationship between a star\'s mass and luminosity (mass-luminosity relationship) for main sequence stars and provided a basis for the stellar temperature scale.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Labes)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Term meaning \"landslide\" that is used in naming planetary features.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lacaille, Nicolas Louis De)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lacaille, Nicolas Louis de (1713-1762))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lacaille, Nicolas Louis de (1713-1762)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(French astronomer and map-maker who made the first accurate maps of the southern skies earning the title \"father of southern astronomy.\" He helped confirm Newton\'s prediction that the Earth bulges at the equator, and, between 1751 and 1753, he mapped nearly 10,000 stars from the Cape of Good Hope, introducing several new constellations.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lacerta)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lacerta (The Lizard))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lacerta (The Lizard)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Small, dim, northern constellation between Andromeda and Cygnus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lagrange, Joseph Louis)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lagrange, Joseph Louis (1736-1813))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lagrange, Joseph Louis (1736-1813)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(French astronomer and mathematician who determined, after making calculations of the stability of the solar system, that there would be no significant changes for a long time. He also invented the calculus of variations and advocated a decimal base for the metric system.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lagrangian Points)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Five points in which a small body can remain balanced in the company of two massive bodies that are in common orbit around each other. Only two of these points are stable.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Landsat)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Six NASA satellites carrying various types of cameras and infrared sensors used for surveying the Earth\'s resources and equipped to monitor oceanic and atmospheric conditions and to detect variations in pollution levels.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Langley Research Center)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(NASA facility in northern Virginia for design of aeronautical and astronomical technology.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Larissa (1989 N2))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Irregularly shaped, soot-black satellite of Neptune which was discovered by Voyager 2 in 1989.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Laser)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Acronym for Light Amplified by Standard Emission of Radiation. Lasers work by using the fact that atoms can absorb only a certain amount of energy before their electrons move to a higher energy level. When a photon of just the right energy level shines on an atom in an excited state, it can stimulate the atom to give off an identical photon. This second photon moves in the same direction, and with equal energy, as the first. Energy pumped into a laser-making devise puts atoms at a higher energy state. Then mirrors are used to reflect the photons back and forth, and then the light becomes so intense that it escapes through a partially reflecting mirror as an intense beam.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Last Quarter)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Moon\'s phase when it is waning and looks like a lighted semicircle; one of two half moons (the other is the first quarter).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Latitude)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Coordinate system for determining angular distance north or south of the equator. All lines of latitude are parallel.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Leavitt, Henrietta Swan)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Leavitt, Henrietta Swan (1868-1921))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Leavitt, Henrietta Swan (1868-1921)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(American astronomer who, using variable stars in the Magellanic Clouds, determined that the period of pulsation for stars known as Cepheid variables is related to their luminosity (period -luminosity ratio). )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lemaitre, Georges Edouard)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lemaitre, Georges Edouard (1894-1966))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lemaitre, Georges Edouard (1894-1966)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Belgian astronomer who proposed the Big Bang theory. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lens)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Transparent material with at least one curved surface that is used to alter the direction of light rays. Lenses are divided into two types: Converging and diverging. Converging lenses can produce a real image, while diverging lenses cannot.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Leo)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Leo (The Lion))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Leo (The Lion)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Northern zodiacal constellation that is best seen during the spring and located between Cancer and Virgo.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Leo Minor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Small, dim, northern constellation located between Leo and Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Leonid Meteors)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Annual meteor shower appearing for about two days around November 17. This shower is associated with Comet Temple-Tuttle 1866-1. The greatest concentrations of meteors occur every 33 years when the comet passes closest to the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Leonov, Alexei Arkhipovich)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Leonov, Alexei Arkhipovich (b. 1934))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Leonov, Alexei Arkhipovich (b. 1934)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Cosmonaut who was the first person to walk in space, outside of his Voskhod 2 capsule for 10 minutes on March 18, 1965. Leonov was also the Soviet commander of the Apollo-Soyuz Test Project. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lepus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lepus (The Hare))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lepus (The Hare)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Southern constellation lying just south of Orion that is best seen during the winter.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Leverrier, Urbain Jean Joseph)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Leverrier, Urbain Jean Joseph (1811-1877))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Leverrier, Urbain Jean Joseph (1811-1877)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(French mathematician and astronomer who, after carefully studying the orbit of Uranus, predicted that there was another large planet tugging at it. Leverrier, like Lagrange, studied the stability of planetary orbits. He also updated Langrange\'s data, particularly correcting his mass for Venus. He studied the motions of comets, which earned him a reputation for being a gifted analyst who figured out the discrepancies in the predicted orbits of celestial bodies. His crowning glory was when he independently, but at the same time as Adams, predicted the position of the planet Neptune.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Libra)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Libra (The Scales))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Libra (The Scales)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Northern zodiacal constellation lying between Virgo and Scorpus that is best seen during the spring.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Libration)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Rocking motion of the moon due to the fact that its rotation period and orbital period are equal and the shape of its orbit is not perfectly round. A libration also results from the fact that the moon\'s orbit is inclined 5 degrees to the Earth-sun orbit plane. Even though the same side of the moon always faces the Earth, both of these librations allow us to view 59% of its surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lick Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(University of California observatory that is located on Mount Hamilton and whose main telescope is a 120 inch (305 cm) reflector.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Life Support)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Life Support (Life Support System))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Life Support (Life Support System)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Machinery that simulates Earth-like conditions inside a spacecraft and protects the crew from the hazards of space. In the cabin of the space shuttle, for example, the life support systems provide astronauts with air to breathe and water to drink. Heating and cooling systems shield them from the extreme temperatures of space. All spacecraft that carry a crew must have some kind of life support system.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Light)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Portion of the electromagnetic spectrum to which the human eye is sensitive. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Electromagnetic Radiation.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Electromagnetic Radiation.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Light Pollution)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Light from cities and other sources that brightens the sky and interferes with astronomical observing. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Light, Velocity of)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Light, Velocity of ()
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(c)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(\x01\x0e+0200c\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis())
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Light, Velocity of (\x01\x0e-0100\x01\x0e+0100\x01\x0e+0200c\x01\x0e-0200\x01\x0e-0100\x01\x0e+0100)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Speed light travels in a vacuum. Although light travels 186,282.397 miles (299,792.458 km) per second in a vacuum, it travels slower through denser mediums, such as water or glass.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lightyear)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Distance light travels through a vacuum in a year. This is equal to 5.8786 trillion miles (9.4607 trillion km). It must be remembered that this is a measure of distance, not time.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Limb)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Edge of celestial objects. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Local Group)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Group of about 30 galaxies, including the Milky Way, that are held together by their mutual gravitation. The largest galaxy in the local group is the Andromeda galaxy, whose mass is about twice that of the Milky Way.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lockyer, Sir Joseph Norman)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lockyer, Sir Joseph Norman (1836-1920))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lockyer, Sir Joseph Norman (1836-1920)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(English astronomer and pioneer of astrophysics who discovered helium in the spectrum of the sun. He also discovered and named the sun\'s chromosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Longitude)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Coordinate system for determining position east or west of the prime meridian. Lines of longitude are not parallel since they all originate from, and meet at, the poles. Each 15\xc2\xb0 of longitude rotates under the sun in one hour.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lovell, Sir Alfred Charles Bernard)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lovell, Sir Alfred Charles Bernard (b. 1913))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lovell, Sir Alfred Charles Bernard (b. 1913)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio astronomer and founder of the Jodrell Bank Radio Observatory. He also established that meteors originated in the solar system after he bounced radio waves off them and discovered that their orbits were elliptical.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lovell, James Arthur)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lovell, James Arthur (b. 1928))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lovell, James Arthur (b. 1928)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Commander of the ill-fated Apollo 13, whose moon landing was canceled after an explosion ruptured an oxygen tank in the service module. He was also a crew member of Apollo 8, the first spacecraft to carry humans into lunar orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lowell, Percival)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lowell, Percival (1855-1916))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lowell, Percival (1855-1916)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(American businessman and amateur astronomer who, although best known for his mistaken maps of canals on Mars, pushed for a search for Planet X which was thought to be perturbing the orbit of Neptune.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lowell Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Astronomical observatory in Flagstaff, Arizona, that was founded by Percival Lowell.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Luminosity)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Measure of total output of radiation produced by a star or any glowing body in a certain amount of time. Luminosity of stars depends on their surface area, temperature, and size. The luminosity of stars is often express as a number on the absolute magnitude scale. The lower the number the more luminous the star. Luminosity is in no way related to a star\'s distance.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lunar Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Eclipse\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lunar Module)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lunar Module (LM))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lunar Module (LM)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Two stage descent craft of the Apollo Program. The Lunar Module had a height of 22 feet 11 inches (7m) split between the descent stage (10\' 7\"; 3.2 m) and the ascent stage (12\' 4\"; 3. 8 m). The ascent stage was a tight fit, containing room for little more than astronauts with space suits and lunar rocks. After blasting off from the moon, the ascent stage was able to make the maneuvers necessary for docking with the command module by 16 small thrusters.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lunar Orbit)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The path a spacecraft or satellite follows when circling the moon. During the Apollo missions, for example, the command and service modules remained in lunar orbit while the lander descended to the surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lunar Roving Vehicle)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lunar Roving Vehicle (LRV))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lunar Roving Vehicle (LRV)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Moon car used by Apollo astronauts. This 480 lb. (218 kg) rover was able to surmount foot high obstacles, two-foot crevices, and slopes of 20 degrees. It carried an antenna to keep astronauts in direct contact with the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lunation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Full cycle of lunar phases.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lupus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lupus (The Wolf))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lupus (The Wolf)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Southern constellation between Scorpius and Centarus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lynx)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lynx (The Lynx))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lynx (The Lynx)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Dim northern constellation near Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Lyra)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Lyra (The Lyre))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Lyra (The Lyre)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Small but prominent northern constellation best seen in summer. Lyra has many interesting astronomical sights, such as the \"double\" star Epsilon Lyrae, the Ring nebula (M57), and Vega, the sixth brightest star in the sky.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(M13)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Hercules Globular Cluster)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Hercules Globular Cluster\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(M32)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Andromeda Galaxy Illustration)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Andromeda Galaxy Illustration\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Magellanic Clouds)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Two small irregular galaxies orbiting the Milky Way at a distance of 160,000 lightyears. They were named after Ferdinand Magellan, who was the first prominent European to describe them. They are visible to the naked eye, but only if you are south of 20 north. There appears to be a stream of hydrogen gas connecting these satellite galaxies to the Milky Way. Both Magellanic clouds are much smaller than the Milky Way: the Large Magellanic cloud has one-thirtieth the mass of the Milky Way, and the Small Magellanic Cloud has one-two hundredth. The LMC also has lots of interstellar dust, but the SMC has very little.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Magnetosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Starts at an altitude of 300 miles (500 km) and is the outermost layer of the Earth\'s atmosphere. In this layer, ionized particles are trapped by the magnetic field and interact with the solar wind. Charged particles from the solar wind blow the magnetosphere into a teardrop or comet shape.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Magnitude)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Measure of an object\'s brightness that was instituted by Hipparchus. His classification system had six levels, with one being the brightest stars and six being the stars that are just visible to the unaided eye. Norman Pogson realized in 1856, after photometers had finally been used to measure stars\' brightness, that first magnitude stars were about 100 times brighter than sixth magnitude stars. Each magnitude is about 2.5 times brighter than the previous one. He then proposed a new classification system with the difference between first and sixth magnitude being exactly 100. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(With the advance of newer and better telescopes, astronomers have had to give stars higher and higher magnitudes. For example, the unaided eye can see to the sixth magnitude, but the Hubble Space Telescope can see to the 28th magnitude.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Absolute Magnitude and Apparent Magnitude.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Absolute Magnitude and Apparent Magnitude.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Main Sequence)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Diagonal line on the Hertzsprurg-Russell diagram that appears when the temperatures and luminosities of many stars are plotted on a graph. The majority of stars that presently exist are stable stars, steadily fusing hydrogen into helium, and these stable stars compose a line that extends from hot, blue stars in the upper left to cool, red stars on the lower right.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Mariner Spacecraft)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Series of American planetary spacecrafts that were the first to visit another planet, with Mariner 2 successfully flying past Venus in 1962. Mariners 2 and 5 made measurements of Venus\' atmospheric density and temperature. Mariners 4, 6, 7, and 9 photographed the Red planet, Mars. Mariner 9 was the first artificial space probe to orbit another planet. And, to this day, it is from Mariner 10 that we have our best pictures of Mercury.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Mare)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Mare (Plural Maria))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Mare (Plural Maria)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large dark plains scattered over the near side of the moon. They are made of basalt (a dark lava type rock) that oozed out of the moon 4 billion years ago, covering many older craters like asphalt covers potholes in an old highway. These plains were named mare (\"sea\" in Latin) because early astronomers thought they were filled with water.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Mars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Fourth planet from the sun and sometimes called the Red Planet due to its rusty color. Although Mars resembles Earth in many ways, such as a near 24-hour rotational period, a 23.98\xc2\xb0 orbital tilt of its axis, atmospheric clouds, dried river beds, volcanoes, and polar ices caps, it is very much unlike Earth in other ways. Mars shows many signs of impact craters, the climate is very cold and dry, and occasionally planet-wide dust storms circle the planet. Mars does not have a liquid core or any plate tectonics.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Mars orbits the sun at an average distance of 141,636,000 miles (227,941,000 km) and takes 687 days to complete one orbit. Mars has a radius of 2,030 miles (3,384 km), a density 3.9 times that of water, and is inclined 1.85\xc2\xb0 to the plane of the ecliptic. In order to escape from Mars\' gravity, you must travel 3.0 miles (5.0 km) per second.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Early in Mars\' history it had an atmosphere similar to Earth\'s, but now it possesses one that is only 1/250th as dense as Earth\'s (practically a vacuum). Mars\' atmosphere is composed mainly of carbon dioxide (95%) with traces of argon, nitrogen, water, oxygen and carbon monoxide. The trace amounts of water that exist on Mars cannot exist in a liquid state because of the cold temperature, but there is some frozen water on the surface at the poles, and some scientists think quite a bit of the water hides under the surface as permafrost. In the summer the temperature ranges from -100\xc2\xb0 C just after dawn to around 0\xc2\xb0 C at noon, in the winter the temperature drops to around -150\xc2\xb0 C at night.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Weather)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Weather\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There are occasional planet-wide dust storms, which have high winds and a thin layer of frost forms. Every spring the carbon dioxide that is frozen in the polar ice caps dissolves and enters the atmosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Topography)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Topography\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There is a line encircling the planet separating the older, higher southern hemisphere from the younger, low-lying northern hemisphere. The southern hemisphere still shows signs of the meteor bombardment it took when Mars was first formed. The northern hemisphere however, shows few old meteor craters and is only thinly populated with new ones. There are structures on Mars that appear to have been formed by flowing water, suggesting that at one time Mars had a different climate than it does now. There are polar ice caps which are composed of a core of water ice with a layering or carbon dioxide.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Mars also contains Olympus Mons, the highest volcano in the solar system, and Valles Marineris, a canyon system that is over 3,100 miles (5,000 km) long.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Moons of Mars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons of Mars\x01\x0e-0100)
