[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Messier, Charles)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Messier, Charles (1730- 1817))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Messier, Charles (1730- 1817)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(French astronomer and avid comet hunter. After being fooled by the comet-like appearance of the Crab Nebula, he began to catalogue other blurry objects which were not comets, eventually producing a book of 45 such objects in 1771. Later, he expanded his book to include 103 objects, such as Andromeda (M31) and the beautiful Whirlpool Galaxy (M51). After his death his list of objects was again increased to 110, based on notes Messier had written about objects he had viewed. The objects on the now famous list include galaxies, open clusters, globular clusters, and different types of nebula. Many of these objects can be seen with the naked eye, and all can be viewed in most any backyard telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Meteor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(An extraterrestrial particle, usually the size of an apple seed, that slams into the Earth\'s atmosphere and often produces a bright tail. Meteors tend to follow in the wake of a comet because comets lose mass when they approach the sun. Six meteors can be seen per hour on any clear night.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(In 1966, a meteor storm over the  southwest United States displayed 100 meteors each second, all radiating from the same point in the constellation Leo.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100In 1966, a meteor storm over the  southwest United States displayed 100 meteors each second, all radiating from the same point in the constellation Leo.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Meteor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(An extraterrestrial particle, usually the size of an apple seed, that slams into the Earth\'s atmosphere and often produces a bright tail. Meteors tend to follow in the wake of a comet because comets lose mass when they approach the sun. Six meteors can be seen per hour on any clear night.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(In 1966, a meteor storm over the  southwest United States displayed 100 meteors each second, all radiating from the same point in the constellation Leo.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100In 1966, a meteor storm over the  southwest United States displayed 100 meteors each second, all radiating from the same point in the constellation Leo.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Meteor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(An extraterrestrial particle, usually the size of an apple seed, that slams into the Earth\'s atmosphere and often produces a bright tail. Meteors tend to follow in the wake of a comet because comets lose mass when they approach the sun. Six meteors can be seen per hour on any clear night.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(In 1966, a meteor storm over the  southwest United States displayed 100 meteors each second, all radiating from the same point in the constellation Leo.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100In 1966, a meteor storm over the  southwest United States displayed 100 meteors each second, all radiating from the same point in the constellation Leo.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Meteor Shower)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(When more meteors than usual seem to radiate from a single point in the sky, called the radiant. Meteor showers occur when the Earth plows through a dust trail left in the wake of a comet. Most meteor showers average 50 meteors per hour, but on occasion can reach 100 meteors per minute.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Meteorite)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The material of a meteor that hits the Earth. Meteorites can only come from meteoroids larger than 6 inches because smaller meteoroids will burn up in the Earth\'s atmosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Meteoroid)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Solid objects that are smaller than an asteroid and roam interplanetary space. They are called meteors if they strike Earth\'s atmosphere, and they are called meteorites in the unlikely event that they survive all the way to the ground.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Michelson, Albert Abraham)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Michelson, Albert Abraham (1852-1931))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Michelson, Albert Abraham (1852-1931)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(American physicist who was the first person to measure the diameter of a star other than the sun. He is perhaps best known, however, for his accurate measurements of the speed of light and for his experiment with Edward Morley, which demonstrated that light travels at a constant speed through space.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Michelson-Morley Experiment)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Michelson - Morley Experiment)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Michelson - Morley Experiment\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Experiment performed in 1887 that sought to detect the effects of ether. It was once thought that space was filled with a substance called ether because it was believed that light needed some medium to travel through. By splitting a beam of light and then recombining it after bouncing it off mirrors, Michelson and Morley were able to determine that the Earth did not travel through ether.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Micrometeorite)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Meteoroid small enough to make it through the Earth\'s atmosphere intact.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Microscopium)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Microscopium (The Microscope))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Microscopium (The Microscope)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation lying near Sagittarius.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Milky Way)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Spiral galaxy composed of 200 billion stars. It is where our sun and all its planets reside. The Milky Way is shaped like a disk with a large bulge called the nucleus at the center. It looks similar to two halves of a tennis ball placed over the hole in a phonographic record, only the record would be 100,000 lightyears across and the tennis ball bulge would be 20 lightyears across. The galaxy is rather thin compared to its width; at the outer regions, where the sun lies, the Milky Way is only about 1,000 lightyears thick.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There are three main parts of the Milky Way that can be separated by the kinds of stars in each: the halo, the nucleus (bulge), and the disk.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The halo is composed of stars that formed before the Milky Way collapsed from a spherical cloud into the disk that it is now. These stars are very old, and, since they were formed before the Milky Way collapsed, they reside outside the plane of the galaxy (above and below the phonographic record). Also, since these stars formed about 15 billion years ago, they are poor in elements heavier than hydrogen and helium. Arcturus is a star from the halo that happens to be presently passing through the disk close to the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The bulge (tennis ball) is composed of low mass stars that have an age similar to that of the stars in the halo. The bulge contains most of the visible mass of the galaxy. At the center of the bulge lies an intense source of radio emission which some astronomers think is supermassive black hole. Unfortunately much dust and gas in this part of the galaxy blocks our view with optical telescopes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The disk is composed of the youngest stars. The disk is not very homogeneous. For example, the disk stars tend to form groups called open clusters, and there are concentrations of inter-galactic gas which form the spiral arms. The spiral arms are rich in hydrogen as well as young, hot blue stars. The disk is not perfectly flat; towards the edges it is warped, possibly due to the effects of the Milky Way\'s several satellite galaxies. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There are elongated interstellar grains composed mainly of silicon and graphite in the galaxy. These grains absorb light, making it impossible to see further than 6,000 lightyears along the galactic plain. Radio telescopes, however, can penetrate these dust particles; thus, most of our information about the shape of our galaxy comes from studies using radio waves.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There appears to be a sphere of dark matter, called the corona, circling the Milky Way that contains 10 times the visible mass of the galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Andromeda, as it might appear from a distant planet in the Small Magellanic Cloud, hangs just right of center in this illustration. The spiral dominating the sky above is our galaxy. Its nucleus is less than a tenth as distant as Andromeda\'s. Coincidentally, our sun is situated directly above Andromeda and horizontally left of our galactic center.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Andromeda, as it might appear from a distant planet in the Small Magellanic Cloud, hangs just right of center in this illustration. The spiral dominating the sky above is our galaxy. Its nucleus is less than a tenth as distant as Andromeda\'s. Coincidentally, our sun is situated directly above Andromeda and horizontally left of our galactic center.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(The Great Rift obscuring the glow in the nucleus of our galaxy. Deneb in Cygnus is at the left end of the Rift. Sagittarius is at the lower right.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The Great Rift obscuring the glow in the nucleus of our galaxy. Deneb in Cygnus is at the left end of the Rift. Sagittarius is at the lower right.\x01\x0e-0100)
