[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Moon Illusion)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Optical illusion of the moon appearing larger than it really is when near the horizon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Mount Palomar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Site of Hale Observatory\'s 200 inch (508 cm) reflecting telescope. Located outside of San Diego, it is more properly called Palomar Mountain.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Mount Wilson)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(NW of Los Angeles, site of Hale Observatory\'s 100 inch (254 cm) reflecting telescope. Mount Wilson\'s effectiveness has been sharply reduced by light pollution from Los Angeles. From 1917 to 1957, this telescope was the largest in the world. Hubble used this telescope to find evidence that the universe is expanding and that the Milky Way is an average galaxy. At Mount Wilson, Shapley found that our solar system is 30,000 lightyears from the center of the Milky Way, and Baade did the research that led to distinguishing population I from population II stars. Hale used the observatory to explore sunspots, and right now more research on sunspots and solar activity is being done at Mount Wilson. Future plans at this observatory are to use a new telescope to map Mars and to look at how its dust storms begin and develop.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Musca)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Musca (The Fly))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Musca (The Fly)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Small southern constellation near Crux, the Southern Cross.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(NGC205)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Andromeda Galaxy Illustration)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Andromeda Galaxy Illustration\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nadir)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Point 90 degrees below the horizon and directly under one\'s feet.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(NASA)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(NASA)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100NASA\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Established in July of 1958, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration is a federal agency authorized to regulate all civilian aeronautic research and space programs.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(National Radio Astronomy Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(National Radio Astronomy Observatory (NRAO))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100National Radio Astronomy Observatory (NRAO)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio observatory that was founded in 1957 at Green Bank, West Virginia.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Navigation Satellites)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Satellites that can determine the position of any object on the Earth to within a few feet. They work by using the Doppler effect. At a regular time interval they emit a signal, and, since the position of the satellite is known, by figuring the time it takes the signal to reach your position a computer can calculate where you are. The more navigation satellites that there are, the more accurately one can pinpoint locations.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Neap Tide)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Tides.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Tides.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Nebula (Cloud))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nebula (Cloud)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Regions of dust and gas in space. Examples of nebulas are the Orion nebula, the Horsehead nebula, the Trifid nebula, the Lagoon nebula, and the California nebula. Some, such as emission nebulas (for example, the Orion nebula), glow by themselves and are usually red; others, such as reflection nebulas (for example, the Pleiades), are nebulas that reflect the light of nearby stars. Absorption nebulas, such as the Horsehead nebula, do not glow at all and only appear as a dark pattern against a light background. Nebulas are places where new stars can be born and sometimes where an old star has blown itself apart in a supernova such as the Crab Nebula.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Nebulas are clouds of gas and dust many lightyears across.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nebulas are clouds of gas and dust many lightyears across.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Nebula (Cloud))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nebula (Cloud)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Regions of dust and gas in space. Examples of nebulas are the Orion nebula, the Horsehead nebula, the Trifid nebula, the Lagoon nebula, and the California nebula. Some, such as emission nebulas (for example, the Orion nebula), glow by themselves and are usually red; others, such as reflection nebulas (for example, the Pleiades), are nebulas that reflect the light of nearby stars. Absorption nebulas, such as the Horsehead nebula, do not glow at all and only appear as a dark pattern against a light background. Nebulas are places where new stars can be born and sometimes where an old star has blown itself apart in a supernova such as the Crab Nebula.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Nebulas are clouds of gas and dust many lightyears across.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nebulas are clouds of gas and dust many lightyears across.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Nebula (Cloud))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nebula (Cloud)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Regions of dust and gas in space. Examples of nebulas are the Orion nebula, the Horsehead nebula, the Trifid nebula, the Lagoon nebula, and the California nebula. Some, such as emission nebulas (for example, the Orion nebula), glow by themselves and are usually red; others, such as reflection nebulas (for example, the Pleiades), are nebulas that reflect the light of nearby stars. Absorption nebulas, such as the Horsehead nebula, do not glow at all and only appear as a dark pattern against a light background. Nebulas are places where new stars can be born and sometimes where an old star has blown itself apart in a supernova such as the Crab Nebula.)
