[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Nebulas are clouds of gas and dust many lightyears across.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Neptune)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Eighth planet in the solar system, although until 1999 it will be the ninth because Pluto is currently closer than Neptune to the sun. Neptune\'s average distance from the sun is 2.79 billion miles (4.5 billion km) or 30 a.u. Its nearly circular orbit takes it once around the sun every 165 years. In order to escape from Neptune\'s gravity you must travel faster than 14.1 miles (23.5 km) per second. Neptune rotates once every 16 hours, 6 minutes. Neptune has a radius of 14,731 miles (24,552 km) and a mass of 17.2 times that of Earth, making it the densest of the giant gas planets. Neptune\'s core is most likely liquid. It had been suspected for a few years that Neptune had rings, but this was not confirmed until Voyager 2\'s visit in 1989. Neptune\'s rings are clumped into arcs which reflect about as much light as coal does.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Neptune\'s atmosphere is mainly composed of hydrogen, methane, and probably helium. Neptune\'s surprisingly stormy atmosphere contains a storm about the size of the Earth that has been labeled the \"Great Dark Spot\". A smaller storm, D2, and a white spot, nicknamed Scooter, also lie in Neptune\'s atmosphere. Neptune, like Jupiter and Saturn, gives off more heat than it receives from the sun. It is possible that Neptune is still cooling from when it was formed. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Discovery)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Discovery\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Neptune was discovered by calculations done independently by John Adams in 1843 and Urbain Leverrier in 1846. Since Uranus\' observed orbit was always off its predicted orbit, it was thought that something must be disturbing the orbit of Uranus, and, based on Newton\'s theory of gravity, Adams and Leverrier calculated Neptune\'s position. In 1846, Johann Galle used Leverrier\'s calculations and quickly found Neptune, less than 1\xc2\xb0 from where Leverrier had predicted.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nereid)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Faint moon of Neptune that was discovered in 1949. It is 210 miles (340 km) wide, 3,424,400 miles (5,511,000 km) from Neptune, and orbits it every 360 days, 4 hours, and 48 minutes. Nereid\'s orbit, which is tilted 27\xc2\xb0 from Neptune\'s equator, has an eccentricity of 0.75, the largest eccentricity of any satellite in the solar system. On Voyager 2\'s 1989 visit, it was seen that Nereid looks like an asteroid, and that it is a salt and pepper moon: one side appearing bright with an albedo of almost 1 and the other much darker with an albedo of around 0.25.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Neutron)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(One of the basic constituents of an atom, resembling a proton, but with no charge. Neutrons stabilize the nuclei of atoms by easing the like-charge repulsion between protons. Neutrons are slightly heavier than protons but carry no charge. They are always located in the nuclei (center) of atoms.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Neutron Star)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(A star with a mass between about 1.5 to 3 times that of the sun, the core of which has collapsed under the pressure of gravity after it\'s nuclear fuel has been exhausted.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(At the point in the collapse where the inward crush of gravity balances the pressure between the neutrons, much of the original mass in the outer layers of the star is blown away in a supernova explosion. The progenitor star, once larger than the sun, has now shrunk into a neutron star about the size of a city. The resulting density of the neutron star is almost unimaginable. A thimble full of neutron star matter would weigh 100 million tons and an ocean-liner full would contain the entire mass of the moon. If one could drop a marble sized chunk of neutron star material to the ground, it would punch a hole clean through the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(New Moon)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Period when the moon is exactly lined up between the sun and the Earth, and therefore is dark to the Earth. If the moon\'s orbital plane was not tilted, there would be a solar eclipse at every new moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Newton, Sir Isaac)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Newton, Sir Isaac (1642-1727))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Newton, Sir Isaac (1642-1727)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(British mathematician who is most famous for his proposal of a theory of universal gravitation, where gravity increases with mass and decreases with the square of distance from an object. This means that an object 3 times farther away will have a gravitational pull 9 times less (3 X 3), if both bodies have the same mass! Newton also generalized his three laws of motion:)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_list_start(, 1)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Every object stays in a state of rest or motion unless a force acts upon it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(Every object stays in a state of rest or motion unless a force acts upon it.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Change in motion (acceleration) is proportional to the force that creates it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(Change in motion (acceleration) is proportional to the force that creates it.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Every action has an equal and opposite reaction.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(The Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Newton published his ideas in \x01\x0e+0200Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica\x01\x0e-0200 (\x01\x0e+0200The Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy\x01\x0e-0200) in 1687. Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz independently developed calculus, which can be thought of as the mathematics of change, in the mid to late 1600\'s. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(Every action has an equal and opposite reaction.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] Newton published his ideas in \x01\x0e+0200Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica\x01\x0e-0200 (\x01\x0e+0200The Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy\x01\x0e-0200) in 1687. Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz independently developed calculus, which can be thought of as the mathematics of change, in the mid to late 1600\'s. 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 11)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. Every object stays in a state of rest or motion unless a force acts upon it.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] \x01\x0e*032. 2. Change in motion (acceleration) is proportional to the force that creates it.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] \x01\x0e*033. 3. Every action has an equal and opposite reaction.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] Newton published his ideas in \x01\x0e+0200Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica\x01\x0e-0200 (\x01\x0e+0200The Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy\x01\x0e-0200) in 1687. Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz independently developed calculus, which can be thought of as the mathematics of change, in the mid to late 1600\'s. 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 11)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Newton, who also worked with telescopes, determined the cause of spherical aberration, which he was able to avoid by using a parabolic mirror instead of a spherical mirror. Newton\'s work with telescopes led him to develop his own, which is now known as the Newtonian reflector.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(Optiks)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Newton also experimented with prisms. He realized that if Snell\'s law (which talks about how much light will be bent when it travels from one medium, such as air, to another, such as water) applied, as was thought, to the beam of light as a whole, the whole beam would be bent the same amount and no spectrum would be produced. He realized that the law must instead apply to each individual color and that white light could be thought of as a mixture of all colors. Each of these would be bent a different amount as it went through the prism, producing a spectrum. In 1704, he published his findings in \x01\x0e+0200Optiks\x01\x0e-0200.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Newtonian Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Type of reflecting telescope that uses a parabola-shaped (parabolic) mirror to collect and focus light. The parabolic mirror reflects light to a flat diagonal mirror that is mounted in the tube, which then reflects the light into lenses that are perpendicular to the tube. Because of their simple but effective design, Newtonian reflectors are popular among astronomers.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(NGC)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(New General Catalogue of Nebulae and Clusters of Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200New General Catalogue of Nebulae and Clusters of Stars\x01\x0e-0200. This catalogue, compiled in 1888, is now the standard list of many deep sky objects.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nodes)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Two points where the moon\'s path crosses the ecliptic (Earth -sun orbit plane).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Norma)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Norma (The Level))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Norma (The Level)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Small, dim, southern constellation lying between Lupus and Apa.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Northern Lights)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Common name for aurora borealis.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nova)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Explosion on the surface of a star in a binary system, which causes the star to become 100,000 times as luminous as normal. Novas are named after the constellation in which they are spotted and the year in which they are most luminous. Often the star was invisible to the naked eye before the explosion, and, to people who did not use telescopes, the nova appeared to be a new star (\"nova\" is Latin for \"new\").)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Novas are believed to happen in double star systems of old stars, such as red giants and white dwarfs. As the red giant expands, some of its gas comes close enough to the white dwarf for its gravity to reel in the gas. When enough of this gas (about 100 times the mass of the Earth) has built up on the white dwarf, a nuclear reaction occurs, and the white dwarf spews out gas and other material. This explosion causes the white dwarf\'s luminosity to increase (sometimes to more than that of 100,000 suns), and, after the explosion, the luminosity settles back to its normal level. It is thought that once the stars settle down again after the explosion, the white dwarf will accumulate more material from the red giant, and there will be another explosion.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Scientists once thought that novas occur every 1,000 to 10,000 years in such systems. Now, however, they believe there is a period of at least 100,000 years between novas because the force of the nova shoves the two stars away from each other, and it takes time for gravity to pull them close enough for the red giant to give the white dwarf material again.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Nutation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(S-shaped wobbling of the Earth\'s axis due to change in the angle of the axial tilt. This change is caused by the gravitational effects of the moon on Earth, which cause Earth to wobble like a top that is being pulled. This change also causes the Earth\'s rotation period to vary.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Oberon)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Outermost moon of Uranus, lying 361,782 miles (583,520 km) away from the planet. Oberon was discovered in 1787 by Herschel and was the first moon of Uranus to be discovered. It has a brownish surface that is pitted with many craters. They are surrounded by bright rays splashed out by bombarding meteors. It appears that something flowed out of them after impact. Oberon has a diameter of 964 miles (1,554 km) and orbits Uranus in 13 days 11 hours 15 minutes and 36.5 seconds.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Object Glass)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Antiquated name for the objective lens of a telescope. The objective lens is the primary light gathering optical glass of a refracting telescope.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Observatories, History of)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Although people have been looking at stars since prehistoric times, they have only recently begun to build buildings specifically for viewing the sky. People were initially interested in predicting the movements of the stars mainly for purposes of astrology. It has been suggested that one of Stonehenge\'s (2500 BC to 1700 BC) purposes may have been to track the movements of stars. However, at this time, this cannot be stated with certainty. It is also believed that the famous \"Tower of Babel\" may have been an astrological observatory of the Babylonians. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Hipparchus\' observatory on Rhodes, founded in 150 BC, seems to be the first instance of using observing tools, such as quadrants (which were used to measure altitude) in an observatory. Observatories that resemble more closely those used today were built in Damascus and Baghdad from 800 to 900 AD. In 1260, an observatory, which was used to modify the Ptolemaic view of the universe, was built in what is now Iran. Then, in 1420, a prince named Ulugh Beg built an observatory, which he used to produce a star catalog. Tycho Brahe\'s famous observatory, Uraniborg, was built on Hven in 1576.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(None of these observatories, however, had telescopes, which were not invented until 1609. In the 1600\'s, 1700\'s, and 1800\'s, observatories equipped with progressively larger telescopes were built at Cape Town, Greenwich, Paris, and Washington, DC. Today, the observatory with the largest optical telescope is on Mauna Kea in Hawaii. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph( In the 1930\'s, people moved from just observing in visible light to observing with other wavelengths of radiation. After Karl Jansky observed radio waves coming from the center of the Milky Way in 1931, radio telescopes were built. One of these, the Very Large Array, is composed of 27 radio telescopes, whose observations can be combined to form one image.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(With the advent of the space age, many telescopes, including the Hubble Space Telescope, the Infrared Astronomical Satellite, and the Orbiting Solar Observatory, have been launched into space, above the Earth\'s atmosphere. This allows for unobstructed observations of space as well as observations in wavelengths that are absorbed by the atmosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Occultation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Passage of one astronomical body in front of another. Occultations allow astronomers to determine the nature and size of objects. For example, the occulting of a star by Uranus allowed astronomers to detect rings encircling the planet, and the occulting of a star by Pluto allowed astronomers to determine that it had an atmosphere, as its light gradually dimmed.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Octans)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Octans (The Octant))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Octans (The Octant)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation between Pavo and Chameleon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Olbers_Henrich_Wilhelm_Mattaus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Olbers, (Henrich) Wilhelm (Mattaus) (1758-1840))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Olbers, (Henrich) Wilhelm (Mattaus) (1758-1840)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(German astronomer who proposed Olbers\' Paradox and studied comets. He discovered five, including Olbers\' Comet, and he calculated that the Earth would pass through the tail of Biela\'s Comet, a previously discovered comet. Olbers also proposed that comets\' tails always point away from the sun because the solar wind blows them back. Furthermore, Olbers believed that a planet existed between Mars and Jupiter. However, after discovering the asteroid Pallas in 1802, Olbers decided that this planet must have broken up and formed the asteroid belt.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Olber\'s Paradox)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Olbers\' Paradox)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Olbers\' Paradox\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\"Why is the sky dark?\" Olber said that if one were to start with the assumption that the universe is infinitely old and that it contains roughly the same amount of stars in every area, then every line of sight should lead to the surface of a star, and the night sky should be ablaze with light. Various reasons have been proposed for the obvious fact that the sky is not shining brightly. Olber himself thought that the reason was due to interstellar gas; others thought it was due to the red shifting of all the galaxies; later, people would say that the reason that the sky is not ablaze with light is that the universe is not infinitely old, and, thus, stars have not had time to fill the universe with light. This last explanation is the correct one: the night sky is dark not because the universe is expanding, but because it is still young.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Olympus Mons)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Olympus Mons)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Olympus Mons\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Highest point on Mars and the tallest mountain in the solar system. Olympus is a 88,600 ft. (26.4 km) high volcano with a base around the size of Arizona. From Earth its true nature was not noticed, and, due to cloud cover, it was originally named Nix Olympica (Snows of Olympus).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Oort Cloud)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Cloud, located about 40,000 to 50,000 a.u. from the sun, where most of the comets in our solar system originate. Comets are thought to be released from this cloud when it is disturbed. The existence of this cloud which is thought to be made of the remains of the formation of our solar system, was proposed by Jan Heinrich Oort in 1950.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Oort, Jan Hendrik)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Oort, Jan Hendrik (b. 1900))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Oort, Jan Hendrik (b. 1900)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Dutch astronomer who added immensely to our knowledge of the structure and size of the Milky Way. After completing a comprehensive analysis of star motions and distributions he accurately calculated the visible mass of the Milky Way and the sun\'s place in it. In 1950 Oort proposed that there is a spherical cloud of icy, tail-less comets surrounding the sun about one lightyear away. When perturbed by passing stars, the Oort cloud hurls comets towards the solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Open Cluster)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Formless star cluster containing anywhere from a few stars to several thousand in a region usually several light years across. Open clusters are relatively young, often containing hot, highly luminous stars. They are located in the disk of the galaxy (which is why they are sometimes called galactic clusters) in the spiral arms. The member stars are much more spaced out than in globular clusters. Some well known open clusters include the Pleiades (M45), The Beehive (M44), and the Jewel Box.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Ophiuchus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Ophiuchus (The Snake Handler))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ophiuchus (The Snake Handler)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large equatorial constellation that lies near Serpens and is visible in the summer. Nowadays, few companies need to hire a \"serpent-holder,\" and the constellation is obscure and hard to recognize. Although not traditionally a zodiac constellation, the ecliptic passes through its southern part. Barnard\'s star and several globular clusters lie in Ophiuchus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Opposition)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Position of a planet outside of Earth\'s orbit (superior planet) when it is opposite the sun in the sky and has an elongation of 180 degrees. Planets are at their full phase at opposition and are closest to Earth. Since Mars\' orbit is a bit elongated, some of its oppositions bring it closer to Earth than others. A favorable of opposition brings the planet near its closest possible approach with Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Orbit)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Path bodies follow as they move around one another.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Orion)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Orion (The Hunter))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Orion (The Hunter)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Bright, large equatorial constellation that lies near Lepus and Eridanus and is easily visible by the three stars that form the belt. Ancient Sumerians thought of Orion as a sheep, not a hunter. One of the most famous features of Orion is its nebula (M42) in his sword that is just visible to the unaided eye. It is referred to as the Orion Nebula. Orion also contains the famous, brilliant stars Betelgeuse and Rigel.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Orion Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Orion Nebula (M42))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Orion Nebula (M42)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large emission nebula located in the sword of Orion. The nebula is 1,300 lightyears away and 25 lightyears in diameter. Parts of the nebula are an active stellar nursery with some stars being only a few thousand years old (only infants by star lives). It contains enough material to form 100,000 stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Oscillating Universe)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Universe model contending that the universe has cycles of expansions and contractions. If there is enough matter in the universe, then the present expansion will be halted and the universe will slowly begin to collapse back in on itself. Although no one can say for sure, some astronomers predict that if this were to happen then there might be a rebound causing a new universe to be formed.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Outer Planets)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Outer Plants)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Outer Plants\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Planets further than the Earth from the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Owl Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Located in Ursa Major and, at 1.5 lightyears across, is one of the largest planetary nebulas known.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pallas)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pallas (Minor Planet 2))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pallas (Minor Planet 2)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Second asteroid to be discovered. Pallas was discovered by Olbers in 1802, and it orbits the sun every 4.61 years. With a diameter of 324 miles (523 km), Pallas is the second largest asteroid.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pandora)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Outermost, irregularly shaped shepherd moon on the F-ring of Saturn, which was discovered by Voyager.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Parabola)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Figure formed by slicing a cone at a right angle to the base. Many astronomical telescopes employ a mirror shaped like a parabola because such a shape brings incoming light rays to a single sharp focus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Parallax)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Parallax (From the Greek Word Meaning \"Change\" or \"Variation\"))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Parallax (From the Greek Word Meaning \"Change\" or \"Variation\")\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Change in the apparent position of an object relative to the background when viewed from two locations. To better visualize parallax hold your thumb at arms length and look at it with just one eye . Now, holding your thumb steady switch eyes. Notice how the thumb seemed to jump because it was viewed from two different places.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(In the same way, astronomers use opposite points in the Earth\'s orbit to measure the parallax of nearby stars. The jump or parallax astronomers measure is much less due to the great distances of the stars. Even the nearest stars have parallaxes less than 1 thirty sixth-hundredth of a degree. Only the closest stars to Earth can have their distances determined by parallax. However the measurement of parallax, where possible, is important because it is one of the only ways to directly measure distances in the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The first star to have its parallax measured was 61 Cygni in 1836. This also gave conclusive evidence that the Earth moves through space.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Parsec)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Parsec (Abbreviation for Parallax Second))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Parsec (Abbreviation for Parallax Second)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Distance at which a body would have an annual (viewed one year apart in the Earth\'s orbit) parallax of one arc second(one thirty sixth-hundredth of a degree). A parsec is equal to 3.2616 lightyears. A kiloparsec is 1000 parsecs, and a megaparsec is 1,000,000 parsecs.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Parallax)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Parallax\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pavo)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pavo (The Peacock))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pavo (The Peacock)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Southern constellation between Ara and Tucana.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pegasus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pegasus (The Winged Horse))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pegasus (The Winged Horse)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large northern constellation that represents the winged horse of Greek mythology and is best seen in the fall. Pegasus lies between Pisces and Andromeda and is easily recognized by of its asterism, the Great Square of Pegasus. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Penumbra)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Partially shaded area of an eclipse.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(Partially shaded area of an eclipse.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Outer, light-shaded area of a sunspot.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_listitem(Outer, light-shaded area of a sunspot.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_list(\x01\x0e*031. 1. Partially shaded area of an eclipse.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] \x01\x0e*032. 2. Outer, light-shaded area of a sunspot.
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Periastron)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Periastron (From the Greek Words Meaning \"Near Star\"))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Periastron (From the Greek Words Meaning \"Near Star\")\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Point in the orbit around a star at which a body comes closest to that star. Periastron is usually used to describe binary stars, butt it can also apply to planets.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Perigee)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Perigee (From the Greek Words Meaning \"Near Earth\"))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Perigee (From the Greek Words Meaning \"Near Earth\")\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Point in an artificial satellite\'s or the moon\'s orbit when it is the nearest to the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Perihelion)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Perihelion (From the Greek Words Meaning \"Near Sun\"))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Perihelion (From the Greek Words Meaning \"Near Sun\")\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Point at which an object which orbits the sun is nearest to it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Period-Luminosity Relation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Relationship between mass, absolute magnitude, and period of pulsation of Cepheid variables as discovered by Henrietta Leavitt in 1912. The brighter a Cepheid variable, the longer its period. Edwin Hubble used this relationship to find the distance to the Andromeda galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Perseids)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Annual meteor shower that reaches its peak on August 11 or 12.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Perseus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Bright northern constellation lying near Auriga and Andromeda. Perseus contains the famous variable star Algol.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Perturbation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Localized gravitational disturbance of an object\'s motion. An example of this is when a comet\'s orbit is distorted if the comet passes too close to Jupiter. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Phase)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Part of a lit object that can be observed from Earth. Only objects that pass between the observer and the sun show a full cycle of phases.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Phobos)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Innermost Martian moon, lying just outside the Roche limit. Due to atmospheric drag, Phobos is slowing down and will crash into Mars in about 40 million years. Phobos (about 45.36 miles or 28 km across at its widest) is the largest of Mars\' two potato-shaped moons, and it is peppered with craters and large grooves. It has an albedo of about five percent due to an outer layer of dust approximately 10 meters thick.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Phoebe)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Outermost moon of Saturn. Phoebe\'s retrograde orbit and extremely small size (only 120 miles or 200 km across) give rise to the idea that it is a captured body, perhaps an asteroid.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Phoenix)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Phoenix (The Phoenix))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Phoenix (The Phoenix)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Southern constellation between Eridanus and Grus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Photometer)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Device used to measure the amount of electromagnetic radiation that an object produces.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Photometry)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Science of measuring an object\'s brightness.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Photosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Photosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Photosphere\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Gaseous surface of the sun. It is composed of 200 miles (300 km) of dense gas at a temperature of 5,800 K. Networks of granules, which show the sun\'s convection processes that bring hot gases and energy to the surface, are visible on the photosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Piazzi, Giuseppe)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Piazzi, Giuseppe (1746-1826))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Piazzi, Giuseppe (1746-1826)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Italian astronomer who was the first person to discover an asteroid. He discovered Ceres, the largest of the asteroids, on January 1, 1801. Although Ceres looked like a star in his telescope (hence the name asteroid, \"starlike\") it could not be found on de Lacaille\'s star chart. This lead Piazzi to follow it over several days, eventually confirming that it was an object orbiting between Mars and Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pic Du Midi Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pic du Midi Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pic du Midi Observatory\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Established in 1882, one of the first high altitude observatories in the world, and the first to use a coronagraph (instrument used to observe the sun\'s corona). It is located in the French Pyrenees and operated by the University of Toulouse.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pictor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pictor (The Painter))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pictor (The Painter)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation between Dorado and Carina.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pinwheel Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pinwheel Galaxy (M33))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pinwheel Galaxy (M33)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large spiral galaxy we view face-on. It is about 15 million lightyears away and lies in the constellation Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(M33, the Pinwheel galaxy in Triangulum, is a member of our Local Group of galaxies. It has a very small central bulge barely visible in the corner of the picture.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M33, the Pinwheel galaxy in Triangulum, is a member of our Local Group of galaxies. It has a very small central bulge barely visible in the corner of the picture.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pinwheel Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pinwheel Galaxy (M33))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pinwheel Galaxy (M33)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large spiral galaxy we view face-on. It is about 15 million lightyears away and lies in the constellation Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(M33, the Pinwheel galaxy in Triangulum, is a member of our Local Group of galaxies. It has a very small central bulge barely visible in the corner of the picture.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M33, the Pinwheel galaxy in Triangulum, is a member of our Local Group of galaxies. It has a very small central bulge barely visible in the corner of the picture.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pinwheel Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pinwheel Galaxy (M33))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pinwheel Galaxy (M33)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Large spiral galaxy we view face-on. It is about 15 million lightyears away and lies in the constellation Ursa Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(M33, the Pinwheel galaxy in Triangulum, is a member of our Local Group of galaxies. It has a very small central bulge barely visible in the corner of the picture.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M33, the Pinwheel galaxy in Triangulum, is a member of our Local Group of galaxies. It has a very small central bulge barely visible in the corner of the picture.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pioneer)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Series of American planetary space probes. Although the first Pioneers failed to accomplish their missions, Pioneer 10 reached Jupiter (the first probe to do so), and Pioneer 11 (or Pioneer Saturn) was the first probe to take close-up pictures of Saturn.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pisces)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pisces (The Fishes))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pisces (The Fishes)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Northern zodiacal constellation located near Pegasus. The vernal equinox is located in Pisces.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Piscis Austrians)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Piscis Austrians (The Southern Fish))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Piscis Austrians (The Southern Fish)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Southern constellation located near Grus and Microscopium.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Planet)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Planet (From the Greek Word For \"Wanderer\"))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Planet (From the Greek Word For \"Wanderer\")\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Object (excluding comets, asteroids, and meteoroids) which does not emit its own light and which orbits a star. There is, however, no clear distinction between what constitutes a planet and what constitutes an asteroid. For example, there has been a debate over whether or not Pluto should be considered a planet since it is an exception (in size, eccentricity, and orbital tilt) to patterns followed by the other planets. Some people have even stated that the largest asteroid, Ceres, should be called a planet since it does follow the pattern of the other planets.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Formation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Formation\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(When stars are born, they have a disk-shaped cloud of gas left orbiting them. As the particles in this gas cloud bump into each other and join together, their greater mass leads to a greater gravitational pull. More particles are attracted, and the mass of the planetesimal (a small particle that will become a planet) gets bigger and bigger until its gravity is large enough to force it into a sphere. Planets\' gravity also forces them to change as they form, and, as their heavier elements sink to the bottom, energy is released.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Solar System)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Solar System\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The nine planets in our solar system are: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto. For a time, there was thought to be an additional planet, which was named Vulcan, between Mercury and the sun, but this theory has been disproved. Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars are often labeled as the terrestrial planets, while Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune are considered gas giants. The terrestrial planets do not have the huge gaseous atmospheres of the gas giants because they are closer to the sun and less massive. After they were formed, many of their gas particles were heated by the sun, which caused the particles to move faster, and allowed them to reach escape velocity. Due to their lower mass and gravity, escape velocity is lower on the terrestrial planets than on the gas giants. Lighter elements, such as hydrogen, were more likely than heavier elements to reach escape velocity. Consequently, most of the hydrogen left on Earth is trapped with oxygen in water.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Planetary Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Expanding gas shell surrounding a hot star. Planetary nebulas are produced when a red giant sheds its outer layers while becoming a white dwarf. Hershel coined the term planetary nebula because, to him, they looked like the disks of planets. Some common examples of planetary nebula are the Ring Nebula (M57), the Helix Nebula, and the Dumbell Nebula (M27).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Plasma)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Ionized gas consisting of pieces of atoms. As gas heats up, collisions with other atoms or strong radiation knocks electrons out of their shells, leaving a soup of electrons and naked nuclei. Most of the visible matter in the universe is believed to exist as plasma.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pleiades)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Young open cluster located in the constellation Taurus. The Pleiades consist of six stars that are visible to the unaided eye and about 400 that are not. The Pleiades are quite young, about 50 million years old. Photographs reveal a reflection nebula that lies behind the Pleiades. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pluto)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Ninth planet from the sun; however, since 1979 Pluto has actually been closer than Neptune to the sun, and Pluto will continue to be the eighth planet from the sun until March 1999. Pluto\'s status as alternately eighth and ninth planet from the sun is due to the large difference between its perihelion (2.73 billion miles or 4.4 billion km from the sun) and aphelion (4.59 billion miles or 7.4 billion km from the sun). The plane of Pluto\'s eccentric (0.250) orbit is tilted 17.2\xc2\xb0 from the ecliptic, and it takes Pluto 248 years to go once around the sun. It takes Pluto 6 days, 9 hours, 36 minutes to rotate once on its axis, which is tilted 122\xc2\xb0 from its plane of orbit.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pluto is the only planet in our solar system that has not been visited by a space probe. Most of the information we have about Pluto came from its 1988 occultation of a star, which showed that Pluto has an atmosphere, and from 1985 to 1989, when Pluto and its moon Charon eclipsed each other, giving an indication of their sizes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(There is debate over whether Pluto should be classified as a planet at all. Pluto\'s radius is just 691 miles (1,151 km), and it is an exception to most of the trends set by the other planets. They all have nearly circular orbits which lie near the ecliptic, and the other planets past the asteroid belt are gas giants. Pluto, on the other hand, as said before, has an orbit tilted 17.2\xc2\xb0 from the plane of the ecliptic, is a mixture of rock and ice, and has a density between 1.8 and 2.1 cm^(3), which is about twice that of water. To escape from Pluto\'s gravity, you must travel 0.7 miles (1.2 km) per second.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(It was determined in 1988, when Pluto\'s occultation of a star caused the star\'s light to twinkle away instead of suddenly blink out, that Pluto has an atmosphere. Pluto and Earth are the only two planets in our solar system that have atmospheres made mainly of nitrogen. Pluto\'s roughly 1.9 mile (3.19 km) thick atmosphere also contains methane and possibly carbon monoxide and argon. The average temperature of its atmosphere is roughly 100 K. Pluto appears to have seasons, with all or part of its atmosphere freezing and falling as snow when it is far from the sun and then melting when it moves closer to the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Surface)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Surface\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pluto has a rocky, reddish surface that is brighter at the poles than at the equator. Its albedo varies from 90% at its polar ice caps to 30% by its equator, with an average albedo of 50%. Pluto\'s surface temperature at the equator is roughly 58 K.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Origin)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Origin\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pluto was once thought to be an escaped moon of Neptune, but the existence of Charon goes against this theory. It is unlikely that both Pluto and Charon, which is about half the size of Pluto, escaped from Neptune\'s orbit. It is now thought that Pluto may have been an asteroid from beyond the solar system that got captured by the sun\'s gravity. Charon and Pluto, which always keep the same face pointing toward each other, are thought to have formed at the same time. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pluto\'s eccentric orbit might once have been circular. However, as Neptune moved out from the sun, Pluto may have been forced into its present orbit, which is in a 3:2 resonance with Neptune\'s (Neptune makes three orbits around the sun in the time Pluto makes two).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pluto Facts:)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pluto Facts:\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Average Daytime Temperature is about 80\xc2\xb0 K)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pressure is unknown)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Pluto has a weak and varying atmosphere which is probably composed of methane. There is no evidence of elements such as oxygen, and carbon dioxide. There is also no evidence of water vapor.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Polaris)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Polaris (The North Star))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Polaris (The North Star)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Star that lies less than one degree away from true celestial north, the place to which Earth\'s northern pole of rotation points. Polaris is actually a double star and was once a Cepheid variable. Although it is the brightest star in Ursa Minor, it is only the forty-seventh brightest in the sky.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Establishing true north with Polaris and a plumb bob.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Establishing true north with Polaris and a plumb bob.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(A one-hour exposure facing our north would show this much apparent motion of true stars. Ursa Minor is vertically below Polaris; Ursa Major is in the lower right.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100A one-hour exposure facing our north would show this much apparent motion of true stars. Ursa Minor is vertically below Polaris; Ursa Major is in the lower right.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Polaris)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Polaris (The North Star))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Polaris (The North Star)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Star that lies less than one degree away from true celestial north, the place to which Earth\'s northern pole of rotation points. Polaris is actually a double star and was once a Cepheid variable. Although it is the brightest star in Ursa Minor, it is only the forty-seventh brightest in the sky.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Establishing true north with Polaris and a plumb bob.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Establishing true north with Polaris and a plumb bob.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(A one-hour exposure facing our north would show this much apparent motion of true stars. Ursa Minor is vertically below Polaris; Ursa Major is in the lower right.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100A one-hour exposure facing our north would show this much apparent motion of true stars. Ursa Minor is vertically below Polaris; Ursa Major is in the lower right.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Polaris)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Polaris (The North Star))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Polaris (The North Star)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Star that lies less than one degree away from true celestial north, the place to which Earth\'s northern pole of rotation points. Polaris is actually a double star and was once a Cepheid variable. Although it is the brightest star in Ursa Minor, it is only the forty-seventh brightest in the sky.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Establishing true north with Polaris and a plumb bob.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Establishing true north with Polaris and a plumb bob.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(A one-hour exposure facing our north would show this much apparent motion of true stars. Ursa Minor is vertically below Polaris; Ursa Major is in the lower right.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100A one-hour exposure facing our north would show this much apparent motion of true stars. Ursa Minor is vertically below Polaris; Ursa Major is in the lower right.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pole)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(One of two tips of the axis around which an object is said to be rotating.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pole Star)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Popular name for Polaris. However, because of precession, in about 5,000 years Alpha Cephei will be the pole star, and, in about 12,000 years, the pole star will be Vega.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pollux)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Orange giant with a magnitude of 1.15 that is the brightest star in Gemini.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Populations, Stellar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Classification of stars into two types based on their ages. Type I stars are relatively young and lie in the spiral arms of the galaxy. Type II stars are older and reside in the halo of the galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Position Angle)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Angle of two binary stars relative to the north. The angle is a clockwise measurement of the fainter stars position relative to the brighter star forms a measure that can be as large as 360.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Poynting-Robertson Effect)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Slowing of small meteor particles orbiting the sun, which causes them to spiral in towards the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Precession)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Slow wobbling of the Earth on its axis. Due to precession, the axis traces out a 47\xc2\xb0 wide circle in the sky every 26,000 years as it changes the direction of tilt. A consequence of precession is in 5,000 years Alpha Cephei will be the pole star, and in 12,000 years Vega will be closest to the celestial north pole.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Prime Focus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Point where the primary mirror of a reflecting telescope focuses an image.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Prominence)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Slender arcs of erupting gas from the sun\'s chromosphere into its corona. They are best seen through an H-alpha filter, have low densities, and seem to be supported by magnetic fields.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Proper Motion)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Yearly drift of a star, measured in arc seconds, caused by its movement through space. The star with the largest proper motion is Barnard\'s star, which moves 10.3 arc seconds a year.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Propulsion)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Propulsion (Propulsion System))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Propulsion (Propulsion System)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Rockets on a spacecraft that deliver thrust to propel the craft through space. Unlike what is portrayed in many science fiction movies, the engines of a spacecraft only need to burn when the craft changes speed or direction. Propulsion systems of today burn chemical propellants that are either solid or liquid. Both have their advantages. Solid fuel is easy to handle and can be stored in rockets forever. However, once ignited, it is difficult to control. The rocket must consume all of its solid fuel before shutting down. Liquid fuel, on the other hand, is easier to guide. Valves in the engine open and restrict the flow and direct the burn. However, liquid must be pumped into the rocket before launch and maintained at a low temperature to keep it stable. Liquid fuels used in propulsion systems include liquid hydrogen, hydrazine, and kerosene.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Also, since there is no oxygen in space, propulsion systems must carry their own to burn the fuel. Scientists are working on propulsion systems that do not depend on chemical propellants. On Earth, the rapid acceleration provided by chemical fuel is required to escape gravity. However, in space, less potent methods work better. Huge sails could use the solar wind for transit between the inner planets. Engineers have pondered using electricity to heat escaping ions. A better idea still is harnessing nuclear fusion as an energy source. Fusion engines could drive interplanetary and even interstellar journeys.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Proton-Proton Chain)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Nuclear fusion producing helium from hydrogen. It takes four hydrogen nuclei, which are also protons, to fuse one helium atom. The sun changes almost 600 million tons of hydrogen to helium through this process every second. Seven percent, or roughly four million tons, of this material is converted into energy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Protostar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Early stage of a star\'s life when gas and dust have condensed enough to glow, but the star has not yet ignited fusion.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Proxima Centauri)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Nearest star to the sun, 4.23 lightyears away, about 0.1 lightyears closer than the other stars in this system. Proxima is a red dwarf with a magnitude of 10.7.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Ptolemy Claudius Ptolemaeus (C. 170 AD))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Ptolemy Claudius Ptolemaeus (c. 170 AD))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ptolemy Claudius Ptolemaeus (c. 170 AD)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(Almagest)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Astronomer who wrote on many topics, I ncluding mathematics, optics, and geography. In \x01\x0e+0200Almagest\x01\x0e-0200, Ptolemy supported a geocentric universe. Although he did consider the heliocentric universe, he rejected it when he could find no conclusive evidence for it. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pulkovo Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Russian observatory founded in 1718 that has consistently produced high quality work.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pulsar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio sources that show short repeating radio patterns with a regularity rivaling that of atomic clocks. Pulsars are spinning neutron stars that sit at the center of supernova remnants. Over 100 pulsars are now known. Pulsars gradually slow their rotation and fade over 10 billion years, with the youngest having periods of a few milliseconds (they spin thousands of time around in one second) and the oldest about four seconds. While most emit radio waves, a few have been observed in visible light and X-rays. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Discovery)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Discovery\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(In 1967, Jocelyn Bell was a graduate student working at Jodrell Bank radio observatory in England. She was part of a team, led by Antony Hewish, that was studying scintillations (twinkling) in quasars. Bell noticed \"scruff\" plotted on the recorder as the telescope surveyed the sky. The team found several sources that sounded like ticking stopwatches. For a few months, the team toyed with the idea that these were alien beacons, nicknaming them LGMs (Little Green Men). Later, astronomers thought pulsars originated from two white dwarfs in a close orbit. They were attempting to make this model fit the observed emissions when they encountered the Crab Pulsar.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Clicking 30 times a second, the Crab Pulsar stirs the center of the Crab Nebula, a remnant of the supernova explosion of 1054. It was realized that only a neutron star has the titanic gravity to spin so swiftly and remain intact.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Formation and Structure)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Formation and Structure\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(In the collapse of a supernova, gravity and pressure are so strong that they squeeze the core into an exotic kind of matter called \"neutron degenerate.\" The mass of 3 suns crunches into a ball the size of a small city. If the parent star had any slight rotation, its degenerate core will whirl around furiously, like a skater drawing in her arms in a tight spin. Also, if the star had any magnetic field, the resulting pulsar will generate a dynamo a million times stronger.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Most bodies do not align their magnetic field with their axis of spin. The Earth, for example, has a geographic north pole and a magnetic north pole that lie several hundred miles apart. In a pulsar, trapped ions whip around and broadcast beams of energy (synchrotron) at their magnetic poles, streaming out a signal like a cosmic lighthouse. To get a clearer picture of a pulsar, think of a flashlight with a beam of light coming out of each end. Placing this double ended flashlight on a flat surface and giving it a spin would be a good model of a pulsar. If Earth happens to lie in line with one of the beams of energy, radio telescopes hear ticks as the beacon sweeps by. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Scientists believe neutron stars have a crystalline surface a few inches thick. Here, gravity and magnetism are so strong they distort atoms into needle shapes. Below the surface lies an ocean of superfluid. If you chill helium down to 4 K, it becomes a superfluid, showing zero viscosity. Pour this superfluid into your bathtub, paddle it around, and then leave. When you return months later, the liquid helium still swirls the same way you left it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Inward toward the core is more bizarre matter. Weird forces buzz fundamental particles into and out of creation. Pulsars prove to be one of the most exotic structures in the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Puppis)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Puppis (The Stern))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Puppis (The Stern)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Southern constellation near Canis Major.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pythagoras)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pythagoras (c. 580 BC- c. 500 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pythagoras (c. 580 BC- c. 500 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Greek mathematician who believed that the Earth was a sphere at the center of the universe. He realized that the sun, moon, and planets follow certain courses through the sky, and he believed that each rested on a celestial sphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Pyxis)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Pyxis (The Navigational Compass))
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Pyxis (The Navigational Compass)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Dim southern constellation between Antler and Puppis.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(QSO)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Quasi Stellar Object, or quasar.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Quadrantic Meteors)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Annual meteor shower around January third and fourth. Its radiant lies in Bootes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Quasar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Star-like source of radio emission. The name quasar is a snappier version of the old name, QSO (Quasi-Stellar Objects). About 200 quasars have been identified.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(In 1963, astronomers found powerful radio sources corresponding to what looked like ordinary stars. When optical astronomers scrutinized quasars\' spectra, they noticed bizarre absorption lines. Maarten Schmitt realized that these \"radio stars\" emit most of their energy in visible light, but it is red-shifted down to radio (See Doppler Effect). Large red shifts imply enormous movement away from Earth, and, using Hubble\'s law, it can be calculated that quasars must lie billions of lightyears away, far too distant to be ordinary stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Astronomers then recognized that if quasars are so far away and shine so brightly, they must produce titanic amounts of power. A typical quasar radiates the energy of 100 galaxies. Also, since quasars change their brightness over a few hours, they must be about as big as our solar system. Astronomers grappled with these observations, wondering what mechanism could supply a dynamo.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Today, they believe quasars are the cores of young galaxies that emitted fantastic amounts of energy when the universe was young.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radar Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Use of radio pulses to study the heavens by bouncing radio waves off objects. Astronomers have employed radar to watch meteor showers and measure distances within the solar system. Using the giant Arecibo dish, signals have been bounced off Venus to penetrate its clouds and map its surface. More recently, the Magellan probe circled Venus and surveyed it in much finer detail, using a special kind of radar. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radial Velocity)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Speed of an object relative to an observer measured in the direction of the line of sight (speed toward or away from an observer). Radial velocity is often measured using the Doppler effect.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radiant)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Point from which a meteor shower seems to radiate. The meteors really follow parallel paths, but from our viewpoint the streaks seem to trace back to one region, the radiant. It is like train tracks that recede to the horizon. Even though the tracks remain parallel, they appear to diverge from one point. Meteor showers are named for the constellation in which their radiant seems to lie.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radiation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Electromagnetic Radiation.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Electromagnetic Radiation.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radiation Belt)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Radiation Belt)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Radiation Belt\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_emphasis(See Van Allen Belt.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Van Allen Belt.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radio Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Use of radio waves to explore the universe. Radio waves have opened new gateways to astronomers and have led to exciting discoveries. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(History)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100History\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(In 1931, American radio engineer Karl Jansky was trying to get rid of an irritating hiss in long-range communication. He eliminated known sources of static, such as car engines and lightning. Observing that the noise seemed to repeat with the rotation of the Earth, he constructed a directional antenna and concluded that the buzz must be extraterrestrial.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Jansky had stumbled upon a strong source of radio waves from Sagittarius. His work inspired amateur enthusiast Grote Reber, who erected the first radio telescope on the plains of Illinois. Although small by today\'s standards, it found many strong radio sources. However, Grote\'s work went unnoticed by the astronomical community for several years. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Not until after World War II did astronomers become seriously interested in studying radio astronomy. The war left a surplus of equipment, so astronomers used the old parts to build new telescopes. The science of radio astronomy was born.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(The Character of Radio Waves)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The Character of Radio Waves\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio forms the long end of the electromagnetic spectrum, with wavelengths ranging from a few centimeters to kilometers. These long wavelengths are not absorbed or scattered by the atmosphere. This lets radio telescopes monitor both during the day and at night. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio radiation is generated in two ways: thermal and non-thermal. Thermal emission is summarized by Wein\'s law, which states that any mass above absolute zero radiates a continuous spectrum. Cold bodies, like interstellar dust and gas, emit most of their energy in radio. Stars do not give off much radio energy because they are too hot, and they shine brightest in visible and infrared wavelengths. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The other way to produce radio is non-thermal. It comes from accelerating electrons and is the way a broadcast tower transmits signals. Non-thermal emission reveals violent acts of nature like jets of plasma or expanding supernova remnants. Sources such as pulsars show synchrotron radiation coming from charged particles trapped in a powerful magnetic field whipping around at nearly the speed of light. Streams of ions swirling into a black hole also shower radio energy as they are consumed.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Radar Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Radar Astronomy\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(One of the first uses of radio astronomy was radar. Radar pulses showed that the moon\'s surface is grainy. Astronomers also used radar to measure the distances to nearby planets and the sun. Also, by analyzing return signals, they were able to clock rotation periods and even to identify Venus\' retrograde spin. Using radar, astronomers have also studied meteors. Their shell of vapor makes an excellent radar target because ionized gas reflects radio waves, allowing them to be tracked even in the day. This confirmed that meteors are specks of dust that orbit the sun. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Recently, NASA launched the Magellan probe to Venus. From orbit, the satellite used radar to pierce the thick atmosphere and map the surface in unprecedented detail. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Galactic Sources of Radio)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Galactic Sources of Radio\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The sky is ablaze with radio emission. With its long wavelength, radio can penetrate the interstellar dust that blocks other forms of radiation. The galactic core, for example, is a strong radio emitter, as was observed by Jansky. Later, the galactic core was resolved into four sources. Astronomers think at least one of these sources is a supermassive black hole that lurks in the galactic core. Its radio signals suggest it was active in the past but has swallowed all its enveloping gas and now waits for more clouds to float by so it can suck them in.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Cassiopeia is the site of the brightest radio emitter, cataloged as Cassiopeia A. It is the expanding shell of a supernova that exploded in about 1667, but it was not seen on Earth because of the obscuring dust. As the shock wave slams into the surrounding interstellar medium, radio waves ripple away from its hot gas. Other supernova remnants that are strong emitters are the Veil Nebula and the Crab Nebula. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The Crab also reveals a pulsar, called Taurus A, which sits at the center and whirls around 30 times a second. A pulsar is a spinning neutron star that shoots radio waves away from its magnetic poles. If a pole sweeps toward Earth, radio telescopes hear bursts that sound like machine gun fire. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio astronomers sometimes listen for signals of less violent origin. Interstellar clouds of hydrogen gas emit radio of 21 centimeter wavelength when their protons and electrons reorient themselves. Astronomers analyzed this radiation to map the Milky Way\'s spiral arms. They know that two arms wind away from the egg-shaped nucleus and that the sun sits in the space between two arms. Radio waves also permit astronomers to probe distant corners of our galaxy. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Extragalactic Radio Sources)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Extragalactic Radio Sources\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(When astronomers direct their radio telescopes outside the Milky Way, they encounter a universe alive with titanic explosions. An object labeled Cygnus A unveiled the realm of radio galaxies. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Radio galaxies went unnoticed by optical astronomers. Only after their radio colleagues urged them did they examine the photographs, which showed jets of plasma. Cygnus A, the first radio galaxy discovered, has two nuclei. Other radio galaxies show lobes of synchrotron radiation rushing away from their core. The goliath galaxy M87, which sits at the center of the Virgo Supercluster, is intense in radio. Scientists saw that plasma was streaming away from its core, and they think a titanic black hole prowls there. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(More active than radio galaxies are the Seyfert galaxies, first scrutinized by Carl Seyfert in 1943. These show active nuclei churning in the center. They provide a link between normal galaxies and quasars. For many years, quasars were mistaken for ordinary stars. However, when astronomers viewed their spectra, they were shocked because these \"stars\" showed no identifiable absorption lines. Then, Maarten Schmidt realized that quasars are such powerful radio emitters because, although they radiate in visible and ultra-violet light, the waves are red shifted down to radio. This implies that they recede at enormous velocities and, thus, by Hubble\'s law, must be very distant. Since quasars are so bright and so far away, they must produce prodigious amounts of energy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Cosmological Radio Astronomy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Cosmological Radio Astronomy\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph( When the Big Bang theory was formulated in the 1940\'s, it predicted that primordial radiation would be red shifted to radio. This prediction was forgotten until 1964 when two physicists working from Bell labs were trying to eliminate an annoying radio hiss in communication. Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson had floundered upon the cosmic background radiation. Nicknamed the \"Echo of the Big Bang,\" this radiation seemed to come from every point in the sky. No fluctuations were found until recently when the COBE (Cosmic Background Radiation Explorer) satellite detected a 1/100,000 change in its strength. Finding the cosmic background radiation has solidified the Big Bang theory, making it the most accepted model of how the universe formed.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radio Galaxies)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Normal looking galaxies that have radio-emitting clouds shooting away from their nuclei. Energy from the nucleus must power these clouds to produce synchrotron radiation. One theory says that a black hole ejects particles from the galaxy, and, as the particles slam into gas surrounding the galaxy, they emit radio waves.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radio Source)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Any natural source of radio waves. Objects like the sun, supernova remnants, radio galaxies, and quasars blaze in the radio region of the electromagnetic spectrum. Astronomers think these sources reveal violent explosions, powerful magnetic fields, or gas whipping around a pulsar or black hole.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Radio Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(One or more antenna, usually parabolic, used to receive faint radio signals from outer space. Because radio waves are millions of times longer than visible light, much larger telescopes must be used to obtain comparable resolution. However, radio telescopes need not be shaped as precisely as optical telescopes, and some use wire mesh to focus the waves. Radio telescopes are the backbone of radio astronomy, a science that has existed since the 1940\'s)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Grote Reber built the first true radio telescope. With it, he found many bright radio sources. Although he published his findings in 1940, he was ignored by most astronomers. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Then, World War II left astronomers with a surplus of radio equipment. Using left-over parts, astronomers constructed the first radio telescopes, which looked like fields of clotheslines strung together with chicken wire. They relied on Earth\'s rotation to sweep the sky. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(The 1950\'s and 60\'s saw large steerable telescopes springing up around the world. Green Bank, West Virginia was the capitol of radio telescope development in the United States, while, in England, it centered on Jodrell Bank. The mammoth steel skeletons of radio telescopes grew up to 250 feet (80 m) wide. In the 1970\'s, there was a trend in radio telescopes towards collections of smaller telescopes networked together. The largest and most sensitive is the Very Large Array (VLA), crossing the New Mexican desert near Sorroco.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(In order to achieve fine resolution, radio astronomers now use Very Long Baseline Interferometry (VLBI), a network of ten antennas stretching from Hawaii to northeast Canada to the West Indies, to combine images from two or more telescopes that are separated by thousands of miles with a computer and to yield resolutions unheard of in the 1960\'s. The arrangement of the VLBI allows its performance to mimic that of a single receiver 8,000 miles (13,300 km) across and gives a resolution of 0.2 milliseconds of arc.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Ranger)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_double_emphasis(Ranger)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ranger\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Series of nine American probes sent to photograph the moon between 1961 and 1965.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_header(Red Dwarf)
[2026-10-18 20:36:00] render_paragraph(Smallest main sequence star and probably the most common type of star. Astronomers think that about 80% of all stars are red dwarfs. Red dwarfs have a cool surface (by star standards), and, since they fuse their nuclear fuel slowly, these stars shine for up to 50 billion years. Because of their small size and cool temperature (2,000 to 3,000\xc2\xb0 K), their luminosity is just 5% to .01% that of the sun. Consequently, astronomers can see red dwarfs only to a distance of 100 lightyears.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Red Giant)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(One of the last stages in a star\'s life cycle, when the core of a main sequence star has depleted its hydrogen and collapses. After the collapse, the core grows hot enough to fuse helium into carbon. Energy radiates away from the core and causes the outer layers of the star to swell to 10 to 100 times the width of our sun. These stars cool to a surface temperature of 2,000 to 3,000\xc2\xb0 K and shine as brightly as 100 suns. Betelgeuse, Arcturus, and Aldebaran are examples of red giants.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Red Shift)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Lengthening of a wavelength of light due to an object speeding away from an onlooker. If a luminous body recedes, its color will redden because the light waves stretch while they travel to your eye. Red shifting of light is similar to the lowering of pitch you hear as a wailing siren speeds away. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(By studying red shifts, astronomers have deduced that the universe is expanding. Galaxies show recession in their spectra, with the most distant galaxies receding the fastest. Hubbell\'s law defines how the red-shift increases with distance.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Reflecting Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Common type of telescope that uses a bowl-shaped mirror to gather and focus light. Since it is easier to grind a large mirror than a large lens and since mirrors do not suffer from chromatic aberration, most modern telescopes are reflectors instead of refractors.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronomers use several kinds of reflectors. The simplest is the Newtonian. It relies on a parabolic mirror to collect light and focuses it into an eyepiece with a tilted flat mirror. A long tube supports the telescope on its mount.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A second kind of reflector is the Schmidt-Cassegrain. Light enters the front plate and bounces off the spherical mirror. It then springs off a second mirror mounted at the front and proceeds through a hole in the main mirror and enters the eyepiece. Many amateurs use Schmidt-Cassegrains because of their small size and portability.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(World\'s Largest Reflecting Telescopes)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100World\'s Largest Reflecting Telescopes\x01\x0e-0100)
