[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Reflection Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Nebula.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Nebula.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Refracting Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Type of telescope that relies on a large lens to view heavenly bodies. Since high-quality glass must be ground in constructing these expensive telescopes and since they suffer chromatic aberration and sag under their own weight, no major observatory has ordered a refractor for fifty years. The largest, using a 40-inch (102 cm) lens, still operates at Yerkes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Refraction)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The bending of light. Some colors or wavelengths are bent more than others.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Regulus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Brightest star in Leo. Called the King star by Copernicus, it blazes blue-white at magnitude 1.36. Regulus is 3.8 times as wide as the sun, and 84 lightyears away. Regulus has a surface temperature of 36,000\xc2\xb0 F (20,000\xc2\xb0 C). Two companions, shining at 7.64 and 13 magnitudes, orbit Regulus.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Relativity)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(History-altering theory describing time and space and how motion and gravity affect them, that was proposed by Albert Einstein. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In 1905, Einstein published his Special Theory of Relativity, which postulates that all observers will measure the same value for the speed of light, no matter how fast they travel. He deduced that time must flow differently for different observers, and that two observers, one stationary and one moving, will measure the same event differently. Following from this is the idea that the time observed by people whose speed approaches that of light will slow down. If it did not, they would obtain a smaller value for the speed of light than people standing still would. (If you are standing by the side of a road, a truck appears to be going 55. If you are driving along next to the truck, it appears to be stationary.) Also, as a person\'s speed approaches that of light, they (and all objects with them) will become shorter. This also insures that the speed of light remains constant. If you are standing still and holding up a meter stick and tell your brother to run from one end to the other, he will run one meter. If you are running and holding a meter stick and tell your brother to run from one end to the other, he will have run more than one meter. If you shorten the meter by the right amount, he will still have just run a meter even though he had to catch up to you to reach the end of the meter stick. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Einstein\'s famous equation E=mc^(2) (Energy equals mass times the speed of light squared) guided physicists to reason how stars shine. It also led them to construct nuclear power plants and atomic bombs.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In 1915, Einstein published his General Theory of Relativity, which describes gravity as a consequence of mass. Einstein wrote that mass distorts space and time, creating gravity. Imagine placing a baseball on a trampoline. The baseball will stretch the fabric like a mass will stretch space and time. General relativity also predicts that light will traverse these curved lines and fall towards a massive body. This was confirmed in 1919 when, during a total eclipse, Sir Arthur Eddington photographed starlight grazing the surface of the sun. The beam curved exactly as relativity predicted. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In most circumstances, the predicting power of Newton\'s Laws work very well. Many physicists confess they prefer Newton\'s simpler principles to Einstein\'s relativity. However, when gravity is powerful, only relativity successfully operates. Also, it predicts such weird bodies as black holes. Thus, relativity is the most accepted theory describing space and time.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Resolution)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Telescope\'s power to distinguish two close objects. There is, however, a limit to how well an Earthbound telescope can resolve heavenly bodies. The atmosphere blurs images and constrains resolution. Orbiting above the atmosphere, the Hubble Space Telescope can make out fine detail.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Recticulum)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Recticulum (The Net))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Recticulum (The Net)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Small faint southern constellation.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Retrograde Motion)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Movement of a body from east to west, backwards from the normal orbits of planets. Some retrograde motion is just apparent. For example, Mars appears to move backward for a time as the Earth\'s orbit catches up with it, but Mars does not really move backward. To picture this type of retrograde motion imagine driving down the highway and passing a large truck. If you watch the truck as you go by, it appears to back up. This happens because you are going forward at a faster speed.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Some masses in the solar system show true retrograde motion. Several moons of Jupiter and Saturn along with Neptune\'s moon Triton, have retrograde orbits. Venus spins west to east, exhibiting retrograde rotation. Scientists have little understanding of why these bodies orbit or rotate backwards.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rhea)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Saturn\'s second-largest Moon. In 1672, Giovanni Cassini discovered Rhea, which is the sixth-closest moon to Saturn and completes an orbit every 4.5 days. Rhea measures 949 miles (1,530 km) across, and its average distance from Saturn is 326,765 miles (527,040). Rhea keeps its icy, cratered side facing Saturn and its dark side away from Saturn as it orbits.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rigel)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Brightest star in Orion and seventh-brightest in the sky. This brilliant blue supergiant blazes at magnitude 0.08, is 78 times wider than the sun, and lies 850 lightyears away. Rigel has several companions. One shines at magnitude 7.7, and the other is a spectroscopic binary with a period of 9.9 days.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Right Ascension)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Celestial equivalent of longitude. Starting at the Point in Aries, it runs east to west and is measured in hours, minutes, and seconds, ranging from 0 hours to 24 hours.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rigil Kent)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Alpha Centauri.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Alpha Centauri.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rigil Kentaurus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Rigil Kentaurus (Foot of the Centaur))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Rigil Kentaurus (Foot of the Centaur)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Arabic name for Alpha Centauri.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Ring Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Ring Nebula (M57))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ring Nebula (M57)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Bright planetary nebula in Lyra. It is 0.66 lightyears across and lies 2,000 lightyears away. Judging from its current expansion rate of 11.6 miles (19 km) per second, astronomers think the nebula is about 5,500 years old.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Roche\'s Limit or Roche Limit)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Smallest distance at which a natural satellite can orbit without being destroyed by tidal forces. For a planet and moon of similar composition, this distance is 2.5 times the radius of the planet..)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Tidal forces tug at a mass when there is a difference between how strongly the near face is pulled compared with the far face. Meteorites and artificial satellites are small enough that tidal differences do not play a significant role in their demise. For larger bodies, like the moon, the Roche limit defines where tidal forces become so strong that they will destroy the satellite. The moon Io sits just outside Jupiter\'s Roche limit. Tidal tugs between Jupiter and Europa stretch and strain Io until volcanoes erupt on its surface. Saturn\'s rings reside within its Roche limit. Roche\'s limit is named for the French astronomer, Edouard Roche (1820-1883) who calculated its existence in 1848.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rocket)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Machine that gives rapid acceleration to propel people and/or equipment, usually into outer space. Hot gases rush out of a nozzle to provide thrust. Rockets of today burn chemical propellant that is either solid or liquid. Both have their advantages. Solid fuel is easy to handle and can be stored in rockets forever. However, once ignited, it is difficult to control. The rocket must consume all of its solid fuel before shutting down. Liquid fuel, on the other hand, is much easier to guide. Valves in the engine open and restrict the flow and direct the burn. However, liquid must be pumped into the rocket before launch and maintained at a low temperature to keep it stable.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Early History)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Early History\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The world\'s first rockets were fireworks used by the Chinese in the mid 1200\'s. Later, they built rockets with explosive tips as weapons. This idea quickly spread. In India, soldiers shot these missiles at invading British forces, and they were so effective that artillery officer William Congreve introduced them to England. Later European wars were fought with weapons that looked like big bottle rockets. They were guided by sticks poking out of their back. English inventor William Hale then replaced the sticks with curved fins that made the rocket spin like a football. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Aeronautical Rockets)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Aeronautical Rockets\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The mid 1800\'s saw writers fantasizing about space travel. These novels inspired amateurs to experiment with large rocket designs, but the true founder of modern rocketry was Konstantin Tsiolkovsky. This Russian theoretician derived the laws of rocket flight that are still used today. He also realized that liquid fuel is better than solid and drew plans for multi-stage boosters.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The task of building the first liquid-fueled rocket fell on American, Robert Goddard, who launched it in 1926. At the same time, the German Society for Space Travel pushed for further advances in booster technology. Werner Von Braun emerged at the forefront of rocket design as his team assembled the V-2, the progenitor of modern ballistic missiles. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Rockets of the Space Age)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Rockets of the Space Age\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(After World War II, Von Braun\'s team surrendered and continued their research in White Sands, New Mexico, where they developed the Redstone missile. Fueled by the space race, the US and USSR embarked on a marathon to build mightier rockets. When the USSR shot its first inter-continental missile, the US responded with the Atlas, Titan and Thor boosters that hurled numerous satellites and early capsules into orbit. When President Kennedy challenged the USSR to a race to the moon, both nations scrambled to erect a super-booster to answer the call. The Russian program produced the Proton rocket, which is still used to launch heavy payloads. The Americans unveiled the Saturn V which reliably launched every Apollo mission to the moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Today, reusable crafts have largely replaced traditional rockets. The Space Shuttle, for example, relies on a pair of solid-fuel rockets during the first part of its launch. Later these drop away and the shuttle ignites liquid fuel. Because of its large payload bay, the space shuttle can carry multiple satellites into orbit at one time instead of firing each with a separate rocket.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Rockets of the Future)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Rockets of the Future\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Scientists are working on rockets that do not depend on chemical propellants. On Earth, the rapid acceleration provided by chemical fuel is required to escape gravity, but in space, less potent methods work well. Engineers pondered using electricity to heat escaping ions. A better idea still is harnessing nuclear fusion as an energy source. Fusion engines could drive interplanetary and even interstellar journeys.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Roemer, Ole or Olaus or Olaf)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Roemer, Ole or Olaus or Olaf (1644-1710))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Roemer, Ole or Olaus or Olaf (1644-1710)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Danish astronomer who, in 1676, first successfully determined the speed of light. In 1672, while staff astronomer at the Paris observatory, Roemer observed that eclipse times for Jupiter\'s moons were sometimes shorter and sometimes longer than calculated. He thought that the differences arose from changing distances between Jupiter and Earth, which forced the light to travel different distances. From this, he calculated light to travel 140,000 miles (225,000 km) per second. Despite this being smaller than today\'s value, it represents the first time anyone thought of the speed of light as finite.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rosette Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Rosette Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Rosette Nebula\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Large wispy nebula in the constellation Monoceros. Astronomers think this nebula is about 500,000 years old. It lies 4,500 lightyears away.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Rotation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Spinning of a celestial object around an axis. The Earth, for example, rotates about the imaginary line drawn between the two poles every 24 hours.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Royal Astronomical Society)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Royal Astronomical Society (RAS))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Royal Astronomical Society (RAS)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(British organization of professional astronomers and geophysicists. It was founded in 1820 in London by John Hershel and received its royal charter in 1831. During the later 1800\'s, many of its members were famous amateur astronomers. Now the organization organizes meetings and publishes research journals.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Royal Greenwich Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Britain\'s oldest scientific institution, established in 1675 by King Charles II. It was built in Greenwich, England to make observations of the moon and chart stars to aid navigators. Its directors bore the title Astronomer Royal. This however, was changed in 1972 because the duties of the director were separated from those of the Astronomer Royal. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The Greenwich observatory later devoted its energy to tracking time. Greenwich mean time became the standard for the world of astronomy. Also, in 1884, Greenwich\'s meridian became the prime meridian from which all other longitudes are measured. Because of smog and light pollution, the observatory was moved to Sussex in 1958. It now houses the 98-inch (250 cm) Isaac Newton reflector.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(RR Lyrae Variables)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Stars showing a short (1 hour to 1 day) cycle of varying brightness. The RR Lyrae phase of stellar evolution occurs in population II (old) stars and last about 80 million years. As with Cepheid variable stars, the real brightness or luminosity can be obtained from their cycle of varying brightness. Since the range of luminosity for RR Lyrae variables is small, their real brightness can be compared to their apparent brightness, which can then used to calculate their distance. In 1920, Harlow Shapley used RR Lyrae variables to map Earth\'s position in the galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Russian 236 Inch Reflecting Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Russian 236 Inch Reflecting Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Russian 236 Inch Reflecting Telescope\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Located in Russia\'s Caucasus Mountains at an altitude of 6,800 feet (2,070 m), this 236 inch (600 cm) reflecting telescope was the largest in the world for more than a decade.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sagan, Carl Edward)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Sagan, Carl Edward (b. 1934))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Sagan, Carl Edward (b. 1934)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(American astronomer and biologist who has worked to popularize astronomy and promote the possibility of finding life elsewhere in the universe. He also, by showing that a mixture of ammonia, hydrogen sulfide, methane, and water produces amino acids when hit by sunlight, demonstrated a possible beginning for life on Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sagitta)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Sagitta (The Arrow))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Sagitta (The Arrow)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Small northern constellation between Vulpecula and Aquila that depicts an arrow shot by Hercules towards Vega. Despite being a small dim constellation, it was one of the forty-eight constellations listed by Ptolemy over 2,000 years ago. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sagittarius)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Sagittarius (The Archer))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Sagittarius (The Archer)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Southern zodiacal constellation lying near Corona Australis and depicting a half-man-half-horse shooting an arrow. This teapot-shaped constellation, best viewed during the summer, contains the Lagoon nebula (M8), the Omega nebula (M17), and the Trifid nebula (M20). It also contains the radio source Sagittarius A, thought to mark the center of our galaxy. The sun reaches its southernmost point while in Sagittarius around December 22 (the winter solstice).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sagittarius A)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Name of a bright radio source thought to be the center of the Milky Way. It was the origin of the radio static that led Karl Jansky to pioneer radio astronomy in the 1930\'s.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sagittarius Arm)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Spiral arm of the Milky Way that lies between the sun and the galactic core.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Salyut)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Salyut)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Salyut\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Series of Soviet space stations, beginning in 1971, built from three cylinders 6.5 feet (2m), 10 feet (3 m) long and 13.5 feet (4 m) wide. The space station measures 39 feet (12 m) in length and was launched by the Soviet Proton booster. The Salyut missions had equipment for astronomical and biological research. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Saros)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Cycle of 6,585.3 days (18 years 11.3 days) during which the sun, moon, and Earth return to the same relative positions and eclipses repeat themselves.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Satellite)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Any smaller body orbiting a larger body. Satellites can be natural, like the moon, or artificial, like the space shuttle. The first artificial satellite, launched October 4, 1957, was the Soviet craft Sputnik I. The first American satellite was Explorer I, launched on January 31, 1958.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Satellites of the planets)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Satellites of the planets\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(See under separate planets and the names of their individual satellites.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Saturn)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Sixth planet from the sun, famous for its bright rings. Saturn is a gaseous ball of mostly hydrogen and helium. Due to its rapid differential rotation of 10 hours 14 minutes at the equator and 10 hours 40 minutes nearer the poles, Saturn assumes a flattened shape (with an equatorial diameter of 74,975 miles or 120,660 km and a polar diameter of 66,960 miles or 108,000 km). Saturn\'s average radius is 34,390 miles (57,316 km). At an average distance of 886,703,000 miles (1,427,010,000 km) from the sun, Saturn takes 29.5 years to complete an orbit. If you wanted to orbit Saturn without being pulled in by its gravity, you would have to travel 21.6 miles (36.0 km) per second.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Saturn and Jupiter may have furthered life on Earth. Currently, a large comet hits the Earth approximately once every 100 million years. If Saturn and Jupiter were not there to deflect comets from Earth, Earth would be hit by a large comet roughly every 100,000 years. These large comets would likely cause the extinction of whatever lives on Earth, preventing evolution.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Structure)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Structure\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Saturn looks yellow-white through a telescope. Through a medium-sized telescope, Saturn\'s belts, which have much less detail than Jupiter\'s, can be seen. The colors on Saturn are less distinct than those on Jupiter because there is less ammonia in Saturn\'s upper atmosphere. Because of Saturn\'s great distance from the sun, its cloud tops are a frigid -250\xc2\xb0 F (-170\xc2\xb0 C). Consequently, there are few bursts of activity as appear on Jupiter, although light and dark spots arise every few months in the cloud tops. The largest eruption to occur recently was in 1991 when the whole equatorial region brightened. Large storms appear to occur on Saturn roughly every 27 to 30 years and to last a few years. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Saturn\'s atmosphere is so cold that the ammonia crystallizes into clouds deeper in the atmosphere. The outer atmosphere consists of hydrogen, helium, and methane. Astronomers think Saturn has a solid iron and rock core that is 8,060 miles (13,000 km) to 8,680 miles (14,000 km) wide and surrounded by miles of ice and metallic hydrogen. At the center, pressures reach 50 million atmospheres, and temperatures climb to 27,000 F (15,000 C). Like Jupiter, Saturn radiates more heat than it receives from the sun. This may either be heat left over from formation or heat produced as the planet contracts. It might also come from \"helium rain.\" This is caused by droplets of helium descending through the atmosphere and releaseing heat. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Saturn\'s famous rings are the most beautiful in the solar system. They look like a solid sheet but are actually composed of millions of individual particles circling Saturn\'s equator. The rings measure 170,000 miles (275,000 km) from rim to rim but are only 1 mile (1.6 km) thick. The rings show a dark space, called the Cassini Division, that is due to the tidal tug of Saturn\'s moon Mimas. Another division in the rings, the Encke division, seems to have a moon orbiting in it. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronomers reported odd shapes on each side of Saturn when they first trained telescopes on the planet, but they could not resolve what they were. Galileo called them \"ears\" when he saw them through his primitive telescope. It was Christiaan Huygens who, in 1655, first realized these were rings. He also observed that, as Saturn orbits the sun, we view the rings at different angles. It is like watching a Frisbee in its flight. First you see its top, then you view it edge-on, and finally you observe its under-side. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Although Cassini postulated that the rings were composed of countless satellites in the 1600\'s, it was only in 1859 that astronomers demonstrated that Saturn\'s rings could not be solid sheets of material. In 1895 American astronomer James Keeler discovered that different parts of the rings showed different Doppler shifts, indicating that they were traveling at different speeds. Then, when Saturn occulted a star, starlight was seen passing through the rings, once more showing that they were not solid. The starlight also showed that some of the particles are more clumped together than others, so astronomers have given letters to the different relative types of rings. The A and B rings are the two large rings that are separated by the Cassini division. The D ring, which is inside the C ring, is the closest to the planet and contains so few particles that one can actually see Saturn through it. The rings are composed primarily of ice particles which give the rings an albedo of 0.70, which is even greater than that of Saturn.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Moons of Saturn)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons of Saturn\x01\x0e-0100)
