[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Here nine of Saturn\'s present disks large enough to be distinguished as disks. The rings are made of tiny chunks of ice.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Here nine of Saturn\'s present disks large enough to be distinguished as disks. The rings are made of tiny chunks of ice.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Saturn Rockets)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Set of powerful rockets that were used for human spaceflight during the Apollo program. Saturn 1 was the first rocket the US developed specifically for space flight.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Saturn V)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Rocket that propelled the Apollo missions to the moon. This colossal rocket stood 363 feet (111 meters) high and weighed 2,850 tons. Designed in 1961, the Saturn V had three stages. The rocket could project a 150-ton payload into Earth\'s orbit or send a 50-ton payload to the Moon. This, the most powerful machine ever built, was first tested in November 9, 1967 by sending a dummy command module into orbit. The rocket delivered 7,600,000 pounds (3,450,000 kilograms) of thrust, which hurled all lunar missions to the moon and carried Skylab into orbit. The Saturn V\'s were launched from complex 39 at Cape Canaveral, the same pads that launch the Space Shuttle today.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Scattering)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Rayleigh Scattering)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Spreading and redirecting of one particle by collision with another. A notable example of scattering is in the Earth\'s atmosphere. This is called \x01\x0e+0200Rayleigh Scattering\x01\x0e-0200 because it was first explained by English physicist Lord Rayleigh (1842-1919). The sky appears blue because molecules and dust in Earth\'s atmosphere scatter blue light more than red.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Schiaparelli, Giovanni Virginio)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Schiaparelli, Giovanni Virginio (1835-1910))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Schiaparelli, Giovanni Virginio (1835-1910)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(canali)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Italian astronomer who, in 1877, first noted lines on the surface of Mars. He named them \x01\x0e+0200canali\x01\x0e-0200, Italian for channels. The name was mistranslated into English as canals, which caused many people to think that they were built by intelligent beings. This misnomer inspired Percival Lowell to formulate the notion that a dying race inhabited Mars and built canals to carry water from the poles to the equator. Schiaparalli, who did not believe Lowell\'s theories, continued to map Mars and name its surface features. He also observed Mercury and Venus and thought that they always presented the same face to the sun, which recently was disproved. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Schiaparelli is most known for discovering that comets leave meteor showers in their wake. He explained that the comet 1861 III caused the Perseid meteor shower and that comet 1866I caused the Leonid meteor shower. His theory predicted the Andromedids after a comet disintegrated as it approached the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Schirra, Walter Marty, Jr.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Schirra, Walter Marty, Jr. (b. 1923))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Schirra, Walter Marty, Jr. (b. 1923)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Only astronaut to fly in Mercury, Gemini, and Apollo missions. He was one of the original 7 Mercury astronauts chosen in 1959.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Schmidt, Maarten)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Schmidt, Maarten (b. 1929))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Schmidt, Maarten (b. 1929)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Dutch-born American Astronomer who first realized that quasars are strong radio emitters because of their enormous red shift.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Schmidt Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Reflecting telescope, invented by Estonian optician Bernard Voldemar Schmidt (1879-1935), that produces quality wide-angle images. Often coupled with a camera, these telescopes use a corrector plate and a spherical (instead of parabolic) mirror to gather light. These telescopes have gained popularity among both amateur and professional astronomers.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Schwarzschild, Karl)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Schwarzschild, Karl (1873-1916))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Schwarzschild, Karl (1873-1916)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Schwarzschild Radius,)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(German astronomer known for his work on the structure and transport of heat from the interior of stars. He is most famous for solving Einstein\'s relativity equations while on his deathbed. His solution for a black hole, whose radius bears his name, says that, when matter is compressed into its \x01\x0e+0200Schwarzschild Radius,\x01\x0e-0200 it collapses to form a black hole.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Schwarzschild Radius)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Radius of the event horizon in a black hole. When light or matter passes the Schwarzschild Radius, it cannot escape the gravitational pull of the dense body because, within this radius, the escape velocity exceeds the speed of light. Since Einstein\'s theory of relativity forbids matter from attaining the speed of light, if a body has an escape velocity greater than the speed of light, no matter can ever escape its gravitational hold.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The Schwarzschild Radius of the sun is only about 2 miles (3 km), while for the Earth it is only about 1 inch (3 cm). This means that the Earth would have to be compressed into a 1 inch ball. A star\'s core must be squeezed into its Schwarzschild Radius during a supernova explosion to turn into a black hole.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Scintillation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Twinkling of a star caused by currents in the Earth\'s atmosphere. As starlight shines through the atmosphere, it is refracted by particles in the Earth\'s turbulent atmosphere. Scintillation mostly occurs in the bottom 5 miles (8 km) of the atmosphere and causes bad viewing because it prevents an astronomer from being able to resolve an image. To fight scintillation, astronomers have built observatories on mountain peaks, above the turbulent layers of the atmosphere. The best spot in the world is Mauna Kea peak in Hawaii, which, at 13,000 ft above sea level, is above most of the Earth\'s atmosphere. Other ways to fight scintillation are to send rockets above the atmosphere or to build observatories above water.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Scorpius)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Scorpius (The Scorpion))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Scorpius (The Scorpion)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Bright zodiacal summer constellation that lies opposite Orion. Scorpius contains many interesting objects, like the triple star Beta Scorpii and the doubles Mu Scorpii and Omega Scorpii. The constellation has the brightest X-ray source, Sco X-1. Star clusters such as the globular cluster M4 and the open cluster M6 populate this region. At the center lies Antares, its brightest star. With Antares at its heart, Scorpius portrays the scorpion that stung and killed Orion.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Scott, David Randolph)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Scott, David Randolph (b. 1932))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Scott, David Randolph (b. 1932)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Commander of the Apollo 15 mission. He, and fellow astronaut James Benson Irwin, explored the moon for 18 hours and traveled 17 miles (27 km) in a lunar rover. They stayed on the moon for 2.75 days. During this time, Scott dropped a hammer and a feather and showed that, without air resistance, both reach the surface at the same time.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sculptor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Sculptor (The Sculptor))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Sculptor (The Sculptor)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Faint southern constellation that lies south of Cetus and contains the galactic south pole (the point at 90\xc2\xb0 south latitude) and a dwarf galaxy in the Local Group.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Scutum)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Scutum (The Shield))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Scutum (The Shield)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Faint southern constellation between Aquila and Serpens that is best viewed during summer. Scutum holds about 200 stars and the magnificent cluster M11.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Scutum Star Cloud)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Scutum)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Scutum\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Selenology)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Study of the moon\'s surface; similar to geology on Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Serpens)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Serpens (The Serpent))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Serpens (The Serpent)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Constellation riding the celestial equator that represents the snake being held by Ophiuchus. This constellation, which is best observed during the spring and summer, has two parts: Serpens Caput (the head) and Serpens Cauda (the tail). The double star Delta Serpenis, the bright globular clusters M5 (NGC5904) and M16 (NGC 6611) are all found in Serpens. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Seti)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(SETI)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100SETI\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence. This project, directed by NASA and privately funded, uses radio telescopes to listen for signals from alien civilizations. The main computer analyzes 8 million radio frequencies, searching for signals that cannot be of natural origin.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sextans)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Sextans (The Sextant))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Sextans (The Sextant)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Faint southern constellation located between Leo and Hydra that is visible during the spring.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Seyfert Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Galaxy with a small active nucleus. Such galaxies expel clouds of gas around their core, indicating something exploded there in the past. These galaxies were studied by Carl K. Seyfert, and about 100 such galaxies are now known. About 1% of spiral galaxies have been identified as Seyfert galaxies.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A typical Seyfert galaxy holds a small nucleus that emits blue, ultraviolet, and radio radiation. These cores, called \"mini quasars,\" may furnish a link between normal galaxies and quasars. Changes in light from the cores that occur over a period of months may be due to electrons spiraling along magnetic fields (synchrotron radiation).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Shapley, Harlow)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Shapley, Harlow (1885-1972))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Shapley, Harlow (1885-1972)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(American astronomer who discovered that the Milky Way is far larger than was thought and that the sun is not located near its center. Shapley determined this by surveying Cepheid variables in globular clusters. He surmised that the sun sits about 50,000 lightyears from the center of the Milky Way. However, later he found that dust dimmed galactic starlight, and he calculated the Milky Way to be about 100,000 lightyears across and the sun to lie 30,000 lightyears from its center. This value is still accepted today.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Shepard, Alan Bartlett)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Shepard, Alan Bartlett (b.1923))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Shepard, Alan Bartlett (b.1923)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Freedom 7)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(First American launched into space. He rode \x01\x0e+0200Freedom 7\x01\x0e-0200 in a suborbital shot that lasted a little over 15 minutes, carrying him to an altitude of 116.5 miles (187.5 km). In February 1971, he commanded Apollo 14. Shepard and Edgar Dean Mitchell made two moonwalks totaling 9.25 hours. They explored the Cone crater to collect samples. Shepard retired from spaceflight in 1974.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Shooting Star)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Common name for a meteor. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sidereal Day)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Sidereal day)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Sidereal day\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time it takes the Earth to complete one rotation with respect to any fixed star. Astronomers once thought the sidereal day was constant but now know that it lengthens by about 0.003 seconds every year because the Earth\'s rotation rate is slowing down. The mean sidereal day is 23 hours, 56 minutes, and 4.1 seconds long.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sidereal Period)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time it takes an object to orbit its parent body with respect to background stars. For planets, this is also called their year. It differs from what is observed from Earth because the Earth is also moving. The moon, for example, has a sidereal period of 27.32 days, and cycles through phases in 29.53 days. The cycle of phases is named the apparent or synodic period.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sidereal Year)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time it takes Earth to orbit the sun with respect to the background stars. This has been clocked at 365.26 solar days or 366.26 sidereal days.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Siderite)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Iron meteorite. Siderites have an iron-nickel mixture with traces of carbon, cobalt, copper, and phosphorus. Siderites are solid masses with a density about 7.8 times that of water. They are classified according to their nickel content: those with 5-6 percent nickel are hexahedrites, those with 6-20 percent nickel are octahedrites, and those with more than 20 percent nickel are ataxites. All iron meteorites form inside their parent asteroid and slowly crystallize. Siderites possibly formed in asteroids that collided and shattered between 1 million to 1 hundred thousand years ago.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Siderolite)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Meteorite that has a roughly equal mixture of iron and stony minerals.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Siding Spring Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Australian observatory located 3,822 feet (1,165 m) above sea level. It provides a majestic view of the southern sky and is located 18 miles (28 km) from Cooabarabran, New South Wales.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Singularity)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Theory that predicts matter can be crushed into a point with zero volume. All of the mass of a black hole is concentrated at this point, forcing space and time to curve back on itself. Also, scientists believe all the mass in the universe was at one time contained in singularity before the Big Bang. The known laws of physics do not apply at singularity.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sirius)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Brightest star in the night sky, also known as the Dog Star. It twinkles at -1.47 magnitude and at a distance of 8.7 lightyears, it is the fifth closest star to the sun. Sirius has a surface temperature of 20,000 F (11,000 C). Located in Canis Major, Sirius is 1.76 times as wide as the sun and has 2.32 times the sun\'s mass. Blazing bright white, it shines 23 times more brightly than the sun. Sirius also has a white dwarf companion of magnitude 8.4. The two stars orbit each other approximately every 50 years. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Many ancient cultures used Sirius to track time. The Egyptians, for example, waited for Sirius to rise. It signaled the annual flood of the Nile.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Skylab)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(First American space station. Skylab was launched on May 14, 1973 into a 270 mile (435 km) near-circular orbit. About one minute into the flight, Skylab\'s micrometeoroid shield launched early and was destroyed. The shield was painted white and would have protected the station from over-heating. The first crew saved Skylab by attaching another cover. They also fixed a jammed solar panel. Skylab had four sections and housed three crews, the last staying a record 84 days. In June 1978, solar flares heated and expanded the atmosphere, dragging Skylab out of orbit. In 1979 it plunged into the Indian Ocean.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Apex)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Point in the sky toward which the sun is traveling; similar to other stars\' proper motions. William Hershel first tried to calculate which direction the sun was traveling relative to other stars by measuring their radial velocity and proper motion. He thought the sun was moving toward Hercules, but today astronomers think the sun drifts in the direction of Lyra at about 12 miles (20 km) per second. Opposite the solar apex sits the antapex, away from which the sun is moving.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Constant)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Amount of heat striking a theoretical surface that is perpendicular to the sun\'s rays and just outside the Earth\'s atmosphere. The solar constant was first assessed by American astrophysicist Charles Greeley Abbot (1872-1973). When the Earth sits at its average distance from the sun, the accepted value is 2 calories per minute per square centimeter. This equals about 1.3 kilowatts per square meter. The solar constant fluctuates by about one or two percent due to varying solar activity.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Cycle or Sunspot Cycle)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Eleven year period in which sunspot activity seems to repeat a maximum and minimum. Heinrich Schwabe first proposed the solar rhythm in 1843, saying it was a 10 year cycle. Later, scientists found its average length to be closer to 11 years, but the cycle can last from 7 to 17 years. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(F.G.W. Sporer found that, near the beginning of a new cycle, sunspots emerge in the middle latitudes north or south of the solar equator. Later, spots move closer to the equator, becoming more abundant over 4.5 years. Then, during the next 6.5 years, the spots decline. Near the end of a cycle, old spots are seen at the equator while, at higher latitudes, new spots coalesce.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Sunspot activity may affect other storms on the sun\'s surface. Violent explosions like flares and prominence follow a similar 11 year cycle. They may be governed by the sun\'s magnetic field, which flips direction every sunspot cycle and has its own 22-year rhythm. Solar wind and action in the chromosphere and corona erupt during solar maximums. The 11-year cycle may even affect the magnetic field of the Earth, which scientists have observed to follow an 11-year cycle. Some have even suggested that the 11-year cycle influences Earth\'s weather. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Day)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time it takes Earth to rotate once on its axis with respect to the sun. The average solar day is equal to 1.0027 sidereal days. The difference in times occurs because, while the Earth spins, it travels in its orbit around the sun. It must rotate an extra degree each day to present the same face to the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Eclipse\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar System)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Family of planets, asteroids, and comets that orbit the sun. The sun, containing over 99 percent of the mass of the solar system, sits in the center and commands its entourage as the sole source of light and heat. The sun dominates gravitational interactions out to about 2.4 lightyears from its center. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Scientists classify the planets into two groups: the terrestrials and the gas giants. The terrestrials (meaning Earth-like) are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars. These rocky bodies are smaller than gas giants and have solid surfaces. Astronomers think that, because terrestrial planets orbit closer to the sun, they were what remained after the sun swept lighter elements out of the inner solar system. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The gas giants are enormous balls of light gas. The layers of gas grow denser as you travel towards their cores. Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune are gas giants, but Pluto is not.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A wide gap, where the asteroid belt is found, lies between Mars and Jupiter. Asteroids, also called planetesimals, are chunks of rock ranging in size from 600 miles (960 km) to a few inches across. Although most asteroids reside in the main belt, smaller belts such as the Apollo group, Amor group, and the Trojan group also circle the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The final members of the sun\'s family are comets. They resemble a snowball the size of a small city. These bodies have long eccentric orbits that periodically carry them near the sun. During their visit, the sun vaporizes their surface, and they grow long wispy tails. Most comets fit into either short-term or long-term classifications. Short-termers visit the inner solar system relatively frequently. Halley\'s comet, with a period of 76 years, is considered a short term comet. They remain in the space marked by the farthest planets. Long-term comets have periods of up to 100,000 years. They shoot far away from the planets on a return visit to the Oort cloud, which is a spherical nebula of dust and ice left over from when the solar system formed.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Time)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time that is measured by the sun\'s position in the sky. Solar time is based on the position of the sun, while sidereal time is based on the position of the background stars. Sidereal time is much more accurate and is used in observatories.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Mean Solar time,)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Apparent solar time, as shown on a sundial, does not flow at a constant rate because the Earth\'s eccentric orbit and tilt cause variations. \x01\x0e+0200Mean Solar time,\x01\x0e-0200 which removes these variations, is the time registered on Earth clocks.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solar Wind)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Flood of atomic particles radiating from the sun\'s corona. These particles, mostly protons and electrons with a few helium nuclei, shoot away at 220 to 500 miles (350 to 800 km) per second. The solar wind averages 5 protons and 5 electrons per cubic centimeter as it rushes past Earth. It has long been thought to be the link between storms on the sun and changes in the Earth\'s magnetic field (magnetosphere). )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Scientists study the solar wind indirectly by the phenomena it causes. The solar wind blows back the tails of comets, for they always point away from the sun. It also makes distant radio sources twinkle. Solar wind also sweeps the Earth\'s magnetosphere into a comet shape and induces the Van Allen radiation belts. The magnetosphere fluctuates in a 27-day cycle responding to the sun\'s rotation. Like many other features of the sun, the solar wind follows the 11-year solar cycle and blows the strongest when the corona is most intense. When flares erupt, the solar wind strengthens. Particles then ride Earth\'s magnetic field lines and slam into the atmosphere, causing an aurora.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solstice)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time when the Earth\'s poles are tilted maximally towards the sun. For the north pole, this happens around June 22 (summer solstice) while for the south it occurs near December 22.(winter solstice). During the solstice, the sun sits farthest from the celestial equator, at 23.5\xc2\xb0 north or south.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Watching the sunrise on the winter solstice from within an ancient structure oriented for that purpose can be a moving experience. This is one of several such structures in Putnam County, New York.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Watching the sunrise on the winter solstice from within an ancient structure oriented for that purpose can be a moving experience. This is one of several such structures in Putnam County, New York.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solstice)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time when the Earth\'s poles are tilted maximally towards the sun. For the north pole, this happens around June 22 (summer solstice) while for the south it occurs near December 22.(winter solstice). During the solstice, the sun sits farthest from the celestial equator, at 23.5\xc2\xb0 north or south.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Watching the sunrise on the winter solstice from within an ancient structure oriented for that purpose can be a moving experience. This is one of several such structures in Putnam County, New York.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Watching the sunrise on the winter solstice from within an ancient structure oriented for that purpose can be a moving experience. This is one of several such structures in Putnam County, New York.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Solstice)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time when the Earth\'s poles are tilted maximally towards the sun. For the north pole, this happens around June 22 (summer solstice) while for the south it occurs near December 22.(winter solstice). During the solstice, the sun sits farthest from the celestial equator, at 23.5\xc2\xb0 north or south.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Watching the sunrise on the winter solstice from within an ancient structure oriented for that purpose can be a moving experience. This is one of several such structures in Putnam County, New York.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Watching the sunrise on the winter solstice from within an ancient structure oriented for that purpose can be a moving experience. This is one of several such structures in Putnam County, New York.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sombrero Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Located in the constellation Virgo, the Sombrero Galaxy, which looks like the hat for which it is named, is difficult to classify. From our view the Sombrero galaxy has a large center bulge surrounded by a ring of dust. But, since we see it edge on, it is difficult to determine if it is a spiral galaxy or a lenticular galaxy (a cross between a spiral and an elliptical galaxy). However, since H II regions, which are found in lenticular galaxies, have been found in the Sombrero galaxy, it has been classified as a spiral galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Southern Cross)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Crux.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Crux.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Soyuz)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Series of 40 Soviet crew-carrying spacecraft used for long-duration flights from 1967 to 1981. It had three compartments. At the front was a spherical command module, and behind that rode a cone-shaped section used during launch and landing. In the rear rode the service module that housed electrical systems and thrusters. The craft measured 21 feet (7 m) long, had 360 cubic feet (10.2 cubic m) of space, and weighed 14,750 pounds (6,690 kg). )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Soyuz was first built to carry three cosmonauts into orbit, but during the re-entry of Soyuz 11 in 1971 the capsule lost pressure and killed the crew. Since then, Soyuz has carried two suited cosmonauts, as there is not room for a third.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Space Probe)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Pioneer)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Viking)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Voyager)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Magellan)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Galileo)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Any robotic spacecraft sent to explore space or some planet or moon. The first space probe to visit the moon was the Soviet Luna 1 in 1959. The first space probes to visit another planet were the American Mariner series. The first probe to report from Mars was also of the American Mariner series, and after that came \x01\x0e+0200Pioneer\x01\x0e-0200 and \x01\x0e+0200Viking\x01\x0e-0200. The Soviet Venera was the first prope to successfully land on Venus. More recently, NASA launched two \x01\x0e+0200Voyager\x01\x0e-0200 probes to the outer planets, \x01\x0e+0200Magellan\x01\x0e-0200 to Venus, and \x01\x0e+0200Galileo\x01\x0e-0200 to Jupiter.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Space Shuttle)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Reusable spacecraft that is launched into space like a traditional rocket but then glides back to Earth like an airplane. The United States has the largest and most experienced shuttle program, but Russia and ESA plan a similar agenda. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The space shuttle consists of an orbiter about the size of a DC-10 that has a two-deck cabin and a payload bay. The cabin houses the astronauts at normal atmospheric pressure and has room for up to seven people. The payload bay resembles the fuselage of a jet liner with a roof that cranks open. It carries cargo such as satellites and experiments and has a 50 foot (15 meter) robotic arm to manipulate its freight. It can haul 65,000 pounds (29,500 kg) of equipment into orbit and bring 35,000 pounds (16,000 kg) back to Earth. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(To launch the orbiter, the shuttle relies on twin boosters flanking a huge external fuel tank. The two rockets burn solid fuel to thrust the orbiter for the first few minutes of its launch. They are then cast off and plunge into the Atlantic to be retrieved and used again.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The external fuel tank carries liquid hydrogen and oxygen to power the orbiter\'s three main engines. They burn for about 20 minutes to propel the orbiter into low Earth orbit. The external tank then falls back to Earth and disintegrates over the Indian Ocean. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Missions)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Missions\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The space shuttle performs three types of missions: satellite launching, satellite maintenance and repair, and microgravity experimentation. The large payload enables the shuttle to launch multiple satellites during one mission. Using the manipulator arm, it places weather, communication, and research satellites into low Earth orbit, or it provides a platform to launch them into higher geosychronous orbit. It can also retrieve malfunctioning satellites and repair them or carry them back to Earth. This is less expensive than building and launching a whole new satellite.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Spacelab)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(During a microgravity experimental mission, the cabin provides a workshop to carry out experiments in microgravity at normal atmosphere pressure. The payload bay provides the same conditions in a vacuum. Some missions do a variety of experiments, while others are devoted to one subject, like medicine, remote sensing, or astronomy. Sometimes, the payload bay houses \x01\x0e+0200Spacelab\x01\x0e-0200. It was built by ESA and is a large orbiting laboratory that was assembled as a prototype for future space stations. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The shuttle also carries \"getaway specials,\" which are self-contained experiments donated by schools, universities, and companies and fixed in the cargo bay. This service opens space up to populations that could never before afford it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Future Plans)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Future Plans\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Freedom)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Freedom,)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In the coming years, the space shuttle will be the lifeline to the planned space station \x01\x0e+0200Freedom\x01\x0e-0200. During Freedom\'s construction, the space shuttle will haul modules and materials, and, later, it will ferry people and supplies to and from Earth. Many nations plan to contribute to \x01\x0e+0200Freedom,\x01\x0e-0200 proving countries can cooperate in worthwhile ventures.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(NASA is currently working on a spaceplane that will take off from a runway and climb into space. This \"hyperplane,\" now called the X-30, would carry passengers to the space station. The passengers would then board a space taxi to whisk them to the moon or Mars.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Space Station)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Space Station)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Space Station\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Large permanent satellite built to house astronauts. Space stations provide laboratories where astronauts carry out experiments that are not possible on Earth. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(On April 19, 1971, the Soviets launched Salyut, the first space station which held several crews. America\'s Skylab was hurled into orbit on May 14, 1973. Three crews worked in Skylab. Both Skylab and Salyut were simple space stations transformed from third stage boosters. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Freedom)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A collection of countries from North America and Europe plan to launch a joint space station named \x01\x0e+0200Freedom\x01\x0e-0200. Modules will be ferried by the space shuttle and assembled in orbit. The station may serve as a launching post for missions to the moon and Mars.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Space Suit)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Covering worn by astronauts to protect them from the vacuum of space. It behaves like a personal spacecraft because it provides the astronaut with air and a comfortable temperature and shields the astronaut from deadly radiation. Spacesuits are constructed from layers of nylon, aluminum, and plastic. The suits have joints built into them because they stiffen when inflated.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The first Mercury space suits were modified high-altitude uniforms, and they could be inflated in an emergency. More advanced Gemini suits used an umbilical cord to pressurize astronauts. America\'s first spacewalks were performed in this gear. On the moon, however, astronauts walked free of the umbilical by carrying a Portable Life-Support System (PLSS). Lunar spacesuits were built for harsh conditions. They had an abrasion-proof outer covering, layers of insulation, and a micrometeroid shield. The helmet held twist-down shades and a gold film to protect astronauts from the sun. Also, the gloves and boots had extra layering to grip and tread across rocks.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Before the space shuttle, all suits were custom made for every astronaut. Now, all astronauts share a collection of modular suits. Parts like gloves, helmet, and trunks are sized for each astronaut before liftoff. Each suit is self-contained and protects an astronaut for up to 8 hours.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Specific Impulse)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Rocket engine\'s performance rating. Engineers calculate it by dividing the engine\'s thrust by the weight of fuel burned per second. This gives the time a unit of fuel will produce thrust. The higher an engine\'s specific impulse, the more efficiently it burns.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Speckle Interferometry)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Speckle Interferometer)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Speckle Interferometer\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Method of improving the resolution of an image taken at a ground-based observatory. If multiple shots are taken, a laser can find their interference and make a \"speckle pattern.\" Then, a computer recombines the shots to form one clear picture. Resolutions of 0.01 arc seconds are possible.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectral Lines)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Narrow lines seen when an object\'s light is spread into its component wavelengths or spectrum. The study of spectral lines (spectroscopy) is one of the main keys to understanding the universe. Information about stars and galaxies is obtained from spectral lines. Recall that a spectrum is produced when a beam of light is passed through either a prism or a diffraction grating. Spectral lines may be either bright emission lines or dark absorption lines missing from a continuous spectrum.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Some nebulas show emission lines, while almost all stars, including the sun, have absorption lines in their spectra. The position of lines in emission spectra correspond to the missing lines in absorption spectra. In 1814, Joseph Fraunhofer studied the sun\'s spectrum and identified many elements found on Earth. He also saw the lines of an unknown element and named it helium, from Helios (Greek word for the sun). Spectra from deep sky bodies were first studied in the 1860\'s by William Huggins and Pietro Secchi.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Every element emits its own characteristic spectral lines. Simple elements, like hydrogen, yield only a few spectral lines while more complex elements may have thousands. The red glow of many nebula seen in photographs is from the red emission line of hydrogen. An atom\'s spectral lines are measured in a laboratory, then compared to lines photographed from stars and nebula. Astronomers then find which elements float in nebulas or stars\' outer atmospheres. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronomers use spectra to measure a star\'s surface temperature. Studies called profiles analyze how lines appear across a continuous spectrum. Higher temperatures and pressures force lines to widen and brighten towards the bluer end of the spectrum. Astronomers then use this data to classify stars into spectral types named O,B,A,F,G,K, and M. The hottest, type O\'s, have seething 40,000 K surfaces that blaze bright blue, while cooler class M\'s show a red surface, shining dully at just 2,500 K. Our sun is a class G star at 5,700 K.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectral Type)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(Henry Draper Catalogue)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A way of organizing stars according to their surface temperature. In the 1860\'s, William Huggins and Pietro Secchi first photographed stellar spectra and found that the spacing of absorption lines differed slightly from star to star. Then in the early 1900\'s, E.C. Pickering worked at the Harvard observatory grouping stars with similar spectra. This project grew into the \x01\x0e+0200Henry Draper Catalogue\x01\x0e-0200 of 225,300 stars, published in 1924.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Originally, astronomers named spectra by successive letters of the alphabet. Scientists then realized temperature governs stellar spectra, and they reordered the letters from hottest to coolest: O, B, A, F, G, K, and M. Some strange stars, however, do not follow this scheme. The classes R, N, and S have the same surface temperature as K and M stars but show heavy elements in their spectra. R and N classes have carbon in their atmospheres while S stars contain zirconium. Also, bizarre Wolf-Rayet stars are unusually hot. Astronomers classify them as W.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Stars in separate spectral types differ by the relative abundance of elements in their coronas. For example, between the classes F and G lines of hydrogen weaken while calcium lines strengthen.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Some astronomers specialize in categorizing stars by their spectra. They look at the relative strengths of lines from elements like hydrogen, calcium, and even molecules like carbon dioxide. They can separate the spectra of dwarf stars and giant stars from those on the main sequence. Even though they have the same surface temperature, dwarfs and giants have spectra that show peculiarities not seen in main sequence stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectrograph)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Instrument that sits at the focus of a telescope and records the spectrum of a celestial body. Spectrographs are very important in astronomy, as they allow astronomers to study and classify spectra from stars and galaxies.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectroscope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Instrument used to observe the spectrum of a star or galaxy. It uses a diffraction grating to separate light by wavelength. A spectroscope is similar to a prism that projects a rainbow of colors when white light passes through it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectroscopic Binary)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Double star that orbits so closely to its companion that telescopes cannot split them apart. Astronomers know these are double stars because their spectra cycle through Doppler shifts. While one star approaches us, the other recedes. This causes one spectrum to blue-shift while the other red shifts. In 1889, E. C. Pickering discovered the first spectroscopic binary: Mizar. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(If two stars have nearly equal brightness, astronomers may photograph a different spectrum from each star. This, called a two-line binary, allows astronomers to deduce the mass of each star. Usually, the light of one star overpowers the other. This is called a single-line spectroscopic binary. Astronomers can then only set limits on the masses. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronomers use spectroscopic binaries to find invisible companions. If a luminous star orbits a neutron star or black hole, its spectrum will shift and reveal the mass of its unseen partner.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectroscopy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Study of spectra of heavenly bodies. Using a spectroscope, light from celestial bodies is spread into its component wavelength. Shining white light through a spectroscope yields a rainbow of colors, called the continuous spectrum. When a continuous spectrum shows thin black gaps in the rainbow, it is an absorption spectrum. An emission spectrum shows bright lines against a black background.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In 1859, German scientists Robert Wilhelm Bunsen (1811-1899) and Gustave Robert Kirchoff (1824-1887) proposed the laws governing spectroscopy. One principle states that any mass above absolute zero will emit radiation, with the wavelength determined by its temperature. Humans, for example, radiate heat and glow with infrared radiation. Gas spiraling into a black hole heats up to millions of degrees and radiates x-rays. Another law says that gas heated by a nearby hotter body gives an emission spectrum. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Quantum mechanics, first conceived by Neils Bohr in 1913, states that electrons can only exist certain distances, or levels, from the nucleus of an atom. Electrons can jump between these levels by absorbing only certain frequencies of light. It is like climbing stairs. You start on the first step and expend energy to rise to the next step. There is just step 1 and step 2, but no step between. The same thing happens to an electron when it changes orbits. When the right frequency of light passes by, the electron grabs it and uses its energy to jump to a higher orbit. On the stairs, you might jump two or three steps at a time. So do electrons. If light with more energy but the right wavelength skims by, the electron uses it to jump two or three orbits. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(When an electron leaps into a higher orbit, the atom becomes excited. An atom cannot hold an excited electron for very long so, after about one-billionth of a second, the electron falls back to its ground state and radiates the energy it used to jump. If we shine white light through a cloud of gas, atoms in the cloud will use some of the light\'s energy to jump into a higher orbit. They will then re-radiate the light in a different direction and fall back to their ground state. If when we observe the white light gleaming through the cloud, and see dark lines cutting through its continuous spectrum then we would be viewing an absorption spectrum. If, however, we look at the cloud by itself from a different angle, we would only see the particular frequencies (colored lines) that it re-radiated. We would then be viewing an emission spectrum. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The same thing happens in the outer atmospheres of stars. Cooler gases resembling the sun\'s corona and chromosphere surround the star. Elements in those outer layers absorb and redirect certain frequencies of light. When astronomers view light from these stars, it shimmers through the outer layers. Atoms in the gas absorb certain frequencies, and redirect them, just like in our cloud model. Astronomers observe the star\'s absorption spectrum. If they could block out the bright surface of the star and just look at the stars atmosphere they would see only the redirected light, just as if they looked at the cloud. Actually astronomers do this during an eclipse of the sun. During an eclipse astronomers get a rare opportunity to study the redirected emission light of the sun\'s chromosphere. They are seeing the bright lines of an emission spectrum which are usually overpowered by the bright surface of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Besides the telescope and camera, the spectroscope is the most useful instrument in astronomy. Shining starlight through these instruments reveals to astronomers what elements compose a body, how hot it glows, and whether it approaches or recedes from the Earth. Spectroscopes show some stars to be binaries and have given us the best evidence that the universe is expanding.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spectrum)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Entire range of electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths spanning gamma rays to radio waves. Often, people use this word to refer to just the visible part of the spectrum, which is the spread of rainbow colors.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Speed of Light)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Light, Velocity of)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Light, Velocity of\x01\x0e-0200 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spherical Aberration)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Blurring of an image that is reflected from a bowl-shaped mirror. Different points on the curve of the sphere reflect light at slightly different angles and do not bring the rays to a sharp focus. Spherical lenses suffer from both spherical and chromatic aberration.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Telescope builders have found ways to sidestep spherical aberration. Grinding mirrors into precise parabolas brings light to a fine focus. Also, corrector plates can be mounted at the front of telescopes with spherical mirrors. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spica)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Brightest star in Virgo and the sixteenth brightest in the entire sky. Also called Alpha Virginis, this blue-white star blazes at magnitude 0.96. It is 8 times as wide as the sun and lies 260 lightyears away. Spica is an eclipsing binary with a four-day cycle. The masses of the two stars are 10.9 and 6.8 times that of the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spicules)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Jets of gas shooting away from the chromosphere of the sun, giving it a hairy appearance. These eruptions blast 6,000 miles (9,600 km) into the corona at a temperature of 10,000\xc2\xb0 K. After about 5 minutes, they cool and then dive back to the chromosphere. There are about 500,000 spicules on the sun at any time, and they are grouped by the magnetic field into enormous cells.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spiral Arms)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Bands of bright young stars that wind away from the centers of spiral galaxies.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spiral Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Type of galaxy that shows arms winding away from the center. Some, called barred spirals, have just two arms snaking away from the barred-shaped nucleus. However, most spiral galaxies look like hurricanes and show bands of stars and dust twisting away from their center. The nucleus, containing old (population II) stars, is surrounded by globular clusters. Population I stars inhabit the arms, and scientists think these stars help maintain the galaxy\'s spiral structure. Shock waves from supernova explosions ripple through the arms and perturb dust clouds. They set off bursts of new stars, some of which, after a few million years, become supernovas and continue the cycle.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronomers classify spiral and barred spiral galaxies according to how tightly the arms are wrapped around the center. A tightly wound spiral is grouped as Sa, while looser ones are Sb and Sc, and barred spirals are SBa through SBc.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Our Milky Way is a spiral galaxy with the sun located about 3/5 the way from the center to the edge. The sun completes an orbit around the center about every 240 million years. So far, our solar system has completed about 20 circuits. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sporadics)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Meteor that streaks through the sky but is not part of any shower. On any night, there is an average of 6 sporadics per hour before midnight and 14 per hour after. To understand why, imagine Earth as a bus that never stops. Before midnight, we view the back of the orbit, like peering out the rear of the bus. The only meteors we see must overtake the Earth. After midnight, Earth turns until we face forward in its orbit. Any specks of dust Earth encounters flare into a meteors because we bulldoze over them. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Spring Tide)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Tides.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Tides.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sputnik)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(First series of Soviet satellites. In 1957, Sputnik 1 became the first artificial satellite. It was a nitrogen gas-filled aluminum sphere about the size of a basketball. It carried four long antennas that returned signals about its temperature and pressure. For three months, Sputnik 1 orbited the Earth. Its period was 96 minutes, and its altitude ranged from 142 to 588 miles (228 to 947 km).)
