[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Stafford, Thomas Patten)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Stafford, Thomas Patten (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Stafford, Thomas Patten (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronaut who flew four missions from Gemini to Apollo-Soyuz. Selected as an astronaut in 1962, Stafford piloted the 1965 Gemini 6 mission to rendezvous with Gemini 7. In 1966, he commanded the Gemini 9 mission with Eugene Cernan. He also commanded the Apollo 10 with Eugene Cernan and John Young. Finally, he flew in the July 1975 Apollo-Soyuz docking flight.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Star)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Enormous sphere of hot gas that, at some point in its life, fuses hydrogen into helium. The sun is an example of a typical star. Filling the night sky, stars appear as pinpoints of light because they lie so far away. Sunlight takes about 8.3 minutes to reach the Earth, but light from other stars requires many years. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Under a moon-less sky, away from streetlights, about 3,000 stars appear to the naked eye. A telescope reveals millions more. In fact, astronomers have cataloged over one million stars using the 48-inch Schmidt camera of the Palomar observatory. They surveyed all of the northern hemisphere and the southern hemisphere down to -33\xc2\xb0. They estimate that 800 million stars show up on these plates and that the Milky Way contains 200 billion. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Characteristics of Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Characteristics of Stars\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The size and appearance of stars spans a large range. About 99% are called main sequence stars; these fuse hydrogen into helium. The largest of these, with masses about 80 times that of the sun, are blue supergiants. Their 40,000\xc2\xb0 K surfaces shine with the light of 1 million suns. They must consume hydrogen swiftly to support their enormous weight and only last a few million years. Examples of blue supergiants are Rigel, Spica, and Vega. Blue supergiants, however, are very rare: only about one star in ten-thousand is a blue supergiant.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(More common are stars like the sun. They shine yellow-white with surface temperatures of 5,000 to 6,000\xc2\xb0 K and live about 10 billion years. Examples of sun-like stars are Capella and Procyon, and about 20% of all stars are of this type.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The most common kind of star is the red dwarf. These have only about one-fiftieth the mass of the sun and shine only one-millionth as brightly. Red dwarfs fuse hydrogen slowly because they only need to sustain their small weight. This lets them live for over 20 billion years. The universe is not old enough for any red dwarf to have moved off the main sequence into old age. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Birth of Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Birth of Stars\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Stars are born out of great clouds that are made mostly of hydrogen. The Orion Nebula, one such stellar nursery, contains enough mass to form 100,000 stars. These clouds are stable until the shock of a supernova ripples through them. This compression causes the cloud to collapse onto itself.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Gravity makes the cloud coalesce into spheres which will produce a cluster of stars. In each sphere the great weight of the surrounding layers compresses the core. Once a sphere has contracted enough, it starts glowing red and radiating heat. The sphere is now a protostar.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(For a time, the protostar goes through what is called a T Tauri (named after the star T in the constellation Taurus in which the process was first recognized) phase. Temperatures and pressures at the core of the star continue to climb and strip electrons from hydrogen nuclei. The hot temperatures allow protons to overpower their like-charge repulsion. When the core reaches about 10 million\xc2\xb0 K, the protons collide with enough speed to stick together and form helium nuclei, releasing energy. The moment fusion begins, contraction halts. Radiation from the core balances the inward pull of gravity. The surface then shines and blasts away the surrounding shell of gases. A star is born.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The amount of mass the sphere contains determines how fast the protostar will contract into a true star. Giant and supergaint stars collapse, move through the main sequence, progress off the main sequence into a red giant phase, and become supernovas before lighter stars mature past their T Tauri phase.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Inside a Star)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Inside a Star\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(At a star\'s core, temperatures and pressures allow lighter elements to fuse into heavier elements, releasing energy. Matter inside the core is a soup of electrons and naked nuclei. Between 10 and 15 million\xc2\xb0 K, the proton-proton chain dominates in which four hydrogen nuclei slam together to make helium. Above 15 million\xc2\xb0 K, the carbon-nitrogen-oxygen cycle controls fusion. The heavier elements are catalysts in this reaction. They force hydrogen to fuse into helium, but they never get used themselves. Above 100 million\xc2\xb0 K, helium fuses to carbon and oxygen. Heavier temperatures fuse higher elements: neon, magnesium, silicon, to iron. Each reaction releases energy to fight the relentless crush of gravity. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Evolution of Stars Similar in Size to the Sun)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Evolution of Stars Similar in Size to the Sun\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(When a star like the sun grows into its T Tauri phase, it is about 50 times bigger than the sun and 500 times brighter. As the star contracts, the core heats up and, after 30 million years, fusion ignites. Fusion pressure balances gravitational compression, and the star shines.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Sun-like stars keep churning hydrogen into helium for about 10 billion years. This, called the main sequence phase, is a star\'s adult life, and it spans most of the time a star will shine. But stars do not shine forever.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Eventually all stars approach a crisis in their lives. After shining for billions of years, they deplete their supply of nuclear fuel near the core. A hot core of helium \"ash\" sits at their center. The star tries to lengthen its life by kindling hydrogen fusion in a shell around the core. With its new lease on life, the star swells to 100 times its former size. Its surface cools to a dull red, but, because it grows so large, the star shines a thousand times more brightly than when it occupied the main sequence. In its old age, the star has become a red giant.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The cores of red giant stars sear at 100 million\xc2\xb0 K. They begin to fuse helium into carbon, but this releases less energy than hydrogen-helium fusion. This means the star must fuse faster than it did during its main sequence life. It also means that the red giant phase of a star\'s life lasts only a few million years. During these years, the star is a powerhouse, filling 400 times the space it did before. When the sun becomes a red giant, it may swell so large as to swallow Earth. The outer layers of the red giant eventually grow so tenuous that they drift away into space, forming a spherical shell called a planetary nebula (the ring nebula, M57, is a good example). Only the tiny core of the star remains. This cinder of a star is a white dwarf. When new, this stellar corpse shines white hot at 100,000\xc2\xb0 K. Over billions of years, it radiates away its accumulated heat until it almost reaches absolute zero. Then, this black cinder sits in space and is called a black dwarf. The universe may not yet be old enough to harbor any black dwarfs. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Evolution of Massive)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Evolution of Massive\x01\x0e-0100 \x01\x0e+0100Stars\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Stars containing much more gas than the sun follow a different life cycle. These huge stars must consume hydrogen at an enormous rate to support their crushing outer layers. Even though they are born with more hydrogen, they must fuse it faster. These stars blaze blue-white, shining ten-thousand times more brightly than the sun (the star Deneb is a good example). But their fuel soon runs out.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Blue giant stars live the shortest lives. While most stars shine for tens of billions of years, blue giants stay on the main sequence only a few million. Then they become huge red supergiants, growing so large as to fill the orbit of Mars. They exhaust their store of hydrogen and go on to fuse helium to carbon. But, unlike smaller stars, they have enough weight for their cores to reach the billions of degrees necessary to fuse carbon into oxygen, neon, and on up to iron. Giant stars show onion-like layers with inner shells fusing heavier elements. When iron accumulates in its core, the star nears its fiery death.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Iron is the most stable element in the universe. You cannot produce energy by fusing iron. In fact, iron fusion consumes energy. When iron gathers in the star\'s core, it can no longer postpone its death. In a heartbeat, the core collapses and then rebounds, like a compressed rubber ball. This rebound creates an enormous shock wave that rips through the star and detonates the most titanic explosion in the universe: a supernova.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Supernovas blast the outer layers of the star into an expanding shell that forms a nebula. All elements heavier than iron fuse in the fires of the explosion. At the center of the outburst sits a neutron star or a black hole. These stellar corpses are all that remain of the once brilliant star.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Star Cluster)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Group of stars bonded together by gravity and jointly traveling through space. Our galaxy contains two types: open clusters and globular clusters.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Globular clusters are dense spherical clumps of millions of old (population II) stars. Measuring about 100 lightyears across, they surround the galactic core and formed about 13 billion years ago. These clusters contain red dwarf stars, which have few heavy elements because they formed before heavy elements were made in supernova explosions. Harlow Shapley used RR Lyrae variables in globular clusters to measure their distance and chart the structure of our galaxy. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A few bright globular clusters, like Omega Centauri and M13, appear as fuzzy patches to the naked eye. If we lived on a planet in a globular cluster, we would never have a night. Stars pack these clusters so densely as to wash out the darkness with the light of 20 full moons. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(While globular clusters swarm around the core of the galaxy, the spiral arms contain open clusters. Open clusters are loose associations of young stars that form from the same nebula. Astronomers have charted about 1,000 open clusters and estimate that 18,000 reside in the Milky Way. Unlike stars in globular clusters, stars in open clusters drift apart. Many clusters have short-lived blue giants that die before smaller yellow and red stars form. Astronomers find which is the most massive main sequence star in an open cluster and use this to calculate the age of the cluster. The Pleiades, for example, is only a few million years old because bright blue stars illuminate this open cluster. Open clusters average 10 lightyears across and hold anywhere from about 100 stars to many thousands, as is the case of the \"double cluster\" in Perseus, gloriously seen with a small telescope. The Beehive (M44) in Cancer, and the Wild Duck Cluster (M11) in Scutum are two other beautiful open clusters.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(M13, a globular cluster of stars located in the direction of the constellation Hercules.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M13, a globular cluster of stars located in the direction of the constellation Hercules.\x01\x0e-0100)
