[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M13, a globular cluster of stars located in the direction of the constellation Hercules.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Stellar Association)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Groupings of 10 to 1,000 stars which formed from the same cloud at about the same time. Stellar associations have hot young O or B type stars or younger T Tauri stars. Members of these associations have not yet drifted apart but will disperse after about 10 million years.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Stonehenge)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Prehistoric circle of giant stones near Salisbury, in southern England. Evidence shows it was constructed in three stages. The first, Stonehenge I, built between 3100 and 2300 BC, had a circular ditch and a circle of 56 holes, which were quickly filled in. Stonehenge II, built between 2150 and 2000 BC, had a realigned entrance and two circles made from stones that were brought from Wales, 240 miles (385 km) away. Stonehenge III, built between 2100 BC and 1100 BC, had caps for the vertical stones that were made of a ring of horizontal ones, some weighing 50 tons. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(No one is quite sure why Stonehenge was built. Current theory says that it was created for either religious or astronomical purposes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Summer Triangle)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Distinctive asterism formed by three bright summer stars: Vega, Deneb, and Altair. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sun)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Star at the center of our solar system. The sun contains over 99% of the mass in the solar system and controls the orbits of planets, comets, and asteroids to about 2.4 lightyears from its center. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Our sun is one of the 200 billion stars inhabiting the Milky Way. It lies about 23,000 lightyears from the galactic core. This average population I (relatively young) star lies in one of the spiral arms and travels 150 miles (250 km) per second. The sun circles the core in close to 240 million years.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(General Characteristics)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100General Characteristics\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The Earth orbits the sun at an average distance of 93 million miles. Our sun displays a yellow-white disk one-half a degree across. Because it is so near, the sun is the only star to show surface features. It is an average yellow star of spectral type G2 with a surface temperature of 5,700\xc2\xb0 K and an internal temperature of 15 to 20 million\xc2\xb0 K. It appears blindingly bright in our sky with an apparent magnitude of -26.5 (500,000 times brighter than a full moon), but it has an absolute magnitude of only 4.6. Compared with all stars, the sun is rather large (red dwarfs make up more than 80% of stars), measuring 865,000 miles (1,392,000 km) across, but when compared with a giant star or supergiant star the sun is tiny. It is 109 times wider than the Earth but holds over one-million times the Earth\'s volume. The sun, with a mass of 2.19 X 10^(27) tons (1.99 X 10^(30) kg), is 330,000 times more massive than Earth, but it is only one-quarter as dense. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Since the sun is not a solid ball, it shows different rotation rates at different latitudes. The sun spins once every 24.7 days at the equator, 28.2 days at mid-latitudes, and 34 days near the poles. The accepted value of 25.38 days is called the sidereal rotation period. It is different from what is observed on Earth because, while the sun rotates, the Earth revolves in its orbit, and the sun must turn a little more to present the same face. The time it takes for the sun to rotate enough to present the same face twice to the Earth is called the synodic period and is 27.27 days long. The sun\'s axis of rotation is tilted 7.25\xc2\xb0 to the plane in which the Earth orbits the sun (ecliptic).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Surface Layers and Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Surface Layers and Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The sun\'s surface, called the photosphere, is not a solid surface, but a shell of dense gas a few hundred miles thick. As you descend through this layer, the temperature starts at 4,300\xc2\xb0 K and increases to 9,000\xc2\xb0 K. As you go from the photosphere outward, the photosphere makes a transition to the chromosphere, a thinner transparent region of hot gas that is about 12,000 miles (19,200 km) thick. Temperatures here rise to 1 million degrees K. Beyond the chromosphere is the corona, a near-vacuum containing gas particles at a temperature of 4 million degrees K. The corona shoots millions of miles above the photosphere and becomes the solar wind, a stream of particles that has been detected out to the orbit of Jupiter. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The spectrum of the sun shows thousands of dark absorption bands called Fraunhofer lines. These reveal the chemical composition of the sun\'s atmosphere. The abundance of elements show the nature of gas from which the sun formed: about 90 percent hydrogen, 8 percent helium, and 2 percent heavier elements. The same proportions of heavy elements appear on both the sun and the Earth. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Energy Production)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Energy Production\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The sun churns out 3.8x10^(38) ergs of energy per second, with every square centimeter of photosphere radiating the same amount of energy as a 9-horsepower engine. This is thousands of times more energy than that used to power all the lights in all the homes on Earth. The Earth lies so far away that it intercepts only a small portion of this energy. Even so, every square meter on Earth receives an average 1.3 kilowatts of power, a value called the solar constant. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(For centuries, scientists debated about how the sun shines. Ideas about crashing meteors and burning coal surfaced, but these predicted that the sun could blaze only a few million years, and geological evidence showed that the Earth has been around for longer than that. In the early 1900\'s, a theory emerged from Einstein\'s famous equation E=mc^(2). People realized that only nuclear fusion could supply energy for the sun\'s 10 billion year life. One reaction, called the proton-proton chain, that could power the core involves colliding hydrogen nuclei, which are converted to helium. It dominates in the cores of stars cooler than 15 billion K. In hotter cores, the carbon-nitrogen-oxygen cycle (CNO for short) dominates. These elements are not changed, but they help the star to fuse hydrogen into helium and create energy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Scientists are uncertain which reaction powers the sun\'s core. They do know, however, that the sun changes 4 million tons of matter into energy every second and has enough mass to remain stable for another 5 billion years. Then, in its old age, it will swell into a red giant and blaze for a few more million years. The sun will eventually shed its outer layers and die as a white dwarf.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sundog)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Circular disk of light that is 22\xc2\xb0 away from the real sun. Sundogs usually appear in pairs and are caused by refraction of sunlight by ice crystals in the atmosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sun Pillar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Vertical shaft of light that usually appears over the rising or setting sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Sunspot)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Cool dark patches on the surface of the sun. Sunspots range from blotches 900 miles (1,500 km) wide to systems spanning 90,000 miles (150,000 km). Sunspots grow larger and more numerous as eruptions in the corona increase. Sunspot clusters may cover millions of square miles. These spots look like nerve cells, showing a dark central umbra with a surrounding lighter penumbra. The penumbra is about 500 degrees cooler than the surrounding photosphere, and the umbra is 1,600 degrees cooler. These cooler temperatures allow molecules to form, and astronomers find gases shooting away from the umbra to the penumbra. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Scientists think the sun\'s magnetic field, which is thousands of times stronger than the Earth\'s, blocks hot convection currents from reaching the surface. Like other magnetic phenomena, sunspots follow an 11 year cycle. Spots seen during a maximum outnumber those of a minimum by 100 to 1. However, sunspots rarely venture farther than 40 degrees away from the sun\'s equator or closer than 10 degrees to it. Most groups last about 10 days. The magnetic field remains until the spots vanish.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Supercluster)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Collection of clusters of galaxies. These enormous bodies resemble sponges, with galaxies strewn along filaments and huge voids showing no luminous matter. Our Local Group is part of the Virgo supercluster. The average supercluster has twelve galactic clusters and spans hundreds of millions of lightyears. About 50 of these massive groupings are known.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Supergiant Stars)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Biggest and brightest stars in the universe. Supergiants contain from 20 to several hundred times the mass of the sun and shine a million times as brightly. Since supergiant stars live just a few million years and since only about 1 star in 10,000 is a supergiant, astronomers can only find a few of them. However, these powerhouses shine across great distances of galactic space. When astronomers view spiral arms in distant galaxies, all they see is the light of these supergiant stars. Sixteen of the one hundred brightest stars in the night sky are supergiants. Betelgeuse and Antares are examples of 2 red supergiant stars.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Superior Conjunction)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Lining up of Mercury or Venus with the sun and the Earth, with Mercury or Venus on the opposite side of the sun from the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Superior Planet)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Planet that orbits farther than the Earth from the sun. All the planets from Mars to Pluto are superior planets.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Supernova)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Explosion that occurs when an enormous star has depleted its supply of nuclear fuel. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Type I)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Type I\x01\x0e-0100: If there is a double star system in which one star is a white dwarf and the other is a red giant, enough material from the red giant may fall onto the dense core of the white dwarf to send out shock waves and produce a supernova explosion.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Type II)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Type II\x01\x0e-0100: Toward the end of their brief lives, massive stars fuse elements past carbon. They make oxygen, neon, silicon, etc., all the way through iron. Stars cannot produce energy by fusing iron.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(So, as iron accumulates in the core, the star can no longer support its great weight. In an instant, the core of the star collapses to form a sphere of neutrons (neutron star). This stabilizes the core, and the star rebounds like a compressed basketball. This creates a shock wave and a burst of neutrinos that rip through the star and detonate the greatest explosion known in the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(For a few weeks, supernovas shine as brightly as an entire galaxy. Astronomers can spot supernovas millions of lightyears distant. These titanic explosions create all elements beyond iron. Thus, in a real sense planets, cities, and humans are made of star dust, for every element on Earth and in our bodies once churned in the fires of a supernova. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(After the supernova explosion, the star\'s outer layers blast into the surrounding space, creating a nebula that grows for millennia. Supernova shock waves ripple through the arms of spiral galaxies. They enrich the interstellar medium with heavy elements and perturb nebula, which coalesce into new stars. They also shoot cosmic rays through the universe.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(List of Historical Supernovas)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100List of Historical Supernovas\x01\x0e-0100)
