[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(The supernova of 1054 AD as it appears today.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100The supernova of 1054 AD as it appears today.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Supernova 1987A)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Closest and brightest supernova observed from Earth since 1604. First seen in the Large Magellanic Cloud on the night of February 24, 1987, its progenitor star was Sanduleak -69 202. At its peak in mid-May, it reached magnitude 2.8.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Surveyor Probes)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Series of seven American robot probes that were sent to the moon. The triangular surveyor spacecraft stood ten feet (3 m) tall on three spidery landing legs and carried solar panels and a high-gain antenna atop its mast. As the craft approached the moon, a thruster maneuvered it into orbit. Three smaller rockets slowed the probe as it descended, landing at about 8 miles (5 km) per hour. On the Moon, it weighed 620 pounds (280 kg),1/6 its Earth weight.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Although Surveyor 2 crashed into the moon, and Surveyor 4 might have crashed, the Surveyor probes provided us with a great deal of information about the lunar surface. Each mission carried a television camera with a movable mirror. Surveyors 3 and 7 had mechanical scoops to collect lunar soil. Surveyors 5, 6, and 7 had boxes of chemicals to analyze lunar soil. They found basalt-like rocks, later confirmed by Apollo missions. The Surveyor program showed it was safe for humans to land on the moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Synchronous Orbit)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(When a satellite remains stationary (above the same place on the planet) with respect to a point on the surface of its parent body. For example, weather satellites are in a synchronous orbit with the Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Synchrotron Radiation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Wave energy emitted by charged particles whose course was altered while they were racing along at nearly the speed of light. In many cases this involves electrons spiraling around magnetic field lines. Synchrotron radiation shows a much different spectrum than thermal (temperature produced) radiation. Scientists first observed this in particle accelerators called synchrotrons. How fast the particle speeds and how much the particle weighs determines the wavelength of the radiation produced.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Electrons produce more synchrotron radiation than heavier particles such as protons. They emit synchrotron radiation in the magnetic fields of Jupiter, solar flares, supernova remnants, and explosions from galaxies and quasars. Some bodies, like the Crab Nebula, have such a powerful magnetic field that their synchrotron emission is visible.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Synchronous Rotation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(A condition that occurs when the periods of a celestial body\'s rotation and revolution of a satellite are the same. The moon, for example, shows the same face to Earth because of its synchronous rotation. In the distant future, tidal drag will force the Earth into a synchronous rotation, always displaying the same face to the moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Synodic Period)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Time taken by a body to return to the same position in the sky. Synodic period differs from sidereal period because of Earth\'s perspective. As Earth travels in its orbit, our view of the solar system changes against the fixed background stars. When we time how long a planet takes to return to the same position in the sky, we have also moved. It is like trying to measure the speed of another motorist on the freeway. If we travel 50 miles (88 km) per hour and the car next to us goes 55 miles (96.8 km) per hour, we measure it as only cruising 5 miles (8.8 km) per hour. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The ancient Greeks measured planets\' years by their synodic period. They thought the Earth stood fixed in the center of the solar system while all other bodies revolved around it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Syzygy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Rough alignment of the Earth, sun, and moon or of the Earth, sun, and another planet. Syzygy names both a conjunction and an opposition.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Tarantula Nebula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Large bright cloud of ionized gas in the Large Magellanic Cloud.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Tau Ceti)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Star similar to the sun that lies 11.4 lightyears away. It is the seventh-closest star to the sun, shines at magnitude 3.5, and is one of the few nearby stars that can be seen with the naked eye.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Taurid Meteors)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Annual October-November meteor shower. A thousand years ago, the Taurids, which come from the dust trail left by Encke\'s comet, were the heaviest meteor shower. Today, the Earth has cleared most of the dust away.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Taurus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Taurus (The Bull))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Taurus (The Bull)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Northern zodiacal constellation showing a bull charging towards Orion. Best seen in the winter, Taurus contains two bright open clusters: the Pleiades and the Hyades. The bright red star Aldebaran, which represents the bloody red eye of the bull, appears to be part of the Hyades. However, it actually lies between Earth and the Hyades. Near the southernmost horn of Taurus is the Crab Nebula (M1), site of the 1054 supernova explosion that was recorded by the Chinese and Anasazi. The Taurid meteor shower radiates from a point near Epsilon Tauri. Near the same spot sits Hind\'s variable nebula, which contains T Tauri, the prototype of a class of irregular variables. The sun wanders through Taurus from mid-May to mid-June.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Tektites)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Small glass rocks that measure up to 4 inches (10 cm) across and are found in the south eastern part of the United States, north and north eastern Africa, Czechoslovakia, and Australia. They are round or tear-drop shaped and contain little water. This distinguishes them from volcanic glass and indicates that they formed and cooled quickly. Tektites are similar to glass formed in meteor impacts, and some have blobs of iron from meteorites.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Scientists believe tektites form when a meteor impacts the Earth or moon. Since these rocks do not show streaks from cosmic ray collisions, they could not have traveled far in space. Many tektite fields are located near known meteor craters.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Device that collects and focuses light from celestial bodies into a point, making modern astronomy possible. However, because stars are already pin-point sources of light, they cannot be magnified. The first telescope appeared in 1608 and was constructed by Dutch lens maker Hans Lippershey. The next year, Galileo was the first to train a telescope on the heavens. Using a crude 30 power scope, Galileo found four moons circling Jupiter, phases in Venus, and craters and mountains on our moon. These sightings, supporting the Heliocentric model of the solar system, dealt a fatal blow to Aristotle\'s geocentric model.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The earliest type of telescope was the refractor. It used a lens mounted at the front of the tube to collect and focus light. However, refracting telescopes suffer from chromatic aberration. One way to remedy this is to build telescopes with very long focal lengths. Before 1721, astronomers like Christiaan Huygens and Giovanni Cassini used telescopes that were 150 feet (46 m) long and had a lens only a few inches wide. Called \"aerial telescopes,\" these monsters could discern such objects as rings and moons around Saturn, but they required dozens of workers to steer them. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Then, in 1721, John Hadley built the first reflecting telescope. It had a six-inch (15 cm) aperture in a tube that was 6 feet (1.8 m) long. Since reflectors do not suffer from chromatic aberration, they can hold large apertures in short tubes. Hadley built a Newtonian reflector that had a parabolic mirror that reflects light to tilted plane mirror. The plane mirror then directs light into the eyepiece. Today, most telescopes are Newtonian reflectors. Although it seems that high magnifications would be useful in astronomy, astronomers rarely use them because the distortions due to air turbulence will be evident in a highly magnified image.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Telescopium)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Telescopium (The Telescope))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Telescopium (The Telescope)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Faint southern constellation that is located south of Scorpius.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Telstar)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(American communication satellites, built by AT&T, that look like shiny beach balls with antennas protruding from their surfaces and are capable of intercontinental communication. In 1962, Telstar 1 linked television broadcasts between North America and Europe.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Terminator)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Line dividing the day and night sides of a planet, moon, or asteroid.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Tethys)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Fourth moon from Saturn. It is 657 miles (1,060 km) wide and was discovered by Giovanni Cassini in 1684. Tethys orbits Saturn every 45 hours and 18.5 minutes in a circular path 183,150 miles from the surface. It is made of frozen gas and has a density 1.1 times that of water. Odysseus, Tethys\' large (250 mile; 400 km wide) crater, has a central peak and is the largest crater with a central peak in our solar system. Also, about 10% of the surface of Tethys is taken up by a large valley named Ithaca Chasma.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Thales of Miletus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Thales of Miletus (c.624 BC-c.546 BC))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Thales of Miletus (c.624 BC-c.546 BC)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Greek philosopher, credited as a founder of modern physical science. One of his students is thought to have been the philosopher Anaximander. Thales thought that everything was made of water and that Earth was a disk floating in a huge ocean. Thales plotted the path of the sun across the sky, and legend says that he predicted the eclipse of May 28, 585 BC. Records indicate that the eclipse halted the battle between Lydians and the Medes. Because of this eclipse, historians have been able to accurately date this battle, making it the earliest dated battle.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Thor Rocket)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(American booster based on the Thor intermediate-range ballistic missile. The rocket stood 65 feet (19.8 m) tall and 8 feet (2.4 m) wide. It had a single engine like the one in the Atlas rocket, producing 150,000 pounds (70,000 kg) of thrust by burning liquid oxygen and kerosene. In 1963, it was withdrawn from military use, but it has proven itself worthy as a space launcher. Thor launched the Pioneers, Explorers, and other American satellites and probes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Thuban)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Third-magnitude star in the constellation Draco that was the North star about 5,000 years ago, during the time of the Egyptians. They built the Great Pyramids approximately aligned to Thuban. Due to precession, Thuban lost its place to Polaris as the bright star nearest the north pole. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Tides)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Cycle of rising and falling of the sea. Two tidal bulges, one on the side of the Earth closest to the moon and the other on the side of the Earth farthest from the moon, are formed by the gravitational tugs of the sun and moon. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Tides are strongest near the new and full moons because then the sun, moon, and Earth form a straight line. The tidal strength of the sun reinforces the moon and causes a spring tide (so named because the water springs up). Tides are weakest near the moon\'s first and last quarter when the moon sits at a right angle to the line connecting the Earth and sun. The tidal tugs of the sun and moon tend to cancel each other and cause a neap tide (when the tides are weak, neither high nor low). Daily tidal differences range from a few inches to many feet, and they depend on the phase of the moon and the shape of the coast.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Time)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Period, that is measured or measurable, during which or through which an event happens. Ancient cultures tracked the patterns of day and night, the changing phases of the moon, and the rhythm of the seasons. The day, month, and year are units of time based on astronomical cycles, and they are the building blocks of our calendar. Hours, minutes, and seconds are convenient subdivisions of our day, ingrained in our culture since ancient times. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Primitive clocks used falling sand or dripping water to mark time. These crude contraptions caused errors in tracking time, and they had to be reset according to astronomical observation. Observers either noted when the sun reached its lowest or highest point or they recorded the rising and setting times of bright stars. More precise clocks were developed in the 16th and 17th centuries when navigators had to chart their longitude using transit times of the sun and other stars. Observatory clocks and nautical timepieces were used by many seafaring nations. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Until the 1920\'s, scientists used the rotation of the Earth as a standard to measure time. They believed that no clock better than the Earth could ever be found. They were wrong. In 1925, the Synchronome-Shortt clock started timing the Earth\'s rotation at the Royal Greenwich Observatory, and it was found that the rotation period varied. Astronomers called this variation nutation. The period varies by up to 0.003 seconds per day. In the 1960\'s, more accurate clocks, like the quartz-crystal devices in wrist watches and atomic clocks, were built.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Titan)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Largest moon of Saturn and the only moon known to have a substantial atmosphere. Titan was discovered by Christiaan Huygens in 1655. At an average distance from Saturn of 759,080 miles (1,221,620 km), it orbits Saturn every 15 days, 22 hours and 41.5 minutes. In 1944, the American planetary scientist Gerard Kuiper found a methane atmosphere around Titan. Astronomers think it has clouds of methane and hydrogen that holds heat in a greenhouse effect and holds the temperature at 94\xc2\xb0 K. Atmospheric pressure on Titan is 1.6 times that on Earth. Titan measures 3,200 miles (5,150 km) across, but the clouds may penetrate 100 miles (160 km), making the surface 3,100 miles (4,990 km). Titan, therefore, is larger than Mercury and has a denser atmosphere than Mars. Except for Ganymede, Titan is the largest moon in the solar system. It has a density of 1.6 times that of water, causing scientists to speculate that the surface is made of slushy ice with seas of liquid methane. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Titan Facts:)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Titan Facts:\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Temperature is about 94\xc2\xb0 K)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Pressure is 1.6 Earth atmospheres)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Atmosphere contains 78% nitrogen)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Titan has only traces of oxygen, and it has no liquid water, water vapor, or carbon dioxide.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Titan Rocket)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Group of American space boosters, modified from the Titan inter-continental ballistic missile. The two-stage Titan I missile burned kerosene and liquid oxygen. Titan II could store its fuel while it stood in its missile silo. NASA modified the Titan to launch Gemini capsules into orbit. The first stage stood 63 feet (19.2 m) tall and delivered 430,000 pounds (195,000 kg) of thrust. The second stage measured 27 feet (8.2 m) and produced 100,000 pounds (45,000 kg) of thrust.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Titania)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Fourth moon from Uranus. Discovered in 1787 by William Herschell, Titania completes an orbit every 8 days 16 hours and 56.5 minutes. It lies an average distance of 270,260 miles (435,910 km) above Uranus and is 1,100 miles (1,800 km) across. Scientists think that Titania is made mainly of water ice, with a little bit of rock and methane frost.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Titius-Bode Law)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Bode\'s Law.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Bode\'s Law.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Titov, Gherman Stepanovich)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Titov, Gherman Stepanovich (b. 1935))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Titov, Gherman Stepanovich (b. 1935)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Second Russian in space. In August 1961, he became the first human to spend a whole day in orbit. He circled the Earth 17 times in Vostok 2, riding through space for 25.3 hours.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Tombaugh, Clyde William)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Tombaugh, Clyde William (b. 1906))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Tombaugh, Clyde William (b. 1906)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(American astronomer who found Pluto on February 18, 1930 while working at the Lowell Observatory.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Total Eclipse)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_emphasis(See Eclipse.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Eclipse.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Trapezium)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Cluster of stars at the heart of the Orion Nebula. Four of these are young blue stars which shine at magnitudes 5.1, 6.7, 6.7, and 8.0. Through a telescope they form a distinct trapezoid. Larger apertures will show fainter stars in the group shining at 11th magnitude.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Transit)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_list_start((null), 1)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Moment when a heavenly body crosses the meridian (imaginary north-south line which passes through the observer\'s zenith). This is their highest point. Astronomers monitor the transit of stars to gauge the rotation of the Earth, vital in establishing standard time. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_listitem(Moment when a heavenly body crosses the meridian (imaginary north-south line which passes through the observer\'s zenith). This is their highest point. Astronomers monitor the transit of stars to gauge the rotation of the Earth, vital in establishing standard time. 
