[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Umbriel)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Third closest moon of Uranus, discovered in 1851 by William Lassell. It completes an orbit every 4 days, 3 hours and 27.5 minutes at an average distance of 165,110 miles (266,300 km). Umbriel is about 690 miles (1,117 km) across. Its surface, which is darker than those of Uranus\' other moons, seems to have been resurfaced recently. Some of its many craters are brighter than the rest of its surface.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Universe)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Everything there is. The universe contains all space, time, energy, and matter. The study of the universe is called cosmology.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(History)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100History\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Many ancient civilizations held the Earth as the center of the universe. In ancient Greece, Aristotle maintained that the Earth sat motionless while a great celestial sphere rotated around it. Ptolemy promoted this model and tried to account mathematically for the motions of the planets.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The geocentric model of the universe stood for 2,000 years until scientists realized that Earth was a planet that revolved around the sun. They also realized that stars must be similar to the sun but lie much farther away than the planets. William Herschel examined where the sun must sit relative to other stars. From watching the Milky Way wrap around the sky, he correctly concluded that our galaxy is disc-shaped. From counting stars, Herschel determined that the sun must lie at the center of this disk, but he did not realize that obscuring dust blocks starlight beyond about 4,000 lightyears. The dust lets us only view a small section of our galaxy.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(For the next 200 years, astronomers thought the Milky Way encompassed the whole universe. Then, in the early 1900\'s, a debate erupted over the \"spiral nebulas\" seen scattered around the sky. Some astronomers thought that these were only collections of stars within our own galaxy, while others believed they were \"island universes\" like the Milky Way. Edwin Hubble finally settled the debate.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In the 1920\'s, Hubble photographed many spiral nebulas with the newly-built Mount Wilson telescope. With its enormous mirror, he resolved Cepheid variable stars in distant galaxies. Using the period-luminosity relationship, discovered by Henrietta Leavitt, he determined that these bodies must lie hundreds of thousands or millions of lightyears away, far outside of the Milky Way.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(As Hubble scrutinized the spectra of distant galaxies, he noticed red shifts. A trend emerged where more distant galaxies showed stronger red shifts, implying they receded faster. He generalized this relationship into Hubble\'s Law, founding modern cosmology.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Modern Cosmology)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Modern Cosmology\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Astronomers now interpret the recession of distant galaxies as evidence that the universe is expanding. This means that, in the past, the universe was smaller and, in the distant past, it was much smaller. Cosmologists theorize that, about 15 billion years ago, everything in the universe was compressed into a tiny kernel of energy. At the beginning of time, this concentration exploded in a blast of unbelievable power called the Big Bang.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Today, most astronomers accept the Big Bang model as accurate. They point to galactic red-shifts and the cosmic background radiation discovered by Robert Wilson and Arno Penzias in 1963. They are also engaged in a search to answer the question:)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(What is the Fate of the Universe?)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100What is the Fate of the Universe?\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Today, cosmologists wonder whether or not the universe will expand forever. The problem hinges on how much mass the universe contains because the amount of mass decides whether gravity is strong enough to halt the present expansion. Researchers have calculated that four possible fates await the universe:)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_list_start(, 1)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(If there is not enough mass, expansion will continue forever. Stars will churn galaxies for hundreds of billions of years until no nuclear fuel remains. The once luminous galaxies will dim and rush away from each other forever, and, after infinite time, the temperature of the universe will drop to absolute zero.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_listitem(If there is not enough mass, expansion will continue forever. Stars will churn galaxies for hundreds of billions of years until no nuclear fuel remains. The once luminous galaxies will dim and rush away from each other forever, and, after infinite time, the temperature of the universe will drop to absolute zero.
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(If the force of gravity equals the force of expansion, universal expansion will slow to a crawl. Galaxies will forever hold their positions, just creeping away from one another. In this scenario, stars will again consume all nuclear fuel while the cosmos grows cold.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_listitem(If the force of gravity equals the force of expansion, universal expansion will slow to a crawl. Galaxies will forever hold their positions, just creeping away from one another. In this scenario, stars will again consume all nuclear fuel while the cosmos grows cold.
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(If the force of gravity overpowers expansion, galaxies will stop receding. Red-shifts will turn into blue-shifts as the universe collapses on itself. As matter compresses together, it will heat up until it loses its identity. Atoms will disintegrate. Everything will squeeze together in a catastrophe called the Big Crunch. The giant telescopes of today peer towards the edge of the observable universe, hunting for the critical mass needed to halt expansion. So far, only about 30 percent has been found, but astronomers think most of the mass may hide as dark matter. Candidates for dark matter include exotic particles, black holes, brown dwarfs, and even non-luminous galaxies.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_listitem(If the force of gravity overpowers expansion, galaxies will stop receding. Red-shifts will turn into blue-shifts as the universe collapses on itself. As matter compresses together, it will heat up until it loses its identity. Atoms will disintegrate. Everything will squeeze together in a catastrophe called the Big Crunch. The giant telescopes of today peer towards the edge of the observable universe, hunting for the critical mass needed to halt expansion. So far, only about 30 percent has been found, but astronomers think most of the mass may hide as dark matter. Candidates for dark matter include exotic particles, black holes, brown dwarfs, and even non-luminous galaxies.
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] , 3)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(If the Big Crunch occurs, there may be another Big Bang, creating another universe. There might be an endless cycle, called the Big Bounce, of Big Bangs and Big Crunches.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_listitem(If the Big Crunch occurs, there may be another Big Bang, creating another universe. There might be an endless cycle, called the Big Bounce, of Big Bangs and Big Crunches.
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] 
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] , 3)
