[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Universal Time)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Universal Time (UT))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Universal Time (UT)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Same as Greenwich Mean Time. It is the standard astronomers use to time events like eclipses and transits. Universal time is based on the motion of the sun and is mathematically linked to sidereal time. Astronomers keep universal time in synch with shorter sidereal time by using leap seconds.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Uraniborg)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Castle-like observatory of Tycho Brahe located on the island of Hven, north of Copenhagen, Denmark. Completed in 1580, it was used by Brahe for 20 years. Now, only its ruins remain.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Uranus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Uranus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Uranus\x01\x0e-0100 )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Seventh planet from the sun and the first to be discovered with a telescope. Uranus is a gas giant four times wider than the Earth. Its radius is 15,035 miles (25,059 km). If you wanted to escape from Uranus\' gravity, you would need to travel at 12.7 miles (21.2 km) per second. At its brightest, Uranus is just visible to the naked eye, but it was not identified as a planet until March 13, 1781 by Sir William Herschel. Uranus is so far away from the sun that it receives only 0.27% of the light that falls on Earth, and the temperature at the top of the atmosphere is probably about -350\xc2\xb0F (-212\xc2\xb0 C) Viewed through a telescope, Uranus appears as a small green ball. Only five of its fifteen moons can be viewed from Earth. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Physical Properties)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Physical Properties\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Uranus orbits the sun at an average of 19.18 a.u. (1,783,000,000 miles or 2,869,600,000 km), twice the distance from Saturn to the sun. Its nearly circular orbit (eccentricity of 0.047) is inclined only 0.75 degrees to the ecliptic, and it completes an orbit every 84 years. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Because of changing distances from the Earth, Uranus\' brightness varies by up to 26%, and it changes brightness as it rotates through its 17.3 hour day. Uranus\' most puzzling feature is its axis of rotation\'s large (98\xc2\xb0) tilt ( it is tipped almost sideways), making it lie nearly in its orbital plane. This gives the poles of Uranus a 42 year summer and a 42 year winter.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(In 1976, a team led by James Janesik photographed Uranus with the University of Arizona\'s 1.54 meter telescope in near-infrared wavelengths. By stretching the resolution of their telescope to its limits, they observed clouds of methane and ammonia. Then, in 1977, a team led by James Elliot witnessed Uranus eclipse a bright star. They found that the star dimmed five times before encountering Uranus, and they thought these resulted from dark rings orbiting the planet. Later observations found eleven rings. When Voyager 2 visited Uranus in 1986, it photographed the planet\'s rings, revealing them to be composed of charcoal-like substances with one of the lowest albedos in the solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(When Voyager 2 showed Uranus to have a magnetic field, scientists used it to calculate the present estimated rotational period of 17.3 hours. Scientists now believe that Uranus has a rocky core that is surrounded by a sea of water, methane, and ammonia. Even though this sea is estimated to have a temperature of thousands of degrees Celsius, it does not boil away because of the huge pressure on it. This sea is thought to be the cause of Uranus\' magnetic field.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Moons of Uranus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Moons of Uranus\x01\x0e-0100)
