[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Ursa Major)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Ursa Major (The Great Bear))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ursa Major (The Great Bear)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Major northern constellation that is best viewed in the spring and lies on the opposite side of Polaris from Cassiopeia. It is circumpolar north of the mid-latitudes. Its seven brightest stars form a pan-shaped asterism named the Big Dipper. The Alpha and Beta stars, Dubhe and Merak, form the pointer stars that lead to Polaris. Dubhe is a binary, with members shining at magnitudes 2.0 and 4.8 and orbiting each other every 44 years. Alioth, the Epsilon star, is a spectroscopic binary that completes a cycle every 5 days. The other multiple stars are Gamma (Phekda) and Eta (Benetnash ) and Zeta (named Mizar). Five of the stars in the dipper form an association that travels through space together. Also, many galaxies, like spiral M81 and M101, populate Ursa Major. Planetary nebula M97, also called the Owl nebula, resides inside Ursa Major. Finally, Ursa Major contains Lalande 21185, a magnitude 7.48 red dwarf that is the fourth-closest star to the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(LEFT:  In late Fall, the Big Dipper lies above the Hudson River near Kingston, NY; the pointer stars are almost vertical beneath Polaris.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100LEFT:  In late Fall, the Big Dipper lies above the Hudson River near Kingston, NY; the pointer stars are almost vertical beneath Polaris.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(RIGHT:  At the same time in Sarasota, Florida, the Dipper takes a dip, much of it skimming below the horizon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100RIGHT:  At the same time in Sarasota, Florida, the Dipper takes a dip, much of it skimming below the horizon.\x01\x0e-0100)
