[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Ursa Minor)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Ursa Minor (The Lesser Bear))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Ursa Minor (The Lesser Bear)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Northern-most constellation whose brightest star, Polaris, is less than one degree from the celestial north pole. The second brightest star, Kochab, was the north star between 1,500 BC and 300 AD. Ursa minor is more commonly known for its asterism: the Little Dipper.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(US Naval Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(US government-owned astronomical observation post, located in Washington DC, with telescopes in Argentina, Arizona, Florida, and New Zealand. From its establishment in 1830, it has made accurate sky charts for US Naval navigation. In 1844, it was renamed the US Naval observatory and was moved to its present site in 1893. USNO, which contains one of the best astronomical libraries in the world, is responsible for the establishment of time zones in the United States.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(V-2 Rocket)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(First modern ballistic missile fired by the Germans against enemy targets during W.W.II and the first rocket to pass the speed of sound. The V-2 was built by a team of German engineers under Werner von Braun and first tested on October 3, 1942. The V-2 stood 46 feet (14 m) tall and 65 inches (1.65 m) wide. It weighed 28,000 pounds (12,700 kg) and generated 56,000 pounds (25,400 kg) of thrust to deliver a 1-ton payload 200 miles (320 km). The V-2 was first fired on an enemy on September 6, 1944. After the war, Americans captured V-2 and based the American Redstone rocket on it.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Valles Marineris)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(System of ravines on Mars that dwarfs the Grand Canyon. The gorges run east-west in the northern hemisphere for 3,100 miles (5,000 km). Its canyons average 3.6 miles (6 km) deep and 96 miles (160 km) wide. At the western end lies Noctis Labryinthus, a complex fault system which outlines geometric figures. Erosion on the canyon floor and rock debris in the walls suggest water once flowed through them, but most of the canyon was shaped by faults.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Van Allen, James Alfred)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Van Allen, James Alfred (b. 1914))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Van Allen, James Alfred (b. 1914)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(American physicist who managed the V-2 upper atmosphere research after W.W.II and designed the Aerobee sounding rocket. Van Allen is best known for the instruments he placed on America\'s first satellite, Explorer I. He installed Geiger counters on the probe that counted cosmic ray particles high in Earth\'s atmosphere. They discovered two donut-shaped regions, named Van Allen Radiation belts, which showed that Earth has a magnetosphere.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Van Allen Belts)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Two shells that surround the Earth and trap cosmic radiation. These torrid (donut-shaped) belts collect high-energy electrons and protons that spiral down Earth\'s magnetic field from the solar wind. James Van Allen found these radiation belts in 1958 by studying data returned by Explorer I)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(The cross section of both belts resemble huge ears that lie at heights of 1,900 miles (3,000 km) and 14,000 miles (22,000 km) above Earth\'s equator. Space probes orbiting Venus and Mars show that these planets do not have radiation belts, meaning that they do not generate strong magnetic fields. Jupiter, on the other hand, has Van Allen Belts 10,000 times stronger than Earth\'s. Its large mass and rapid rotation drives Jupiter\'s magnetic dynamo. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Van De Kamp, Peter)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Van De Kamp, Peter (b. 1901))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Van De Kamp, Peter (b. 1901)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Dutch-born American astronomer who began surveying nearby stars for planets outside our solar system in 1937. He photographed the proper motion of Barnard\'s Star, the second closest star to the sun and observed how it wobbled. In 1956, he concluded that a planet 1.6 times the mass of Jupiter circled it. After further observation, in 1963 he announced that two massive planets orbited Barnard\'s star, and in 1974, he announced that the Star Epsilon Eridani had a companion 6 times the mass of Jupiter. Van De Kamp\'s claims have yet to be conclusively proven true or false.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Vandenberg Air Force Base)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Headquarters at Lompoc, California, of the Western Test Range for rocket and missiles. The government plans to build a pad there to launch the Space Shuttle into polar orbit (an orbit that circles the poles instead of the equator).)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Variable Star)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Stars that change their magnitude over a cycle. About one-third of all stars vary their light output. Variable stars are important because they allow astronomers to gauge stellar distances and sizes. They also revealed the universe to be dynamic, not static as the Greeks believed. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Eclipsing Variables)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Eclipsing Variables\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Some stars show a change in their brightness because they have obscuring dust swirling around them. Astronomers think thick disks of coalescing matter surround T Tauri stars. This matter eclipses the starlight, making it appear to change irregularly. Other stars, such as the sun, have spots that blemish their surface and change their light output. Their radiance varies slightly as sunspots drift across their disks. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Some binaries are variable when the plane of their orbit is in Earth\'s line of sight and the stars eclipse each other. Eclipsing binaries are handy because astronomers can measure the size of each member by timing the length of the eclipses. These pairs repeat their cycle of brightening and dimming with monotonous regularity. A famous example is the star Algol in Perseus. The ancient Arabs beheld its 2.87 day cycle, calling it \"the ghoul.\" )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(True Variables)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100True Variables\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Some variables swell and contract to change their luminosity due to shock waves that ripple out from their core and bloat their exterior. The most famous and first to be discovered are Cepheid variables, named after the prototype star Delta Cephi. Identified by John Goodricke in 1784, these red giants pulsate in cycles of 2 to 40 days.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Henrietta Leavitt found a direct relation between the length of a Cepheid variable\'s period and itsabsolute magnitude after studying them in the Smaller Magellanic Cloud. Since all the stars in this cloud are about the same distance away, she observed how their cycle was related to their brightness. This famous equation is called the period-luminosity relation. Edwin Hubble later used Leavitt\'s data to calculate the distances to nearby galaxies and settled the debate of whether these \"spiral nebulas\" were collections of dust inside the Milky Way or true galaxies standing by themselves.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Resembling Cepheids are the W Virginis stars. These stars were confused for Cepheid variables because of their periods, but they are four times dimmer. This led people to estimate their distance four times than what it actually was and, because Cepheids are often used as celestial yard-sticks, to overestimate distances in the universe. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Still another type of variable is the RR Lyrae. These stars alter their brightness by about one magnitude over several hours. All RR Lyraes have the same absolute magnitude, so astronomers use them as \"standard candles\" to determine the distances to globular cluster. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Not all variables show regular cycles and thus not all variable stars can be used as distance indicators. The brightness of some irregular stars, like Betelgeuse in Orion, changes unpredictably in a few hours. Other irregulars, like Mira, follow rough cycles that may last a year or more, fluctuating their light output by a factor of 1700! Astronomers are still trying to understand what causes Mira variables to change so drastically.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Vega)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Brightest star in Lyra and the fifth-brightest in the sky. Shining at magnitude 0.04, Vega is a white star lying 26 lightyears away. It is 3 times wider than the sun. Due to precession, the Earth\'s axis will drift near Vega around 14,000 AD, and Vega will then sit near the celestial north pole, though not as closely as Polaris is today.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Vela)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Vela (The Sails))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Vela (The Sails)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Southern constellation, once part of Argo Navis, that contains a bright section of the Milky Way and lies near Centaurs. Its brightest star is a visual binary of magnitudes 2.22 and 4.79.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Venus)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Second planet from the sun and the closest planet to Earth. Venus\' radius is 3,631 miles (6,052 km). Venus is 0.72 a.u. from the sun and has an orbit of 224.701 days. It turns once on its axis every 243 days, in a retrograde rotation (its direction of rotation is opposite from its direction of orbit). If you wanted to escape from Venus\' gravity, you would need to travel at 6.2 miles (10.3 km) per second. Because of their similar mass and distance from the sun, Earth and Venus are often called twins. Their similarities end, however, with their size and distance from the sun.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Atmosphere)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Atmosphere\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Venus\' thick atmosphere prevents direct optical observation of its surface. This thick atmosphere also results in a surface pressure that is 93 times that on Earth at sea level. Although Venus\' atmosphere contains 30,000 times the carbon dioxide in Earth\'s atmosphere. overall the two planets have roughly equal amounts of carbon dioxide except that Venus\' is in its atmosphere while Earth\'s is trapped mainly in rocks. Nitrogen makes up 3.5% of Venus\' atmosphere compared to 78% for Earth. While Earth\'s atmosphere contains clouds of water vapor, Venus\' contains clouds of sulfuric acid. Atmospheric winds reach up to 100 meters per second on Venus. Because these winds blow in the same direction that the planet rotates, they are called \"super-rotation\" winds. Because it has no magnetic field, Venus is bombarded by the solar radiation.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Surface)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Surface\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Venus\' surface is approximately 500 million years old, which means that it has been resurfaced at least once since it formed roughly 4.5 billion years ago. This resurfacing may have come from Venus\' crust shrinking or expanding, or from volcanic activity. There is debate over whether Venus\' new surface came about through one cataclysmic event or through a higher rate of volcanic activity in the past that gradually died down to its present level. Eighty percent of Venus\' surface is covered with plains that were formed by deposits from volcanic activity. Even though Venus currently has quakes and possibly volcanic activity, there is no evidence (such as strings of volcanoes) that it has a global system of plates that drift about, as the Earth does.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Before Venus\' surface was mapped, it was thought to be similar to swampy areas on Earth. However, Venus has no liquid water. It is not clear whether Venus once had water and lost it or never had much water at all. However, even if Venus once did have water, its current surface temperature of 800\xc2\xb0 F (460\xc2\xb0 C) would not allow it to remain in liquid form because this temperature is hot enough to melt lead. The winds at Venus\' basaltic surface are very slow, reaching just one meter per second.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Although Venus\' surface does contain impact craters, its thick hot atmosphere prevents them from being smaller than 1.86 miles (3 km) across. An object small enough to make a crater smaller than this would burn up in Venus\' atmosphere. Venus\' atmosphere, however, does permit some double craters to form. These are thought to be created when a meteor breaks up in the atmosphere and the pieces fall together.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Venus has a 4,216 mile (6,800 km) long canyon, which is the longest canyon in the solar system.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Venus Facts:)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Venus Facts:\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Average Daytime Temperature is 859\xc2\xb0 F (477\xc2\xb0 C))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Pressure is about 93 Earth atmospheres)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Atmosphere contains 96% carbon dioxide, 3.5% nitrogen, 0.1% water vapor, and only traces of oxygen)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Vernal Equinox)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Vernal Equinox (Spring Equinox))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Vernal Equinox (Spring Equinox)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Moment when the sun crosses the celestial equator heading north. The vernal equinox signals the end of winter in the northern hemisphere and the end of summer in the southern hemisphere. The crossing point also marks the zero point of right ascension in the sky, used to locate heavenly bodies east or west. The sun\'s crossing point used to be in Aries, but, because of precession, the sun\'s crossing point has drifted into Pisces.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Very Large Array)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Very Large Array (VLA))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Very Large Array (VLA)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(World\'s largest and most sensitive radio telescope. It is located 40 miles (64 km) west of Sorocco, New Mexico. Its 27 steerable dishes, each 82 feet across, can be rearranged because they ride on railroad tracks that outline a Y shape. Each arm in the Y is 13 miles (21 km) long. The antennas can catch signals with wavelengths as short as 1 centimeter. Through aperture synthesis, the network achieves the resolution of a single dish 17 miles (27 km) wide.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Very Large Telescope)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Very Large Telescope (VLT))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Very Large Telescope (VLT)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Enormous telescope being built in Chile. Construction started in late 1988 under the direction of the European Southern Observatory. There are plans to erect four giant reflectors that measure 25 feet (8 m) across and process their images to achieve the power of a single telescope 52 feet (16 m) wide.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Very Long Baseline Array)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Very Long Baseline Array (VBLA))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Very Long Baseline Array (VBLA)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Network of ten radio telescopes from Hawaii to northeastern Canada. The antennas are linked by a computer to mimic a telescope 5,000 miles (8,000 km) in diameter, giving a resolution of 0.2 milliseconds of arc.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_header(Vesta)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_double_emphasis(Vesta (Minor Planet 4))
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Vesta (Minor Planet 4)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Fourth asteroid to be discovered. It was identified by Heinrich Wilhelm Matthaus Olbers on March 29, 1807. Vesta orbits the sun in an elliptical path ranging from 2.15 a.u. to 2.57 a.u. from the sun on the inner rim of the asteroid belt. Its orbit, which takes 3.63 years, is only inclined 7 degrees to the ecliptic.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:01] render_paragraph(Vesta is roughly spherical with a diameter of 240 miles (390 km) and mass of 3x10^(17) tons. Its basalt surface gives it an albedo of 26%. Its bright surface and nearness to Earth allow Vesta to achieve magnitude 6. Sharp eyes can distinguish it in a clear, dark sky when it is nearest to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Viking Spacecraft)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Two American space probes dispatched to study Mars in 1976. Both probes had two parts: an orbiter and a lander. The orbiter photographed Mars, searching for suitable landing sites. Once found, the lander descended and conducted experiments to look for life. Cushioned by three legs, the landers stood 7 feet (2.1 m) tall and weighed 1,320 pounds (599 kg). Both landers had mechanical scoops to test the Martian soil and carried video cameras to survey the surroundings. The lander carried a radio dish to send signals from the orbiter to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Visual Binary)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_emphasis(See Binary Star.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0200See Binary Star.\x01\x0e-0200)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Visual Magnitude)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Measure of the brightness of a body in visible light, with wavelengths ranging from about 3,000 to 7,000 angstroms.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Virgo)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Virgo (The Virgin))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Virgo (The Virgin)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Constellation in the equatorial region of the zodiac. The sun crosses the autumnal equinox there around September 21. Gamma Virginis, a double star with components shining at magnitudes 3.63 and 3.60, completes an orbit every 171.76 years. Virgo\'s only bright star is Spica, but the constellation contains thousands of distant galaxies (the Virgo Cluster) including the Sombrero Galaxy, or M104.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Virgo A (M87))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Strongest radio source in Virgo. Astronomers think that this monster galaxy harbors a supermassive black hole and that radio waves radiate from a 4,000 lightyear long jet of gas shooting away from the core. Recent observations by the repaired Hubble Space Telescope have detected violently swirling gases near it\'s core, confirming the presence of a black hole. This is one of the strongest pieces of evidence yet that black holes do exist.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Volans)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Volans (The Flying Fish))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Volans (The Flying Fish)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Southern constellation that contains no stars brighter than magnitude 3.5.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Von Braun, Werner)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Von Braun, Werner (1912-1977))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Von Braun, Werner (1912-1977)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(German-born American rocket engineer, one of the designers of the world\'s largest rocket: the Saturn V. While still in his teens, he aided rocket pioneer Hermann Oberth in the German Society for Space Travel. The German army showed interest in von Braun\'s work and sponsored his Ph.D. dissertation that laid the plans for the V-2.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(In 1944, the Nazi secret police arrested von Braun, charging him with being more interested in designing rockets for space travel than helping the war. He was guilty, but, because his V-2 was so successful, von Braun was released. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Near the end of the war, von Braun and his team fled south to surrender to American forces. They continued their rocket research from White Sands, New Mexico and Huntsville, Alabama, where they built the Redstone missile.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Vostok)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(First Soviet capsule to carry cosmonauts into space. Vostok was a sealed sphere 7.5 feet (2.3 m) across which weighed 5,290 pounds (2,400 kg). It maintained normal air pressure and could house a cosmonaut for 10 days.)
