[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Voyager)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Pair of identical robot crafts launched in 1977 to investigate the gas giant planets. NASA seized this opportunity during an alignment of the outer solar system. The probes used gravity assist to slingshot past one planet on to their next destination. While Voyager 1 visited only Jupiter and Saturn, Voyager 2 explored all the gas giants.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Both Voyagers carried two sets of instruments. One set continuously sampled the craft\'s surroundings by measuring cosmic rays, magnetic field, and the solar wind. The other held cameras and sensors only used during planetary encounters. The probes were powered by a small plutonium-238 reactor and carried a 12 foot (3.7 m) radio dish to relay data back to Earth.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Voyager 1 reached Jupiter in 1979, with its closest approach on March 5. It then raced on to its Saturn rendezvous on November 12, 1980. Then it shot out of the plane of the solar system. Voyager 2 met Jupiter on July 9, 1979 and Saturn during August 1981. Engineers then aimed the probe at Uranus, which it reached January 24, 1986. Finally, Voyager 2 passed Neptune August 24, 1989. Both missions targeted studying the planets\' atmospheres, moons, and rings. They returned thousands of photos, showing detail impossible from Earth. During their brief encounters, the Voyager spacecrafts revealed more about the outer planets than astronomers had learned in 300 years of telescopic observation.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Vulpecula)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Vulpecula (The Fox))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Vulpecula (The Fox)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Faint northern constellation lying near Cygnus, the swan, and best viewed during summer. Its brightest star is only of magnitude 4.63, but it contains planetary nebula M27, called the Dumbbell Nebula. Also, the first pulsar, CP 1919, was found in Vulpecula.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Waning)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Describes the moon when its phases are past full moon and shrinking back to new moon. The period between full moon and last quarter is called waning gibbous, and the period between last quarter and new moon is called waning crescent.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Wavelength)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Distance between peaks of electromagnetic waves. Often measured in angstroms, wavelength is equal to the velocity of a wave divided by its frequency.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Weather Satellite)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Devices that orbit the Earth and return pictures of cloud and storm patterns. It is possible for private citizens, with the aid of antennas and audio tapes or home computers, to tap into the information provided by weather satellites and to predict the weather for.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Whipple, Fred Lawrence)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Whipple, Fred Lawrence (b. 1906))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Whipple, Fred Lawrence (b. 1906)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(American astronomer who, in 1949, suggested the \"dirty snowball\" model of comets. This model says that comets are made of dust and rocks that are held together by frozen gases such as ammonia and methane. Through 1942, Whipple had discovered six comets.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Whirlpool Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Whirlpool Galaxy (M51))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Whirlpool Galaxy (M51)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Beautiful spiral galaxy viewed face-own in the constellation Canes Venatici. The Whirlpool galaxy lies only 13 million lightyears away and is a magnificent sight in backyard telescopes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(M51, the Whirlpool galaxy, is a spiral just under the end of the Big Dipper\'s handle. The smaller galaxy, NGC 5195, has brushed past M51, disrupting the spiral arms in the process.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100M51, the Whirlpool galaxy, is a spiral just under the end of the Big Dipper\'s handle. The smaller galaxy, NGC 5195, has brushed past M51, disrupting the spiral arms in the process.\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Whirlpool Galaxy)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Whirlpool Galaxy (M51))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Whirlpool Galaxy (M51)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Beautiful spiral galaxy viewed face-own in the constellation Canes Venatici. The Whirlpool galaxy lies only 13 million lightyears away and is a magnificent sight in backyard telescopes.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(M51, the Whirlpool galaxy, is a spiral just under the end of the Big Dipper\'s handle. The smaller galaxy, NGC 5195, has brushed past M51, disrupting the spiral arms in the process.)
