[2026-10-18 20:36:02] , 1)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Year)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Time it takes the Earth to orbit the sun. The year we use is the tropical year, which is based on the time from one spring equinox to another and lasts 365.2422 days. The sidereal year, which is measured by the Earth\'s position with respect to stars other than the sun, is slightly longer because of precession and lasts 365.2564 days. The anomalistic year, which is the time from one of the Earth\'s perihelions (closest point in its orbit) to the next and which is affected by the gravity of the other planets, lasts 365.2596 days.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Yegorov, Boris Borisovich)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Yegorov, Boris Borisovich (b. 1937))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Yegorov, Boris Borisovich (b. 1937)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Soviet who, during the Voskhod 1 flight in October 1964, became the first physician in space and a participant in the first multi-person space flight. Voskhod 1\'s 254 mile (409 km) distance from Earth was the largest up to that time for any spacecraft with people on board. During their space flight, Yegorov and the other cosmonauts wore wool clothing and became the first people to wear something other than space suits in space.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Yeliseyev, Alexi Stanislovovich)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Yeliseyev, Alexi Stanislovovich (b. 1934))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Yeliseyev, Alexi Stanislovovich (b. 1934)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Soviet cosmonaut who participated in the January 1969 Soyuz 5 mission. During this flight, which performed the first docking of two-person spacecrafts, Yeliseyev spacewalked to Soyuz 4. With Shatalov, he participated in the October 1969 Soyuz 8 mission, which performed maneuvers with Soyuz 6 and Soyuz 7. He flew on the Soyuz 10 mission in April 1971, which docked with the space station Salyut 1 for a short time. He was also the Apollo-Soyuz\'s flight director.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Yerkes Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Founded in 1875 by Hale with a grant from Yerkes. Located at an altitude of 1,096 feet (334 m) at Williams Bay, Wisconsin, Yerkes Observatory has a 40 inch (102 cm) refracting telescope, which is the biggest in the world.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Young, John Watts)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Young, John Watts (b. 1930))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Young, John Watts (b. 1930)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(American astronaut who was the commander of Gemini 10. He flew in Gemini 3, America\'s first two-person mission, in March 1965. In May 1969, he was the command-module pilot of Apollo 10\'s dress rehearsal for the moon landing. In April 1972, he commanded Apollo 16 and, with Charles Duke, spent a total of 20 hours, 15 minutes on the surface during their 71 hour, 2 minute stay on the moon.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zeeman Effect)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(The splitting of spectral lines into a number of components when a source of light is in a magnetic field. Normally, light is emitted when electrons move around in an atom. Magnetic fields change the way the electrons move around and the light they produce. This effect was discovered by Pieter Zeeman in 1896 and is useful in identifying the presence and strength of magnetic fields around sunspots, stars, and other objects in space.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zelenchukskaya Astrophysical Observatory)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Zelenchukskaya Astrophysical Observatory (Special Astrophysical)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Observatory))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Zelenchukskaya Astrophysical Observatory (Special Astrophysical\x01\x0e-0100 \x01\x0e+0100Observatory)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Located in Russia\'s Caucasus Mountains at an altitude of 6,800 feet (2,070 m) and contains a 236 inch (600 cm) reflecting telescope, which was the largest in the world for more than a decade.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zenith)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Point that is highest in the sky and opposite the nadir. The angle between the zenith and an object is its zenith distance.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zenith Hourly Rate)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Zenith Hourly Rate (ZHR))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Zenith Hourly Rate (ZHR)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Number of meteors that a seasoned observer would observe in good viewing conditions if the meteor shower\'s radiant, or point of origin, was straight overhead.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zodiacal Constellation)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Fifteen degree wide band of constellations through which the moon, the planets (excluding Pluto), and the sun seem to move. Because of precession, the sun no longer enters these constellations on the same date that it used to. The constellations in the circle (Aries, Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra, Scorpius, Sagittarius, Capricornus, Aquarius, and Pisces) are of unknown origin but, they have been around since at least the Middle Ages. The ecliptic also runs through a 13th constellation - Ophiuchus. Because all but five of these constellations represent animals, the ring where they lie was called \"zodiakos kyklos\" (circle of animals) by the Greeks. )
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zodiacal Light)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Light on the ecliptic, best seen in the spring after sunset or in the fall before sunrise, that is produced by a cloud of distant dust particles that reflect light from the sun. This cloud contains particles with as little mass as 10^(-2) grams, and its total mass is around 30^(12) tons. When one of these particles falls to the Earth, it becomes a micrometeorite. Although the sun\'s solar wind sweeps the smaller particles out of the solar system and the sun\'s gravity attracts the larger ones, more dust is created by comets.)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_header(Zwicky, Fritz)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_double_emphasis(Zwicky, Fritz (1898-1974))
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(\x01\x0e+0100Zwicky, Fritz (1898-1974)\x01\x0e-0100)
[2026-10-18 20:36:02] render_paragraph(Swiss astrophysicist who, in 1934, determined with Baade that novas are different from supernovas and that, though most of a star is destroyed in a supernova, a neutron star may be left by the star\'s core.)
